HT_DATA.load("detail", {"AdNJ3fydeao":{"wy":"The most recommended introduction to Svelte, credited with sparking its adoption.","q":{"x":"you can (and I heartily recommend doing so) watch the \"Rethinking reactivity\" video here","i":20336598,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32442217,"author":"kretaceous","month":"2022-08","story":"Excel is pretty dang cool","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Spreadsheets as the original reactivity software","quote":"is an amazing talk which talks about how spreadsheets were the OG reactivity software. Highly recommended.","text":"The talk, Rethinking Reactivity by Rich Harris[0] is an amazing talk which talks about how spreadsheets were the OG reactivity software. Highly recommended. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31960638,"author":"doodlesdev","month":"2022-07","story":"The new wave of React state management","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Creator's presentation on Svelte's reactivity approach","quote":"I HIGHLY recommend listening to this presentation called \"Rethinking reactivity\" by Rich Harris (the creator)","text":"> Does it solve the problem of only redrawing the required minimum (beside the normal VDOM approach)? Yes, in fact I think it's actually pretty hard _not_ to redraw the bare minimum LOL. If you want to know more about Svelte (even if you're not looking to develop with it) I HIGHLY recommend listening to this presentation called \"Rethinking reactivity\" by Rich Harris (the creator) [VIDEO] > But does it solve the problem of growing state which never gets GC'd when components which used to use it are gone? I think the answer to the question is yes. Although the problem I'm speaking mostly about state management is the source of truth problem. Svelte provides a global store to store data, and data stored on local components are just _variables_ (no useState or hooks or anything complicated) where the Svelte compiler handles everything. Something else I'm really eyeing right now is SolidJS which takes a very similar approach to Svelte (compiler instead of library) for frontend development but provides an API that's very familiar to React developers so there's not much of a learning curve (although Svelte has a very easy learning curve too)."},{"id":22674491,"author":"gregors","month":"2020-03","story":"Svelte Is Unappealing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent speaker showing how Svelte approaches problems","quote":"Highly recommend this talk","text":"Highly recommend this talk [VIDEO] Rich Harris is an excellent speaker and it's worth seeing how Svelte approaches some things"},{"id":20706276,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2019-08","story":"Introduction to Svelte","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic intro to Svelte covering React's growing pains","quote":"watch this fantastic intro to svelte talk by it's creator","text":"tl;dr watch this fantastic intro to svelte talk by it's creator: [VIDEO] , it covers some of the growing pains of React that svelte addresses. While it might look like the frontend is going around in circles, there are major & minor differences between the technologies, and they have each introduced novel (to JS at least) things... Off the top of my head (this timeline might not be right but it's how I went through it at least): - Backbone got you away from jquery spaghetti, helped you actually manage your data & models. Often paired with Marionette to handle views (if you squint this is the precursor to components) and bring some order in that part of the app. - Angular bundles backbone's data handling (services), marionette's orderly view separation (views, directives), but they made a fatal mistake in the apply/digest cycle and maybe encouraging a bit too much complexity. Angular is everything bundled together, with consistent usage/documentation/semantics, a familiar programming pattern (MVC), and a large corporate sponsor in Google it caught on like wildfire. - Knockout sees Angular's MVC, laments it's complexity and focuses on MVVM -- simple binding of a model and a relatively simple reactive-where-necessary controller - React comes along and suggests an even simpler world where the only first class citizen is components and everything else comes separate (this isn't necessarily new, there is an article out there comparing it to COM subsystems for windows). React is alm"},{"id":20336598,"author":"CharlesW","month":"2019-07","story":"Svelte is the most beautiful web framework I've seen","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rich Harris's Rethinking Reactivity talk on Svelte","quote":"you can (and I heartily recommend doing so) watch the \"Rethinking reactivity\" video here","text":"It's a shame that the site's down. Until then, you can (and I heartily recommend doing so) watch the \"Rethinking reactivity\" video here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48658503,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-06","story":"Meta Pauses Employee-Tracking Program Following Internal Data Leak","stance":"rec","why":"Spreadsheets as reactive systems versus React's virtual DOM model","quote":"Rich Harris makes the point that React isn't actually reactive","text":"Svelte is awesome. Svelte 5's runes are especially powerful because they let reactivity escape the component boundary. The same reactive model works everywhere, whether you're updating the DOM or building plain application logic. Rich Harris makes the point that React isn't actually reactive: \"React doesn’t have any understanding of the values running through your app. It is not Reactive.\" Rich Harris - Rethinking reactivity: [VIDEO] >Modern JavaScript frameworks are all about reactivity. Change your application's state, and the view updates automatically. But there's a catch — tracking state changes at runtime adds overhead that eats into your bundle size and performance budgets. In this talk, we'll discover an alternative approach: moving reactivity into the language itself. Your apps have never been smaller or faster than they're about to become. He starts with spreadsheets as the archetypal reactive system. Defines reactivity as values automatically updating according to dependency relationships. Contrasts that with React's model of rerunning component functions and diffing virtual DOM trees. Argues that React \"doesn't understand the values flowing through your application\" and therefore isn't reactive in the traditional sense. Virtual DOM is pure overhead: [LINK] >But it turns out that we can achieve a similar programming model without using virtual DOM — and that's where Svelte comes in. React tracks component renders; reactive systems track data dependencies. React doe"},{"id":31965036,"author":"azemetre","month":"2022-07","story":"The new wave of React state management","stance":"rec","why":"History of spreadsheets and reactive programming, critiquing React","quote":"He goes over the history of spreadsheets, reactive programming, and dunks on react.","text":"This talk from Rich Harris should answer most of your Qs: Rethinking reactivity [VIDEO] He goes over the history of spreadsheets, reactive programming, and dunks on react."},{"id":29313923,"author":"Glench","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: Simplest stack to build web apps in 2021?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains some of Svelte's benefits","quote":"you might like these videos that do a great job explaining some of its benefits","text":"Ooh, if you liked what you saw of Svelte, you might like these videos that do a great job explaining some of its benefits: [VIDEO] and [LINK] SvelteKit is very new and I haven't had a chance to use it yet, but my own framework was directly inspired by it. SvelteKit does a lot and looks great — fantastic developer experience, deploys to lots of types of services, SSR/SSG/client-side routing support, etc. And it seems like a great thing for Svelte to have as a default way for developers to start making Svelte components. Personally, I'm going to wait till SvelteKit a bit more mature before I dig in, but I'm guessing I'll end up rewriting my framework on top of SvelteKit at some point."},{"id":24364671,"author":"ric2b","month":"2020-09","story":"React is becoming a black box","stance":"rec","why":"Author's talk on Svelte's design","quote":"If you're curious I recommend either the \"rethinking reactivity\" talk from the author","text":"I was drawn to Svelte by the performance/bundle size but I fell in love with how simple it is to write components and share data between them. Each component is just HTML/CSS/JS in a single file, sort of like Vue but cleaner. If you're curious I recommend either the \"rethinking reactivity\" talk from the author: [VIDEO] or the tutorial: [LINK] Both are great!"},{"id":23377062,"author":"lukeramsden","month":"2020-06","story":"Solid – A declarative JavaScript library for building user interfaces","stance":"rec","why":"Creator explains compile-time DOM update tracking versus virtual DOM","quote":"This is a good talk on the matter from the creator of Svelte himself, if you're interested","text":"This is a good talk on the matter from the creator of Svelte himself, if you're interested: [VIDEO] Basically, it boils down to moving from tracking the DOM state in a VirtualDOM to tracking what DOM updates can happen at the compile stage and then just doing those exact updates to the DOM."},{"id":23118938,"author":"dwoot","month":"2020-05","story":"The Cost of JavaScript Frameworks","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction that convinced the commenter Svelte is superior","quote":"I checked out Svelte, by first watching Rich Harris' video","text":"I don't think anyone is going to sell you on something else, you seem very ingrained in the React ecosystem. More dollars doesn't equate to \"better product\" down the road. Angular came and is still a part of Google. Would anyone starting a new project use it over something like VueJS or React today? Probably not. Right? So that sort of tosses your argument out the window. I don't do JavaScript professionally anymore, but I've worked with jQuery, BackboneJS, lots of AngularJS, some Elm, some VueJS, and some React, but being the curious person that I am, in search of greener pastures, I checked out Svelte, by first watching Rich Harris' video [VIDEO] on \"Thinking Reactivity\", then seeing the benchmarks: [LINK] , and watching Rich and Dan Abramov spar a bit over their challenges. From an outsider with no skin in the JS game, it is clear that Svelte seems far superior now and into the future... 1. it is a compiler (it's like comparing C/C++ to Python) 2. the joy of out-of-the-box animations that use the GPU in CSS (reminds me of the joy of using jQuery for the first time w/ it's OOB animations) 3. you can drop in any other JS lib (doesn't have to be in a particular JS framework ecosystem) and have it inter-op! This is huge! It's because it's literally like writing vanilla JS 4. no need to learn some new syntax like JSX (not a huge fan) Just take a peek. If you don't like Svelte, so be it, but at least you know what it is that you don't like rather than turning a blind eye because"},{"id":21859524,"author":"zeusly","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2019?","stance":"rec","why":"Svelte v3 and the possible future of frontend frameworks","quote":"Talk about Svelte v3 and the (possible) future of frontend frameworks","text":"Rich Harris - Rethinking Reactivity Talk about Svelte v3 and the (possible) future of frontend frameworks Video: [VIDEO] HN discussion regarding Svelte 3: [LINK]"},{"id":21349660,"author":"anderspitman","month":"2019-10","story":"React Concurrent Mode","stance":"rec","why":"Compares a React Concurrent Mode demo to Svelte","quote":"Rich Harris' excellent talk Rethinking Reactivity has an interesting section where he compares a React Concurrent Mode demo to Svelte","text":"Rich Harris' excellent talk Rethinking Reactivity has an interesting section where he compares a React Concurrent Mode demo to Svelte: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20189312,"author":"reactor","month":"2019-06","story":"JavaScript Is the CO2 of the Web [audio]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on Svelte's approach to reactivity","quote":"","text":"May not be exactly what you are looking for, but take a look at [LINK] if you haven't seen already. and this [VIDEO]"},{"id":20242309,"author":"Tade0","month":"2019-06","story":"Vue RFC: Expose logic-related component options via function-based APIs instead","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point for learning Svelte","quote":"Rich Harris' presentation is a good starting point","text":"I haven't seen any, but as a relatively early proponent of Vue(as in: I was there when v2 came out), I found Svelte even easier to get into than Vue. Anyway Rich Harris' presentation is a good starting point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35304156,"author":"ttfkam","month":"2023-03","story":"React: You Might Not Need an Effect","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There is nothing in React I'd regard as \"terse\". Perhaps slightly more terse than the mountain of boilerplate it required previously, but that's as far as I'd go. [VIDEO] We really should accept that all of the following are ABSTRACTION LEAKS and not inherent benefits to web development at large: • shouldComponentUpdate • React.PureComponent • useMemo • useCallback • Concurrent mode They exist solely because React's underlying model is insufficient and we, the developers, are being made to do the computer's job with all this optimization boilerplate. The VDOM is dying. Let it die. It's long past time to move on. Any further React development in the existing foundation is just continuing to build upon sand."},{"id":31782511,"author":"swyx","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a good dev tool pitch","quote":"","text":"i also asked this on twitter so just putting some replies here: - Doug Engelbart [LINK] - Twilio [LINK] - Stripe - \"7 lines of code\" - Netlify - [LINK] - Heroku - [LINK] and git push heroku master - Cloudflare - [LINK] - Node ( [LINK] ) and Deno ( [LINK] ) - Firebase (reportedly) - [LINK] smaller companies/less impactful pitches that i still like - Redux [LINK] - Stackblitz [LINK] - Comm [LINK] - Mongodb [LINK] - Let's Encrypt [LINK] - Figwheel-Clojurescript [LINK] - Svelte [VIDEO]"},{"id":24323656,"author":"leejoramo","month":"2020-08","story":"SC-IM – An ncurses spreadsheet program for the terminal","stance":"reference","why":"Intro to Svelte 3 discussing early academic spreadsheet concept","quote":"he discussed an earlier academic spreadsheet concept.","text":"I believe that VisiCalc was the first to ship a functional spreadsheet. I remember watching RichHarris’s “Rich Harris - Rethinking reactivity” video introducing Svelte 3, and he discussed an earlier academic spreadsheet concept. [VIDEO] I used the following “terminal” spreadsheets VisiCalc on a Radio Shack Model III Microsoft MultiPlan on MS-DOS Lotus 1-2-3 on MS-DOS"},{"id":21377741,"author":"arxpoetica","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: What are your experiences with Svelte (JS framework)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk credited with Svelte's rise in users","quote":"Basically since Rich did his Rethinking Reactivity talk at YGLF back in April.","text":"I've been using Svelte since inception, and you're 100% right, there's been a significant uptick in user base over the last 6 mos. Basically since Rich did his Rethinking Reactivity talk at YGLF back in April.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19722023,"author":"rich_harris","month":"2019-04","story":"Svelte 3: Rethinking Reactivity","stance":"reference","why":"Svelte demo related to SVG performance","quote":"you may enjoy this demo","text":"My view on this is that complexity, like energy, can only really be converted from one form into another. Normally the distinction is between runtime complexity and cognitive complexity — the result of hand-optimised imperative code. In other words, the classic trade-off between developer experience and user experience. Svelte's claim is that converting it into build time complexity is in fact the only sensible thing to do. It doesn't really matter how much complexity is involved in `npm run build` if it means that DX and UX are no longer in tension. But to answer your point about SVG, Svelte has to obey the same fundamental constraints as any other code, so you will eventually hit performance issues (though you may enjoy this demo [VIDEO] ). One avenue I'm currently exploring is whether the same declarative/compiled model can be used as a wrapper around WebGL APIs, though it's very early days."}],"r":[["BcyYyyaPz84",1],["KDRnEm-B3AI",1],["rzJqXd-1dEU",1],["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["wpOxbesRNBc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1]]},"h6fcK_fRYaI":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a beloved story and link the animation when threads touch on consciousness, empathy or identity.","q":{"x":"The Egg is one of my life long favorite histories.","i":38422785,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38422785,"author":"timetraveller26","month":"2023-11","story":"They're Made Out of Meat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated version of a lifelong favorite story","quote":"The Egg is one of my life long favorite histories.","text":"The Egg is one of my life long favorite histories. kurzgesagt did a video animation of it a few years ago [VIDEO]"},{"id":27086247,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-05","story":"Apple Silicon Hardware: SPRR and Guarded Exception Levels (GXF)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Beautiful story about all life being one","quote":"This is another story I tell myself because it is ridiculously beautiful to me","text":"> People aren't \"gifted\" with technology I used the words \"humans having been gifted digital technology\" as I am a marxist and I believe that all the technologies and scientific knowledge that we reverse engineer from nature is gifted to us by a higher power. Others talk about the brilliance of human minds, yet since I see us as a part of nature, there's no distinction in that for me. Another reason I say it that way is because unfortunately the 'great man theory/myth' still reigns supreme in the tech industry [1]. I think though that this 'brilliant mind' story that is commonly told in the west seems to have often justified detrimental and destructive human supremacy/domination over the rest of nature (also leading us to the edge of the cliff now with global warming). Anyways, I think that we all tell ourselves different stories and this is one of mine. This is another story I tell myself because it is ridiculously beautiful to me: [LINK] or Kurzgesagt version: [VIDEO] > I was privileged enough to have my mother sacrifice everything(her property, her life, her family and her homeland) to pull me out of a warzone to provide for me so I would be in a position to be able to build these skills. That sounds incredibly difficult and distressing for her (and possibly for you too). Thank you for sharing this personal story here. > Just calling the whole thing privilege is such a disservice to all she had to sacrifice for me to be able to be in this position. That isn't what I am ref"},{"id":24093078,"author":"bigmattystyles","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: Films that made you see the world differently?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"You live every human life","quote":"I can’t stop thinking about it.","text":"Jean de Florette (1986) and its conclusion Manon des Sources (1986), great 80s French movies, top notch cast you would even recognize in the US. Also Le château de ma mère (1990) and La gloire de mon père (1990). I haven’t watched the second pair since I was a small boy, but I remember being captivated. A complete tangent is this Kurzgesagt video on YouTube where they consider the possibility that you will be every human and live every life. I can’t stop thinking about it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23220146,"author":"stared","month":"2020-05","story":"Electrons May Well Be Conscious","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated reading of Andy Weir's The Egg","quote":"one of my favorite stories","text":"Closely related, especially given the context of consciousness, one of my favorite stories: \"The Egg\" ( [LINK] , or if you prefer read by Kurzgesagt: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":34470373,"author":"majewsky","month":"2023-01","story":"Make Up a Guy","stance":"rec","why":"Animated narration of The Egg","quote":"we should also link to the animated narration of it by Kurzgesagt","text":"Since we're linking to The Egg, we should also link to the animated narration of it by Kurzgesagt: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32400343,"author":"BbzzbB","month":"2022-08","story":"From novice to master, and back again (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Animated version adds music and narration, poignant","quote":"the music, animation and narration makes it quite poignant","text":"Kurzgesagt's short video is great too, it's (quasi-)verbatim, but the music, animation and narration makes it quite poignant. The idea behind the story is quite fascinating to me. If simulationists are right, it's as good or better of a \"why\" as \"origin seeking\" IMO. Not that I believe one way or the other, I just think the idea's interesting to ponder on. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29284071,"author":"freediver","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: I feel so shallow and dumb when I see what other smart people are doing","stance":"rec","why":"Everyone else is you, per The Egg","quote":"all those successful around you are you, as well as the billions less fortunate","text":"A perspective that may be interesting to you - all those successful around you are you, as well as the billions less fortunate - they are also you. \"The Egg\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":40655915,"author":"xjay","month":"2024-06","story":"The 'Dead Internet Theory'","stance":"reference","why":"Animated adaptation of Andy Weir's story","quote":"Kurzgesagt adaptation of the story (31M views)","text":"Somewhat related: \"The Egg\" [2009*] is a fictional short story by American writer Andy Weir. [1] > You, a 48-year-old man who dies in a car crash, meet God, the narrator, who says that you have been reincarnated many times before, and that you are next to be reincarnated as a Chinese peasant girl in 540 AD. God then explains that you are, in fact, constantly reincarnated across time, and that all human beings who have ever lived and will ever live are incarnations of you. [1] [1] [LINK] Kurzgesagt adaptation of the story (31M views): [VIDEO]"},{"id":31449358,"author":"SemanticStrengh","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Where does your mind wander to?","stance":"reference","why":"Related to idea of one shared soul","quote":"related","text":"you might find my last * point interesting about the thesis for multiple selves: [LINK] however the fact there is no criterion for delineating the soul to a volume of matter makes me strongly believe that the rational belief is that we are living a sandboxed illusion and that there is only one decentralized being in the universe. We are the same soul talking to itselfs via the prisms of siloeds lifes, and when you hurt someone, you do in fact, hurt yourself. related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29170648,"author":"freediver","month":"2021-11","story":"Show HN: You.com, private search engine that summarizes the web – built for devs","stance":"reference","why":"The Egg's theme of being everyone","quote":"","text":"I know you had a bad day today Richard Socher. I am sorry for that. Reading through these comments must have hurt, and in some strange \"The Egg\" [1] way hurt me too. I felt as everyone that left a bad comment, and I felt as you, receiving all this. And all that after finally releasing the beta of your product, having worked very hard for that. I am impressed by your stoic attitude and not caving in under all this pressure (this was probably as far as 'blood baths' on HN go). Truly remarkable, even though you obviously made a product marketing mistake here. I encourage you to keep going and carefully read all the advice you got here today, for free. This time, let it all in. The cause is still good, the execution is clearly suboptimal. It's on you to get there. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26472359,"author":"Moodles","month":"2021-03","story":"Magic Mushrooms Are Decriminalized in DC as of Today","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’ve taken mushrooms quite a few times. With a light dose, it’s just been mild, being silly and giggling at colors and nature. Pleasant, but just kind of a fun drug honestly. With a moderate dose, you tend to be introspective and consider your own life and relationships in different ways. Feels like therapy to be honest. Hard work. Emotionally draining. There’s usually a lesson at the end. Overall it is good for me and makes me a better, more empathetic person. With higher doses in a so-called bad trip, it’s sometimes been just extremely confusing and not at all fun, to the extent that I’d forget my own name or what I’ve taken and basically just all memories. It’s very frustrating to be in that state, constantly trying not to fly away but not able to ground yourself. It’s a little disturbing, but not horrific. I think the reason for this is that I am afraid to lose my ego, or die, and I would actually benefit from a slightly higher dose so I'm unable to resist. With really high doses though, it’s been a truly beautiful, enlightening experience. I still think of it sometimes. It’s very difficult to really explain in words, but it feels like kind of extreme empathy with all life. Buddhists would call it going egoless. I just felt very at peace: we all live, we all die, and it’s all okay. I’m not particularly religious or spiritual at all but that experience really changed me. It feels like life is in all these different forms, and you just happen to be one particular variation "}],"r":[["JEYh5WACqEk",2],["MBRqu0YOH14",1],["5sVfTPaxRwk",1],["d_zt3kGW1NM",1],["gSPNRu4ZPvE",1],["_2khAmMTAjI",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["zU7Lww-sBPg",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["yYUuWWnfRsk",1]]},"Uj-rK8V-rik":{"wy":"HN returns to it repeatedly as one of the best candid talks on how a real self-driving system sees the road and where it struggles.","q":{"x":"It's one of the best talks on the subject.","i":17508616,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17508616,"author":"Animats","month":"2018-07","story":"Former Apple Employee Charged with Theft of Autonomous Car Project Trade Secrets","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Widely viewed talk with Google cars handling challenging conditions","quote":"It's one of the best talks on the subject.","text":"That's Chris Urmson's talk at SXSW in 2016 [1], which has been widely viewed. It's one of the best talks on the subject. He said \"How quickly can we get this into people's hands? If you read the papers, you see maybe it's three years, maybe it's thirty years. And I am here to tell you that honestly, it's a bit of both.\" Then he showed video of Google cars dealing with various challenging conditions. This included the famous scene where a Google self driving car encountered a woman in a powered wheelchair using a broom to chase a turkey. The software recognized this as something to avoid, even though it didn't classify it further. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12091679,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-07","story":"Tesla Model S Autopilot Reliability – Why Americans Should Love Tesla","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed look at sensor data, bus collision and hard driving cases","quote":"If you're going to comment on automatic driving, you need to see this.","text":"On a related note, here's Chris Urmson's long talk at SXSW on how Google does automatic driving.[1] This is worth watching. He shows what Google cars sense and gives an overview of how they analyze the data. This is the first time Google has released this level of detail. If you're going to comment on automatic driving, you need to see this. Urmson goes into great detail on Google's 2mph collision with a bus. The sensor data from the Google car is shown. The video from the bus (it had cameras, too) is shown. Exactly what the software was trying to do is discussed. The assumptions the software made about what the bus driver would do are discussed. What they did to prevent this from happening again is mentioned. Most of the talk is about the hard cases. In the beginning, Google developed a highway driving system, but that was years ago. Now they're working hard on dealing with everything that can happen on a road, including someone in an electric wheelchair chasing ducks with a broom. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14726813,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-07","story":"Comma.ai launches an $88 universal car interface called Panda","stance":"rec","why":"Google car handling a wheelchair user chasing a duck","quote":"Watch Chris Urmson's self-driving car talk at SXSW 2016.[1]","text":"Watch Chris Urmson's self-driving car talk at SXSW 2016.[1] Especially the part where the Google car encounters someone in a powered wheelchair chasing a duck with a broom. Google can handle that. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14574745,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-06","story":"Tesla Autopilot","stance":"rec","why":"Sensor visualizations and candid discussion of failures at Google","quote":"Compare Chris Urmson's SXSW talk from 2016 about Google's system.[1]","text":"Compare Chris Urmson's SXSW talk from 2016 about Google's system.[1] Look carefully at the graphics which show what the sensors are detecting, and compare with Tesla's. Watch Urmson discuss hard cases, problems, and failures. Not seeing that with Tesla. Or Uber. Or Cruise. Or Otto. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13370530,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-01","story":"Let’s Talk About Self-Driving Cars","stance":"rec","why":"Google handling construction sites and unusual road situations","quote":"Watch Urmson's video from SXSW.[1]","text":"Except that Google was able to handle construction sites, traffic cones, and people directing traffic last year. Watch Urmson's video from SXSW.[1] Google has put a lot of effort into handling unusual situations. Those cars driving on residential streets in Mountain View and Austin have accumulated data on the hard cases. The California DMV continues to publish autonomous vehicle crash reports. The last one was in October.[2] A Google vehicle was rear-ended at 3mph when it was entering an intersection with poor visibility, detected cross-traffic, and stopped. I'm looking forward to the 2016 self-driving disengagement reports appearing on on the California DMV site.[3] Those were due January 1, covering the year ending 1 December 2016. Then we'll have comparative info on how all the players are doing. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":13259274,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-12","story":"How Self-Driving Cars Work","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of how Google's self-driving system works","quote":"That's worth a watch. It gives a good overview of how Google does it.","text":"This article seems to be a summary of Chris Urmson's talk at SXSW.[1] That's worth a watch. It gives a good overview of how Google does it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11773722,"author":"kyrra","month":"2016-05","story":"Four hundred miles with Tesla’s autopilot forced me to trust the machine","stance":"rec","why":"Google team presentation noting early highway driving success","quote":"This was a good presentation by the Google team","text":"This was a good presentation by the Google team: [VIDEO] I believe they say in there that they got highway driving down really early on. They have been focusing on urban driving for the last few years."},{"id":16766042,"author":"unityByFreedom","month":"2018-04","story":"Tesla crash in September showed similarities to fatal Mountain View accident","stance":"reference","why":"Google found test users misbehaved in semi-autonomous cars","quote":"found that users misbehave [1] when behind the wheel of semi-autonomous vehicles","text":"> Why not the other way around? You do the driving, and the computer is the backup in case you mess up? Unfortunately having the computer takeover has the same issues. If it thinks you're following the wrong lane it could choose to steer you into a barrier, for example. Chris Urmson's team at Google ran user tests at the start of their autonomous vehicle program and found that users misbehave [1] when behind the wheel of semi-autonomous vehicles, despite having been given instruction to pay attention. We're seeing that scenario play out in slow motion in Teslas and perhaps other driver-assistance programs that get less press. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15476016,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-10","story":"Waymo's big self-drive car report","stance":"reference","why":"Covers most of Waymo's report material in more detail","quote":"Chris Urmson's SXSW talk from 2016 [2] covered most of this material in more detail.","text":"Link to actual report.[1] (When you're posting a link to something important, post the link to the real document, not some pundit blithering about it. Yes, you may have to do some digging to find it when the pundit outlet doesn't provide a link.) There's not much new here if you've been following what Waymo is doing. Chris Urmson's SXSW talk from 2016 [2] covered most of this material in more detail. This is more of a PR-level version of that content. The new material covers mainly the limitations Waymo is imposing on their system. It works only where they have very detailed maps of the road system, with more information than even StreetView. This is conservative, but not a bad decision for initial release. They have a button to call customer service. (That's so unlike Google.) [1] [LINK] [2] h [VIDEO]"},{"id":15090666,"author":"Retric","month":"2017-08","story":"Inside Waymo's Secret World for Training Self-Driving Cars","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an unidentifiable object the car should avoid","quote":"I am sure the car had no idea what this was","text":"Can you give an actual example? Because, not being able to identify something is not necessarily an issue as long as the car notices something is there and it should not hit it. EX: I am sure the car had no idea what this was: [VIDEO] but as long as it can tell it's bigger than a bread box and so it should not to hit it that's enough."},{"id":14600924,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-06","story":"Tesla hires Andrej Karpathy","stance":"reference","why":"Lidar-based approach versus Tesla's camera-only strategy","quote":"Deep learning is useful, but it's not magic, and it's not strong AI.","text":"What this really reflects is that Tesla has painted itself into a corner. They've shipped vehicles with a weak sensor suite that's claimed to be sufficient to support self-driving, leaving the software for later. Tesla, unlike everybody else who's serious, doesn't have a LIDAR. Now, it's \"later\", their software demos are about where Google was in 2010, and Tesla has a big problem. This is a really hard problem to do with cameras alone. Deep learning is useful, but it's not magic, and it's not strong AI. No wonder their head of automatic driving quit. Karpathy may bail in a few months, once he realizes he's joined a death march. If anything, Tesla should have learned by now that you don't want to need to recognize objects to avoid them. The Mobileye system works that way, being very focused on identifying moving cars, pedestrians, and bicycles. It's led to at least four high speed crashes with stationary objects it didn't identify as obstacles. This is pathetic. We had avoidance of big stationary objects working in the DARPA Grand Challenge back in 2005. With a good LIDAR, you get a point cloud. This tells you where there's something. Maybe you can identify some of the \"somethings\", but if there's an unidentified object out there, you know it's there. The planner can plot a course that stays on the road surface and doesn't hit anything. Object recognition is mostly for identifying other road users and trying to predict their behavior. Compare Chris Urmson's talk and videos at "},{"id":13748037,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-02","story":"Trump administration re-evaluating self-driving car guidance","stance":"reference","why":"Google/Waymo position on autonomy stated in the talk","quote":"It's in his SXSW talk.[1]","text":"It's in his SXSW talk.[1] It's the Google/Waymo and Volvo position, those being the two companies closest to a product. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12939603,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-11","story":"Nvidia on new self-driving system: “basically 5 years ahead and coming in 2017”","stance":"reference","why":"Google's obstacle handling beyond standard cars and pedestrians","quote":"Only Urmson from Google talks about that much.","text":"Yeah, me too; I was in the 2005 Grand Challenge. Vision processing has made enormous progress since then. Here's Mobileye's guy explaining what they do and what needs to be done.[1] Here's the NVidia guy.[2] Here's Chris Urmson from Google.[3] All three use their vision system to draw boxes around things that look like obstacles. Then the planner uses those boxes to construct a path. The vision system just gives you a rectangular box in image space; the LIDAR based systems turn point clouds into simple 3D solids. These vision systems do OK on things that look like typical road obstacles - cars and people. Other stuff, not so much. Only Urmson from Google talks about that much. The videos from the vision systems don't seem to be doing much with road edge clutter. They're not marking every post, fireplug, and low-hanging branch. Train a deep learning system on cars, trucks, pedestrians, and bicycles, and your system sees only cars, trucks, pedestrians, and bicycles. These vision systems do a lot less than many people think they do. Watch the videos. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12095957,"author":"studentrob","month":"2016-07","story":"Tesla Model S Autopilot Reliability – Why Americans Should Love Tesla","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on Google's 2mph bus collision","quote":"Here's the part where he starts talking about the 2mph bus accident [1].","text":"Cool! Thank you for sharing this. Here's the part where he starts talking about the 2mph bus accident [1]. He says the self-driving car should not have moved. He does not say why. He says they ran a lot of tests to make sure it will not happen again. In plain English, I'd say, when the SDC is stopped on the side of the road, give sufficient time for traffic coming from behind to clear out before the SDC moves forward. Also, if an accident is imminent, being in a non-moving position is probably safer and less likely to cause liability for the SDC than moving. Plus, rolling over some sandbags is probably preferable to being hit by a bus. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12111492,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-07","story":"Misfortune","stance":"reference","why":"Playbacks of situations Google cars encountered","quote":"See Chris Urmson's talk at SXSW, which has lots of playbacks of situations Google cars have encountered.[1]","text":"Google does a lot of driving in simulation, using data captured by real vehicles. They log all the raw sensor data on the real vehicles, so they can do a full playback. They do far more miles in simulation than they do in the real world. That's how they debug, and how they regression-test. For near-misses, disconnects, poor decisions, and accidents, they replay the event in the simulator using the live data, and work on the software until that won't happen again. Analyzing problems which didn't rise to the level of an accident provides far more situations to analyze. They're not limited to just accidents. See Chris Urmson's talk at SXSW, which has lots of playbacks of situations Google cars have encountered.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11501984,"author":"EmployedRussian","month":"2016-04","story":"Post-Mortem for Google Compute Engine’s Global Outage on April 11","stance":"reference","why":"Urmson on why handing drivers controls is dangerous","quote":"Urmson talks about it here","text":"When you are barreling down a highway at 65 miles per hour and are not paying attention (and you wouldn't, because the car drives itself just fine), giving you controls is much more dangerous (for you and others around you) then not. Urmson talks about it here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11317220,"author":"agildehaus","month":"2016-03","story":"Uber seeking to buy self-driving cars: source","stance":"reference","why":"Urmson on snow breaking LIDAR-based localization","quote":"The car simply cannot figure out where it is when there's a significant layer of snow on the ground","text":"They do well in rain but snow remains a problem, not due to icy roads (at SXSW Chris Urmson said they do well on slick roads) but that the \"better than GPS\" LIDAR-based location tracking stops working when snow is on the ground. The car simply cannot figure out where it is when there's a significant layer of snow on the ground because the reference maps stop looking like what the car is seeing. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WU0gvPcc3jQ",1],["3UxZDJ1HiPE",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1],["ACkmg3Y64_s",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["nsJZ_kKjXcE",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["W71BTkUbdqE",1],["BpsMkLaEiOY",1]]},"ZDnShu5V99s":{"wy":"HN keeps pointing to it as a long, fascinating talk on cryptographic, verifiable voting whenever e-voting security comes up.","q":{"x":"Here's a long but fascinating tech talk on a real solution","i":8040127,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":12933885,"author":"kaoD","month":"2016-11","story":"An idea for encrypted, verifiable voting","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Verifiable voter secret ballots, impossible to prove to others","quote":"I can't believe this talk hasn't been posted yet!","text":"I can't believe this talk hasn't been posted yet! Theory and Practice of Cryptography: Verifying Elections with Cryptography [VIDEO] This is probably one of the homomorphic schemes mentioned in TFA. It's very old (2007-ish?) and the state of the art probably advanced, but it's very interesting and addresses a number of points that are not cared for in TFA (like secret ballots verifiable by the voter but impossible to prove to anyone else)."},{"id":19136246,"author":"bubblethink","month":"2019-02","story":"Bounty Hunt Announced on Swiss eVoting System","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on electronic voting opacity","quote":"There was this interesting talk years ago.","text":">The central issue with electronic voting is that it's opaque to observers. There was this interesting talk years ago. [VIDEO] Does anyone in the space have a more up to date reference on where things are at ?"},{"id":13510970,"author":"grzm","month":"2017-01","story":"Time to Take a Stand","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on voting integrity research","quote":"Some good starting points are","text":"Going back to my original comment, widespread faith that the voting process is safe is just as important as whether it actually is. Casting doubt on the process without really solid evidence undermines this faith. By no means do I want to be Pollyannaish about this: it's too important. Which is why I want to see improvements in the voting process. If voting integrity is something you feel particularly strongly about (I certainly do), I encourage to look into the work that's already out there. The 2000 election spurred a lot of research. Some good starting points are: Ronald L. Rivest MIT Auditability and Verifiability of Elections ACM-IEEE talk March 16, 2016 [LINK] --- Ben Adida Google Tech Talks December 19, 2007 Theory and Practice of Cryptography: Verifying Electiosns with Cryptography [VIDEO]"},{"id":12843479,"author":"grzm","month":"2016-11","story":"Votes could be counted as fractions instead of as whole numbers","stance":"rec","why":"Ben Adida on verifying elections with cryptography","quote":"You might find this Google Tech Talk by Ben Adida","text":"I think blockchain might have some application, but I'm unconvinced that computer technology alone is going to solve it. Have a look at this slide deck from Ron Rivest (of RSA fame). [LINK] I think he does a pretty good job of covering the history of modern voting and how technology can be properly applied. You might find this Google Tech Talk by Ben Adida \"Verifying Elections with Cryptography\" interesting as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12846901,"author":"grzm","month":"2016-11","story":"E-Voting Refuses to Die Even Though It's Neither Secure nor Secret","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among useful starting points on verifiable voting","quote":"Here are some links I've found useful","text":"There are documented issues with many existing electronic voting systems. E-voting encompasses a lot of different types of system that can involve computers in the chain, and not surprisingly all of them are equal. There have been a few discussions around voting on HN recently, and that's good. It's an important topic, as trust in the integrity of elections is vital for democracy. There's a lot of existing material out there about verifiable and auditable voting systems, especially in the wake of the 2000 US Presidential elections. I encourage you to take a look at what's out there. It can provide a great foundation for discussion. Here are some links I've found useful: Wikipedia page on End-to-End Auditable Voting Systems [LINK] Ron Rivest slide deck from March 2016. Auditability and Verifiability of Elections [LINK] Ben Adida Google Tech Talks December 19, 2007 Theory and Practice of Cryptography: Verifying Electiosns with Cryptography [VIDEO]"},{"id":12790618,"author":"grzm","month":"2016-10","story":"In Pennsylvania, Claims of a Rigged Election May Be Impossible to Disprove","stance":"rec","why":"Ben Adida on verifying elections with cryptography","quote":"And heres a good talk by Ben Adida speaking at Google","text":"Here's a link to slides from a talk Ronald Rivest gave back in March 2016 at ACM-IEEE: \"Auditability and Verifiability of Elections\" [LINK] It covers a lot of what I'm sure is going to be discussed in this thread, including vote receipts, coercion, verifiability that your vote is counted. Edit to add: And heres a good talk by Ben Adida speaking at Google for a \"Theory and Practice of Cryptography\" TechTalk series entitled \"Verifying Elections with Crytography\" from 2007. Good stuff there, too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9320824,"author":"aianus","month":"2015-04","story":"Estonia's e-residency program expands abroad","stance":"rec","why":"Electronic voting schemes that keep ballot secret","quote":"rather long but very interesting tech talk about electronic voting schemes","text":"Unfortunately this enables someone to coerce or bribe you into voting for a certain candidate, since the coercer would be able to demand to see your receipt and verify you complied. Here's a rather long but very interesting tech talk about electronic voting schemes which maintain the secret ballot property: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8040127,"author":"aianus","month":"2014-07","story":"Software used to count Australian Senate votes is a “trade secret”","stance":"rec","why":"Long talk on a cryptographic voting solution","quote":"Here's a long but fascinating tech talk on a real solution","text":"> counted by hand. By whom? Overseen by whom? Who oversees the overseers? Not to mention people make mistakes, ballots get dumped, and nobody has any evidence their vote was actually counted. Here's a long but fascinating tech talk on a real solution: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2212522,"author":"maeon3","month":"2011-02","story":"Free university lectures on computer science","stance":"rec","why":"Advanced cryptography talk from Google channels","quote":"post some epic content for the more advanced programmer.","text":"Youtube users: 'UserGroupsatGoogle' and 'GoogleTechTalks' post some epic content for the more advanced programmer. Continuous Integration with Hudson: [LINK] Cryptography Theory and practice: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10077426,"author":"Strilanc","month":"2015-08","story":"Decertifying the worst voting machine in the US","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for cryptographic verifiable voting","quote":"","text":"It entirely depends if it's using cryptography or not [1]. For example, using zero knowledge proofs you can be given a receipt that lets you verify your vote was included in the total (without allowing you to prove to an attacker which party you voted for). Using the computers as glorified counters is a security nightmare. Using them for what they're good at (crypto) would be a security boon, with the main downside being how hard it would be for non-computer-scientists to understand the nature of the security. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8795162,"author":"iwwr","month":"2014-12","story":"Independent Report on E-voting in Estonia","stance":"reference","why":"Older talk with more detail on the approach","quote":"There is an older google talk about this delving into more detail.","text":"It's possible via homomorphic encryption, i.e. a way to manipulate cyphertext to make some mathematical operations like sums/products or logical comparisons without revealing the actual secrets. For example, it's possible to compare two encryptions of two numbers and get an encryption of their sum or to know which one is larger without knowing either. However one drawback of such a voting system would be an inability to get actual vote tallies, only an ordering of the candidates. There is an older google talk about this delving into more detail. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7867750,"author":"kaoD","month":"2014-06","story":"V initiative – Building tomorrow’s secure digital democracy","stance":"reference","why":"Voting scheme proving to voter without enabling vote selling","quote":"This scheme provides proof to the voter, but not enough so he can prove his vote to someone else","text":"Why not use a non-bitcoin-based system and completely avoid the 51% attack? [VIDEO] I'm not sure if this is state of the art[0] since the talk is 7 years old. This scheme provides proof to the voter, but not enough so he can prove his vote to someone else, avoiding vote selling altogether. The biggest challenge IMHO is trust in the system. [0] I'd appreciate criticism or any other progress in the field."},{"id":5586025,"author":"Strilanc","month":"2013-04","story":"Programmer under oath admits computers rig elections","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a verifiable voting protocol","quote":"Video with an example of a verifiable voting protocol","text":"Verifying that the right binary was uploaded is solving the wrong problem. Who cares what the source code claims to do? We care about what it actually does. Real cryptographic voting systems use verifiable protocols. You don't trust the machine, you force it to follow a protocol that involves proving its actions are correct at every step. You can verify basically everything except destruction of information (i.e. there's no way to prove with cryptography that a video camera didn't record the voter). Video with an example of a verifiable voting protocol: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["gORNmfpD0ak",1]]},"mbyG85GZ0PI":{"wy":"Recommended repeatedly in 'where do I start with ML' threads as a clear, math-aware introduction.","q":{"x":"I have found the Caltech machine learning intro course to be the most elucidating of any resource so far","i":35545633,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35545633,"author":"bob1029","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: I am a full stack developer, where do I start learning for AI","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Most elucidating ML intro resource, delivered clearly","quote":"I have found the Caltech machine learning intro course to be the most elucidating of any resource so far","text":"I have found the Caltech machine learning intro course to be the most elucidating of any resource so far: [VIDEO] The delivery of the lecture material was like butter to me. Might work differently for others though."},{"id":14769559,"author":"xtiansimon","month":"2017-07","story":"A Curated List of AI and Machine Learning Resources from Around the Web","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enjoyable course with a fresh presentation of the learning model","quote":"one course I very much enjoyed auditing was \"Learning from Data","text":"I'm a neophyte to ML, but one course I very much enjoyed auditing was \"Learning from Data\" [1] taught by Caltech Professor Yaser Abu-Mostafa. I found concept of the _Learning Model_ particularly fresh representation of the ML activity (starting at 14:39 in the first video linked here [2]) [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37265177,"author":"GoldenMonkey","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: In 2023 which is the best path to get good at machine and deep learning?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed as a learning resource for ML","quote":"Caltech machine learning intro course","text":"Caltech machine learning intro course: [VIDEO] karpathy's Zero to Hero series ( [LINK] ) meta llama 2 - is open source [LINK] Tools: ai - hosting Banana - Machine Learning Model Deployment on Serverless GPUs [LINK] pinecone - vector database: [LINK] how to run AI language models on a single cpu pc - [LINK]"},{"id":16168371,"author":"bonkerfied23","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: How to Seriously Start with Machine Learning and AI","stance":"rec","why":"Online lectures explaining the math and context behind each topic","quote":"I like how he explains stuff and adds some context behind the math of each topic.","text":"Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning by Christopher Bishop. Book: [LINK] Notes: Its very very math heavy but if you really want to grasp the concepts and the idea around each topic, this is one of the way to go. Online Lectures: [VIDEO] I like how he explains stuff and adds some context behind the math of each topic. Alternative: [LINK] A pretty good youtube channel that follows up on modern machine learning and he has all of his video tutorial demos on GitHub. Hope this helps fellow Machine Learner :)"},{"id":12723543,"author":"weishigoname","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite YouTube channel for developers?","stance":"rec","why":"Good machine learning lectures worth watching","quote":"there are some lectures about machine learning on youtube. I think they are good to watch","text":"there are some lectures about machine learning on youtube. I think they are good to watch: [VIDEO] and [LINK] and my favorite channels are Standford and MIT open courseware [LINK] and [LINK]"},{"id":11986189,"author":"selimthegrim","month":"2016-06","story":"A Stark Nuclear Warning","stance":"reference","why":"Caltech Learning from Data course covering VC dimension","quote":"He covers the VC dimension in","text":"[LINK] [VIDEO] (incidentally for graycat, Yaser Abumostafa is a Muslim Egyptian immigrant from Cairo) He covers the VC dimension in [LINK] , and leaves the proof to an appendix of the book."}],"r":[["UzxYlbK2c7E",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["lWZ2Bz0tS-s",1]]},"pYXy-A4siMw":{"wy":"The default link for newcomers wanting an approachable, entertaining introduction to AI risk and alignment.","q":{"x":"It's quite an approachable intro, often entertaining","i":34374754,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34374754,"author":"durbleflorp","month":"2023-01","story":"We could stumble into AI catastrophe","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Approachable intro answering common 'why not just do x' objections about AI risk.","quote":"It's quite an approachable intro, often entertaining","text":"I strongly suggest anyone doubtful of the reasoning behind these concerns or asking questions like \"well why don't we just do x to solve this?\" go check out Robert Miles' YouTube channel[1]. It's quite an approachable intro, often entertaining, doesn't take a long time to get through all the core videos, and very thoroughly answers all the \"why not just do x\" questions if you go through the whole set. He also does a good job of introducing a lot of the terms used in the field if you then want to go look up papers and get more into the details. One important point he makes is that when you're making a risk assessment you have to both consider the probability of something going wrong and the scope of the potential consequences. When the potential consequences are an existential threat you don't need a high probability to take them seriously. I also happen to think that if you watch all the material he makes a compelling argument that the odds of something going wrong are fairly high unless we start approaching AI research (and particularly safety and ethical concerns) drastically differently than we are now. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37772391,"author":"albumen","month":"2023-10","story":"Many scientists don’t want to tell the truth about climate change. Here’s why","stance":"rec","why":"Good intro to why AGI could go wrong.","quote":"here's a good intro","text":"If only. There's a lot of space in between \"our morality\" (please do define that) and \"what we want to be\" where AGI can royally screw us. Check out Robert Miles on AI safety, here's a good intro: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36124905,"author":"Veedrac","month":"2023-05","story":"Statement on AI Risk","stance":"rec","why":"Well-presented casual introduction to AI alignment arguments.","quote":"Robert Miles' videos are among the best presented arguments about specific points in this list","text":"The basic argument is trivial: it is plausible that future systems achieve superhuman capability; capable systems necessarily have instrumental goals; instrumental goals tend to converge; human preferences are unlikely to be preserved when other goals are heavily selected for unless intentionally preserved; we don't know how to make AI systems encode any complex preference robustly. Robert Miles' videos are among the best presented arguments about specific points in this list, primary on the alignment side rather than the capabilities side, that I have seen for casual introduction. Eg. this one on instrumental convergence: [LINK] Eg. this introduction to the topic: [VIDEO] He also has the community-led AI Safety FAQ, [LINK] , which gives brief answers to common questions. If you have specific questions I might be able to point to a more specific argument at a higher level of depth."},{"id":36125310,"author":"lambertsimnel","month":"2023-05","story":"Statement on AI Risk","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on AI risk; objective-function warning at 12:40.","quote":"his \"Intro to AI Safety, Remastered\" is a good place to start","text":"I recommend Robert Miles's YouTube channel, and his \"Intro to AI Safety, Remastered\" is a good place to start: [VIDEO] I find Robert Miles worryingly plausible when he says (about 12:40 into the video) \"if you have a sufficiently powerful agent and you manage to come up with a really good objective function, which covers the top 20 things that humans value, the 21st thing that humans value is probably gone forever\""},{"id":33873270,"author":"casebash","month":"2022-12","story":"Show HN: Help, I let GPTChat control my computer","stance":"rec","why":"Introductory AI safety resource suggested for linking.","quote":"","text":"Is there any chance you could add some links to AI Safety Resources such as Joe Carlsmith's Power Seeking AI ( [LINK] ) or Rob Miles Introduction to AI Safety ( [VIDEO] )? Perhaps you could link to a resource on the AI box experiment as well? I know you vaguely gesture at it, but would likely be better to explicitly link people to resources if they haven't heard of it themselves."},{"id":32540962,"author":"WhitneyLand","month":"2022-08","story":"Beyond Hyperanthropomorphism","stance":"rec","why":"Great introduction to AI safety concerns.","quote":"Great intro to the subject here","text":"Venkatesh’s argument is so poor it comes close to “not even wrong” territory. He doesn’t really cite any specific people or literature to disagree with, and instead makes only vague references to the “non-sense” of others. The most significant concerns around AI safety are not discussed nor even mentioned leading it to read almost as if he has no expertise whatsoever in this field, and indeed that seems to be the case. No need for an appeal to authority though - just taking a little time to hear the actual concerns and opinions on topic would be welcome. Great intro to the subject here, I wonder what his response would be? [VIDEO]"},{"id":36119708,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2023-05","story":"OpenAI chief goes before US Congress to propose licenses for building AI","stance":"reference","why":"One among several readings on AI risk.","quote":"","text":"Take your pick? [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] I could keep going but the reading on this spans for tens of thousands of pages of detailed reasoning and analysis, including mathematical proofs and lab-scale experiments."}],"r":[["L5pUA3LsEaw",2],["l9ZqXlHl65g",1],["oVfHeWTKjag",1],["ctS8FzqcRug",1],["ZeecOKBus3Q",1],["nnl6nY8YKHs",1],["yY96hTb8WgI",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1]]},"ainyK6fXku0":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as a surprisingly good cover that brings out the contempt in the lyrics.","q":{"x":"I really like William Shatner's cover. It brings out the contempt in the lyrics. And it rocks.","i":6388558,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":38248209,"author":"ur-whale","month":"2023-11","story":"From Aristocratic to Ordinary: Shifting Modes of Elite Distinction (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Shatner cover for fans of the Pulp original","quote":"you have to listen to this cover by Cap'n Kirk","text":"> Not to mention the Jarvis Cocker fascination If you like the Pulp (original AFAICT) version, you have to listen to this cover by Cap'n Kirk: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33074843,"author":"svnt","month":"2022-10","story":"From Burned Out Tech CEO to Amazon Warehouse Associate","stance":"rec","why":"Shatner cover of Common People is special","quote":"The Shatner cover is something special","text":"“You will never understand how it feels to live your life with no meaning or control, and with nowhere left to go. You’re amazed that they exist, and they burn so bright that you can only wonder why?” —- Pulp, Common People The Shatner cover is something special: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33131678,"author":"ThePhysicist","month":"2022-10","story":"My Trip to Space Filled Me with Overwhelming Sadness","stance":"rec","why":"Great Common People cover from Shatner's touching album","quote":"I found that quite touching as well","text":"I like him, he seems to be a thoughtful person. I once listened to his album (which has a great cover of the song \"Common People\", that jokes about how rich people see the world [1]), in which he reflects on the death of his previous wife, who drowned in their pool, I found that quite touching as well 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28790758,"author":"simias","month":"2021-10","story":"Going from $110k to $24k for more skin in the game","stance":"rec","why":"Surprisingly good Shatner cover of Common People","quote":"I also enjoy the cover of this song by William Shatner that's surprisingly good IMO","text":"Glad you liked it, I also enjoy the cover of this song by William Shatner that's surprisingly good IMO: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9777396,"author":"BuildTheRobots","month":"2015-06","story":"Chromium will no longer download/install the Hotword Shared Module","stance":"rec","why":"Shatner's spoken delivery of Common People is a standout","quote":"His cover of Common People (Pulp) is an immense example of this.","text":"somewhat even more off topic, but: Thanks to William Shatner for lots of things; least of all demonstrating how to deliver a line of dialogue without it sounding like you're reading off the back of a cereal packet and left your glasses at home. His cover of Common People (Pulp) is an immense example of this. [VIDEO] Yes Doctor Who, I'm staring you out whilst shaking a fist at you...."},{"id":6388558,"author":"shimfish","month":"2013-09","story":"The CEO of Panera Bread attempts to live off food stamps for a week","stance":"rec","why":"Shatner's cover of Common People brings out the lyrics' contempt","quote":"I really like William Shatner's cover. It brings out the contempt in the lyrics. And it rocks.","text":"Incidentally, I really like William Shatner's cover. It brings out the contempt in the lyrics. And it rocks. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24549324,"author":"Tomminn","month":"2020-09","story":"Why aren’t you more serious?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Shatner's spoken-word style, related to Has Been album","quote":"","text":"He later did an entire album called \"Has Been\" in this style, so I suspect so. Here's a taste: [VIDEO] I know this because Ben Folds had a hand in it, and I'm a long-time Ben Folds fan."}],"r":[["oVfHeWTKjag",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["uk3A41U0iO4",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["1EWQYAfuMYw",1],["A1vyB-O5i6E",1],["hMOwbNUpDQA",1],["nIoXOplUvAw",1]]},"rgaTLrZGlk0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a concise, approachable starting point for anyone asking how to learn music theory.","q":{"x":"I found this video to be an extremely helpful and concise explanation of music theory basics","i":22446974,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":27715008,"author":"foopod","month":"2021-07","story":"Ask HN: What is a cool technology to learn?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helpful intro for music beginners.","quote":"I found this video really helpful -","text":"Probably right on the edge of what you would define as technology.... Music! I am 30 and have spent my whole life not learning any music or instruments because it is 'too hard'. But now I am getting into music theory and wow it is really interesting and awesome. Highly recommend. I found this video really helpful - [VIDEO] ."},{"id":22446974,"author":"rdlw","month":"2020-02","story":"Programmers generate every possible melody in MIDI to prevent lawsuits","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise explanation showing connections between theory ideas.","quote":"I found this video to be an extremely helpful and concise explanation of music theory basics","text":"I found this video to be an extremely helpful and concise explanation of music theory basics: [VIDEO] \"Learn music theory in half an hour\" is obviously an exaggeration, but it really comes astoundingly close to fulfilling that promise. It contains a lot of information and each part builds on the previous parts, so it requires focus and maybe a few repetitions to 'get it', but I think the approach is fantastic for showing how many ideas of music theory are deeply connected. Not a book as you requested, but hopefully you'll find the other reply useful. As for some of your specific questions: Frequency is the same concept as radio frequency, but in this case refers to something we can directly sense. Radios transmit electromagnetic waves, which are photons moving at a certain rate, measured in Hertz, or cycles per second. Sound frequency refers to movement of air waves, so a more accurate analogy than radio waves is waves in a pond when a rock is thrown in. Human ears are sensitive to frequencies between 20 Hz and 20,000 Hz, so any sound you hear is a combination of frequencies. Natural language is helpful here, since higher frequencies sound 'higher' and lower frequencies sound 'lower'. A tone is a sound at a specific frequency, also known as a note. For example, 440 Hz is designated as the note A4 by the Geneva conventions, and this is what most instrument tunings are based off. Twelve tone equal temperament is the tuning system nearly all modern Western music uses. Certain ra"},{"id":34392965,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2023-01","story":"Interactive Music Theory Cheat Sheet","stance":"rec","why":"Takes the mystery out of music, shows patterns.","quote":"taking away so much mystery and showing 'patterns in the madness'","text":"This is fantastic. I didn't grow up learning music and found so much of it seeming arbitrary and incomprehensible. This really pairs well with a 30min video[0] I watched taking away so much mystery and showing 'patterns in the madness'. It would be good if there were some keybindings to play notes (e.g. digits for I, II, or letters for notes ignoring #/b, or \"r t y u i o p [\"). A toggle to label the notes that are part of the selected key could help a beginner like myself. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21824747,"author":"tarsinge","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: How Can I Learn Music Theory?","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of intervals, chords, and patterns.","quote":"","text":"It really depends on your objectives. If you want to compose pop music (including rock and electronic music) the risk is to lose yourself in complicated harmony \"rules\" that are mostly irrelevant to the style you want to play (on top of my mind parallel fifths prohibited in classical theory but they are commonplace in rock/metal) and only useful to analyze classical pieces. Personally as a guitarist I kind of hacked my way into music theory to being able to improvise over and compose pop/rock/electronic by only using intervals and scale degrees. I can hear chords progressions, I know why a V I cadence works, can identify and play in modes (dorian, lydian...), but I can't name the notes so transcribing into a DAW is cumbersome but at least I can compose freely. Not ideal but considering the minimal time I invested it's a very good return for me. Concretely the idea is to focus only on patterns and formulas. The steps would be to learn to play and hear intervals, then construct chords, e.g. Major chord is 4+3 semi-tones, minor is 3+4, another +3 is minor 7 or +4 for major 7. Then by using a scale (e.g white notes on the piano for key of C major/A minor) it's easy to find the chord on each degree. After that learn a few common chords progressions and being able to identify them in everyday songs should come naturally. Finally practice composing melodies by targeting chord tones and you are ready to start composing. I found this video does a very good job at providing this overvi"}],"r":[["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["0lJKucu6HJc",1],["ELeZAKCN4tY",1],["sR8M4zARBXY",1],["PtKhbbcc1Rc",1],["xOCurBYI_gY",1],["t_rzYnXEQlE",1],["D6EPuUdIC1E",1]]},"-R-eqJDQ2nU":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on fasting and nutrition as a deep dive from the lead researcher on time-restricted eating.","q":{"x":"Dr. Panda goes into detail about fasting and coffee. Great podcast about fasting.","i":17392837,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17392837,"author":"dominotw","month":"2018-06","story":"Losing 100 pounds in 276 days","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Panda goes into detail about fasting and coffee.","quote":"Dr. Panda goes into detail about fasting and coffee. Great podcast about fasting.","text":"I listened to a super interesting podcast about fasting [VIDEO] Dr. Panda goes into detail about fasting and coffee. Great podcast about fasting. Intermittent Fasting Confers Protection in CNS Autoimmunity by Altering the Gut Microbiota [LINK]"},{"id":18007596,"author":"bodhibyte","month":"2018-09","story":"Molecule produced during fasting has anti-aging effect on vascular system","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive on time-restricted feeding, obesity and heart health.","quote":"For those interested in a deeper dive on the benefits of water-only fasting","text":"I'm on a day 4 of a water-only fast and have completed a two-week medically supervised fast as well as many other shorter fast. As an anecdote, I can say water-only fasting in combination with a high nutrient density plant-based diet has helped me overcome some serious allergies and other health conditions. As an added bonus, I've lost 65 pounds from my peak weight over the last few years and my weight has been stable. It's not a panacea, but I've found it to be a net benefit. For those interested in a deeper dive on the benefits of water-only fasting and diets that try to mimic its benefits, as well as time-restricted feeding, see: Valter Longo, Ph.D. on Fasting-Mimicking Diet & Fasting for Longevity, Cancer & Multiple Sclerosis [1] Dr. Valter Longo on Resetting Autoimmunity and Rejuvenating Systems with Prolonged Fasting & the FMD [2] Dr. Satchin Panda on Time-Restricted Feeding and Its Effects on Obesity, Muscle Mass & Heart Health [3] Dr. Satchin Panda on Practical Implementation of Time-Restricted Eating & Shift Work Strategies [4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] [Edit to fix typos and formatting]"},{"id":14638006,"author":"eneveu","month":"2017-06","story":"The secret to a long and healthy life? Eat less","stance":"rec","why":"Interview on why eating timing matters for circadian rhythms.","quote":"As well as this video interview","text":"The issue with Ramadan is that they eat at night. It is indeed intermittent fasting, but it is not as good as intermittent fasting where you eat during the day, because eating is related to peripheral oscillators of our circadian rhythm. I heard about that a few weeks ago while listening to this podcast by Dr. Rhonda Patrick: [LINK] This article might also be of interest: [LINK] As well as this video interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14640450,"author":"moochachana","month":"2017-06","story":"The secret to a long and healthy life? Eat less","stance":"rec","why":"Panda on time-restricted feeding, obesity, muscle mass and heart health.","quote":"Regarding not eating breakfast, watch these videos from FoundMyFitness","text":"The reason you feel sick is because you're body isn't used to the shift from carbohydrates for energy and ketones as energy (body fat). You get brain fog because only fat in the form of ketones can cross the blood brain barrier. So adaptation to ketones are vital. You need to work into it. No different from exercise, or climate adaptation. Having cold showers shifts your body into using ketones more and more. Regarding not eating breakfast, watch these videos from FoundMyFitness: Dr. Satchin Panda on Time-Restricted Feeding and Its Effects on Obesity, Muscle Mass and Heart Health [VIDEO] Ruth Patterson, Ph.D. on Time-Restricted Eating in Humans & Breast Cancer Prevention [LINK] Time restricted eating, AKA your 16/8, is VERY important. Even if you don't immediately notice changes. Those changes could begin to occur 2 to 8 years later. Depending on adherence. Also based on Dr. Ruth Patterson's advice, skipping breakfast leads to eating late into the night, which is not good for your health generally."},{"id":14180878,"author":"dpatrick86","month":"2017-04","story":"You Can’t Trust What You Read About Nutrition","stance":"rec","why":"Satchin Panda describes his circadian-focused eating research.","quote":"Here's a video of the principal investigator (Satchin Panda) talking about his research...","text":"Interestingly, this has been the approach of a dietary study being conducted by a group doing research on a circadian-focused pattern of eating. The study uses a mobile app as part of their distributed clinical trial design. Participants take pictures of their food with only the option of further journaling. Kind of cool. Here's the website for the trial (& app)... [LINK] Here's a video of the principal investigator (Satchin Panda) talking about his research... [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AjSl4n_KdOY",1],["sK5i-N34im8",1],["dGVqrGmwOAw",1],["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["OQ5jsbhAv_M",1],["d6PyyatqJSE",1]]},"RMxB7zA-e4Y":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clearest introduction to what is inside an AC EV charging station and why it is much simpler than people assume.","q":{"x":"A quick intro into EVSE and why there is no power 'charger' in them","i":29440253,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40247482,"author":"Moto7451","month":"2024-05","story":"Creating a low-cost EV charging station with Arduino","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Broader explanation of what goes on inside an EVSE","quote":"In case you don't watch Technology Connections... 1. You probably should, Alec is great.","text":"In case you don't watch Technology Connections... 1. You probably should, Alec is great. 2. Here's a longer broader video about what goes on in an EVSE: [VIDEO] There are too many comments to go sling context left and right, but generally an EVSE is a controller board (what this is implementing), a contactor (this board uses relays), and a GFCI (in North American parlance), which his RCBO provides along with being a current limiting circuit breaker. Contactors are generally better for these use cases for reliability and current handling reasons but it wouldn't surprise me if SCRs and Relays are in use in low cost 16A EVSEs on Amazon."},{"id":37327899,"author":"stetrain","month":"2023-08","story":"EV Chargers Should Be Dumber","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what's inside an AC EV charger","quote":"A helpful video about what's inside an AC EV \"charger\"","text":"A helpful video about what's inside an AC EV \"charger\": [VIDEO] Really the only difference with the fancier ones is they have a screen and payment terminal or NFC terminal for activating and paying. Like a gas pump, although most want you to use their app and membership to save (them) on credit card processing fees. DC fast chargers are a whole different story, where the AC to DC conversion happens external to the car."},{"id":29440253,"author":"rusanu","month":"2021-12","story":"Bosch gives go-ahead for volume production of silicon carbide chips","stance":"rec","why":"Quick intro to what an EVSE is and isn't","quote":"A quick intro into EVSE and why there is no power 'charger' in them","text":"A quick intro into EVSE and why there is no power 'charger' in them, and the space they take is just empty air [VIDEO]"},{"id":26044500,"author":"alexgartrell","month":"2021-02","story":"Oil companies buying up EV charging networks: Shell acquires Ubitricity","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the many safety mechanisms preventing sparks in EVSE","quote":"there seem to be about a million and a half different mechanisms to prevent sparks from chargers.","text":"I'm not an electrical engineer, but from watching this video [0], there seem to be about a million and a half different mechanisms to prevent sparks from chargers. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25877722,"author":"abeppu","month":"2021-01","story":"Electric vehicles close to ‘tipping point’ of mass adoption","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm curious how many EV purchasers live in single family homes, or other places where they can provide their own ability to charge at home. I wonder if a new scale of adoption will require a change where most (or a lot) of street parking is by cheap charging. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1]]},"RuCnZClWwpQ":{"wy":"Recommended as an amazing documentary of traditional ironmaking, often used as a baseline for the labor required.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite videos is a documentary of a village in Africa that goes through the process of producing iron tools from scratch","i":24593725,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24593725,"author":"oflannabhra","month":"2020-09","story":"Iron, How Did They Make It? Part II","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Whole village produces iron tools from scratch to preserve knowledge.","quote":"One of my favorite videos is a documentary of a village in Africa that goes through the process of producing iron tools from scratch","text":"One of my favorite videos is a documentary of a village in Africa that goes through the process of producing iron tools from scratch, even though it is not required anymore, to pass on the knowledge. They find ore, construct furnaces, smelt the ore, refine the iron and then work it. It takes an entire village weeks to do this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32697469,"author":"kragen","month":"2022-09","story":"Making iron from sand [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on traditional ironmaking using only Neolithic materials.","quote":"It's amazing to see how much work the traditional technique required.","text":"If you're interested in traditional ironmaking, Christopher Roy filmed a documentary a few years back on the techniques used up to the beginning of the 20th century in Burkina Faso, From Iron Ore to Iron Hoe: Smelting Iron in Africa : [VIDEO] It's amazing to see how much work the traditional technique required. Though I don't know how much the techniques developed over the last 3000 years, none of the materials or manufacturing techniques exhibited in the documentary seem to require anything more than Neolithic products: leather, clay, wood, rope. (With the exception of using the cast-iron knife to carve the sluice, the same is true of Plant's video.) So it's particularly surprising to realize that the Neolithic started 12000 years ago and the Iron Age only started about 3500 years ago (the Painted Gray Ware culture in India). For 8500 years people all over the world had the tools at their disposal to make iron, but didn't know it. That's 3000 years longer than all of recorded history! It's similarly amazing to realize that from 3500 years ago until 2600-2100 years ago all iron was made by hugely inefficient processes like these rather than with blast furnaces, and that until 1000-500 years ago blast furnaces were only used in China. And it wasn't until Bessemer that they could be used to make steel. What similar opportunities are under our noses today? (More examples in [LINK] .) If you're interested in this sort of material culture unbootstrapping, you might also be interes"},{"id":24669297,"author":"guerrilla","month":"2020-10","story":"Iron, How Did They Make It, Part III: Hammer-Time","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary complements the iron-making blog series.","quote":"which I think more people should see in connection with this series","text":"Oh it's my favorite time of the week again on HN! I truly love these blogs. Someone last week posted this[1] documentary which I think more people should see in connection with this series. But I'm still collecting questions, which I may take to Quora or SO at some point but I'd love to know how did we get started with (i.e. discover) this process and how does it differ from steel. Apparently we will learn about the latter in the next blog though, so I can wait on that. [1]. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17750045,"author":"JorgeGT","month":"2018-08","story":"Google tracks your movements, like it or not","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter found it very amazing and super interesting.","quote":"the video itself is super interesting","text":"They seem to do a similar thing with Youtube. I have both Youtube watch history and Youtube search history off. The other day I watched a very amazing video about iron smelting in Africa. Now, each time a open a Youtube link, that specific iron smelting video appears as \"recommended for me\". So, it is obvious that Google remembers which videos I have watched and openly advertises them again to me. By the way, the video itself is super interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40201887,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-04","story":"A small lathe built in a Japanese prison camp (1949)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows artisanal smelting needs tens of person-days per kilogram.","quote":"artisanal manual processes require tens of person-days per kilogram of iron.","text":"a nail rusted into a lump of hematite is still a lump of very rich ore, and the hazard and labor involved in fishing it out of a pile of rotted wood is not disproportionate to the hazard and labor involved in reducing it back to iron, either by artisanal manual processes or by mass-production processes. artisanal manual processes require tens of person-days per kilogram of iron. see christopher roy's documentary [VIDEO] your point about entropy is confused; it's reminiscent of the creationist argument that evolution is impossible because it produces more ordered structures from less ordered ones. yes, human activities do increase entropy, mostly in the form of slightly increasing the amount of infrared radiation into space from earth, but less so than other biological activities they displace. this is almost completely irrelevant to the question of whether landfills are richer or poorer ore deposits than banded iron formations or karst bauxite deposits, because the contribution of that term to the system's total entropy change is insignificantly small, and can therefore easily be positive or negative"},{"id":16554678,"author":"kragen","month":"2018-03","story":"Lab-grown diamonds threaten viability of the real gems","stance":"reference","why":"Traditional Burkinabe smelting as baseline for labor productivity.","quote":"a traditional blacksmith family in Burkina Faso mining a few kilograms of iron with several person-days of work","text":"On a more-than-proof-of-concept scale, you have [VIDEO] , a traditional blacksmith family in Burkina Faso mining a few kilograms of iron with several person-days of work, which is probably about 1000× as productive as the Primitive Technology guy achieved (perhaps a gram of iron after about a person-day of work). But the US steel industry employs 87000 people to produce about 120 million tonnes of iron and steel per year, which works out to about 5000 kg of iron and steel per person-day. (And that's why Burkina Faso imports its iron instead of smelting it in the way demonstrated in that documentary, the way they had done for the previous 2500 years, until some French guys wrecked a Jeep there in the early 20th century.) So there are something like six or seven orders of magnitude over which \"high-tech\" machinery can increase your iron productivity, or, to look at it another way, over which the price of iron can vary even once you have the know-how."},{"id":10201763,"author":"kragen","month":"2015-09","story":"Building a hut with a tiled roof, underfloor heating and mud and stone walls","stance":"reference","why":"Outlines a workable bloomery approach to smelting iron.","quote":"outlines an approach that's definitely workable, but it's using a bloomery furnace","text":"He did use modern knowledge, just not modern tools. If you're using modern tools and materials, you can build a Hexayurt. And you should! My point wasn't that today access to knowledge is restricted or that houses today are expensive to build — quite the opposite! My point was that building a house with Stone Age tools and materials is very expensive, and that this is indicative of the causes of the general problem of pre-industrial poverty. I agree with you on that! I'm curious how much you could ease the process by using more modern knowledge, but starting without modern tools or materials. (You'll note that the OP used modern thermodynamics to design his kiln.) For example, could you smelt iron from sand? [VIDEO] outlines an approach that's definitely workable, but it's using a bloomery furnace — could you, for example, bootstrap a micro-Bessemer furnace? How about a lathe that you can cut threaded screw fasteners on? (Or even just wooden dowels.) FWIW Bucky's mass-produced-house dream has come to fruition, which is how I was able to live in a trailer park as a child."},{"id":17753658,"author":"brianpan","month":"2018-08","story":"Google tracks your movements, like it or not","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> By the way, the video itself is super interesting: [VIDEO] Covering up your tracks, eh? Ok, we'll play along. Everybody click on the link provided by Jorge \"Totally not into iron smelting more than anyone else\" GT."}],"r":[["oY59wZdCDo0",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["8s7NmMl_-yg",1],["lyu7v7nWzfo",1],["KVl5kMXz1vA",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["eUHBPuHS-7s",1],["a9xAKttWgP4",1]]},"aHun58mz3vI":{"wy":"HN invokes it as the best spoof of how news is formulaically produced, often when discussing media quality or automated news.","m":[{"id":36239411,"author":"chx","month":"2023-06","story":"Charlie Brooker Got ChatGPT to Write a Black Mirror Episode","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brooker is fantastic on how news is reported.","quote":"Charlie Brooker is fantastic: On how news is reported","text":"Charlie Brooker is fantastic: On how news is reported [VIDEO] On how mass shooting is reported and about the saturation [LINK]"},{"id":42921743,"author":"hgomersall","month":"2025-02","story":"He went to jail for stealing someone's identity, but it was his all along","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent though not quite on-point Brooker segments.","quote":"the not-quite-on-point, but nevertheless excellent two related segments below","text":"Most mainstream documentaries are full of fluff. You can generally read the transcript of a half hour programme in a couple of minutes. I thought Charlie Brooker might have a useful segment on it, but all I could find were the not-quite-on-point, but nevertheless excellent two related segments below: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16735681,"author":"cirrus-clouds","month":"2018-04","story":"Sinclair Made Dozens of Local News Anchors Recite the Same Script","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent spoof of news reporting that still rings true.","quote":"Charlie Brooker did an excellent spoof video of the way news is reported.","text":"Charlie Brooker did an excellent spoof video of the way news is reported. The video is 8 years old (made in 2010) but it still rings true today: How to report the news: [VIDEO] Every TV news report on the economy in one: [LINK]"},{"id":10897184,"author":"david-given","month":"2016-01","story":"Al Jazeera America to Shut Down in April","stance":"rec","why":"Best rundown of BBC house style.","quote":"This is the best run-down of the BBC house style I've seen","text":"The Al Jazeera that we get in the UK is apparently run by a lot of ex-BBC people, and uses a lot of the BBC house style, which means and news report will instantly look respectable to a big British market segment. Not that I've ever met anyone in the UK who's ever watched it, of course. This is the best run-down of the BBC house style I've seen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20969089,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2019-09","story":"The Case for Doing Nothing","stance":"reference","why":"Compared to text equivalent of the news-style segment.","quote":"The text equivalent of Charlie Brooker's \"How to report the news\" segment","text":"Pompous boring filler in the house style?[1] Yawn, no thanks. As you say, there are plenty of free outlets of information. What more explanation do you need? “Quality journalism” has its price, and the market doesn’t want to pay for it. Quality software has its price too, but the market prefers Linux. Wait, are you associating “free” with “low quality” or am I? Who can tell with quality these days. One thing’s for sure, it’s a difficult issue. [1] The text equivalent of Charlie Brooker's \"How to report the news\" segment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18486882,"author":"mrleiter","month":"2018-11","story":"Varying Speaking Styles with Neural Text-to-Speech","stance":"reference","why":"Brooker points out standard news shots and news-speak, relevant to automated anchors.","quote":"he pointed out all the standard videoshots and animations used these days to report on a topic [1]","text":"Before Charlie Brooker started producing Black Mirror, he already was a highly observant critic of society and media. At one point he produced a generic news snippet of an unspecified event, where he pointed out all the standard videoshots and animations used these days to report on a topic [1]. He presented this \"report\" with the standard news-speak pronunciation of sentences. With this achievement of Amazon, and the recent development by China in AI news-anchors [2], one is not far off to see news reporting being done mostly by machines. Yes, some videography must still be done manually, but the rest could be automated. What that means for the TV news industry? I don't know, but it certainly deserves discussion. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11094405,"author":"majewsky","month":"2016-02","story":"Twitter, to Save Itself, Must Scale Back World-Swallowing Ambitions","stance":"reference","why":"Example of filler street interviews in news reports.","quote":"Like in Charlie Brooker's \"How to report the news\"","text":"I don't think it's zeitgeist only. Or at least, not on such a short scale. The pre-internet equivalent of that is interviewing random people on the street about current events, to fill the 2:30 of your news report with something vaguely related to the subject. Like in Charlie Brooker's \"How to report the news\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":6897029,"author":"chestnut-tree","month":"2013-12","story":"News is bad for you","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the formulaic way news is reported.","quote":"These clips from Charlie Brooker (British TV presenter) give a good sense of the formulaic way news is reported","text":"I'm from the UK and I'd go so far as to say the majority of the British press are toxic. They have an incredibly inflated view of themselves and they couldn't care less about what's in the public interest (just look at how they've responded to the recommendations of the Leveson Inquiry which investigated the repulsive ethics and culture of the British press). The UK press empahtically follow their own self-interests and agendas (and that includes broadsheets like The Guardian). There will always be individual reporters who rise above the abysmally low standard of reporting - but they are a tiny minority. TV news is better in my opinion - they at least aspire to some measure of impartiality. These clips from Charlie Brooker (British TV presenter) give a good sense of the formulaic way news is reported (strong language in the videos). [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":42521963,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2024-12","story":"Open source maintainers are drowning in junk bug reports written by AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's my contribution for today: Charlie Brooker's How to Report the News - Newswipe - BBC","text":"I had to squint to be sure it was meant as comedy. Here's my contribution for today: Charlie Brooker's How to Report the News - Newswipe - BBC [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QspuCt1FM9M",1],["hzGaO4Kdz2Y",1],["Nnuq9PXbywA",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1],["BBwepkVurCI",1],["MQyxG4vTyZ8",1],["FF-tKLISfPE",1],["PezlFNTGWv4",1]]},"hzyFZcuHmeI":{"wy":"Commenters link it as a concise evidence-based rebuttal to common beliefs about milk's health benefits.","q":{"x":"Here's a great video on the topic from one of the co-authors of this article","i":17481078,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17481078,"author":"JshWright","month":"2018-07","story":"Seriously, Juice Is Not Healthy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great video on the topic by a co-author of the article.","quote":"Here's a great video on the topic from one of the co-authors of this article","text":"And basically no evidence that it's good for you. Here's a great video on the topic from one of the co-authors of this article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43063812,"author":"pinkmuffinere","month":"2025-02","story":"Jane Street's Figgie card game","stance":"rec","why":"Video that first convinced commenter about milk's health impact.","quote":"I think this video is what first convinced me","text":"I don’t think they’re quite as bad as soda, but I do think they’re fairly close. I think this video is what first convinced me (been a long time since I watched, sorry if I’m wrong about small details): [VIDEO]"},{"id":10859040,"author":"justncase80","month":"2016-01","story":"You Can’t Trust What You Read About Nutrition","stance":"rec","why":"More details on milk's effects on calcium, sugar, and anemia.","quote":"Here are some more details","text":"A gallon per week by yourself, or for a household? Milk isn't inherently bad but the things you need to know are: A) Consuming it doesn't really affect your calcium levels. B) Its basically just another sugary drink. C) Excessive amounts of it can cause anemia. D) Don't give much of it to small children due to reason C. Here are some more details: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8621452,"author":"therealdrag0","month":"2014-11","story":"Milk Might Not Be Doing You Much Good","stance":"rec","why":"Milk isn't particularly healthy or beneficial in the ways people think.","quote":"Healthcare Triage has a video on milk being not particularly healthy or even beneficial in ways people think","text":"Healthcare Triage has a video on milk being not particularly healthy or even beneficial in ways people think: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9415138,"author":"lkbm","month":"2015-04","story":"Simple Rules for Healthy Eating","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of Dr. Carroll railing against the milk industry.","quote":"Dr. Carroll regularly rails against \"the milk industrial complex","text":"It's definitely not settled science that you should never eat grains. Yes, some experts say you shouldn't, but if you're rejecting one expert because he disagrees with two others, it's definitely not sufficient to say it's \"based on science\". As for dairy, while he mentioned using butter without paranoia about fat, he didn't seem to advocate for dairy. Eating dairy might be bad, but \"don't eat dairy\" is bad (or ineffective) advice because _people won't follow it_. Don't make perfect the enemy of better. (If it makes you feel any better about him, Dr. Carroll regularly rails against \"the milk industrial complex\"[0][1][2][3].) [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["hT8GZlBBv5k",1],["SFBBjynBpSw",1],["NAsM30MAHLg",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["4QXZ2RzN_Oo",1],["AqeJzItldSQ",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1]]},"pqeJFYwnkjE":{"wy":"HN links it as the authoritative correction to common misuse of the term technical debt, straight from its originator.","q":{"x":"For the best description of technical debt, you can't do better than listen to the man who first coined the term.","i":10894626,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10894626,"author":"philbo","month":"2016-01","story":"Towards an understanding of technical debt","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best description of technical debt from its originator","quote":"For the best description of technical debt, you can't do better than listen to the man who first coined the term.","text":"For the best description of technical debt, you can't do better than listen to the man who first coined the term. Ward Cunningham explains: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34891797,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2023-02","story":"The age of cargo cult Agile must end","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what technical debt really means","quote":"A mess is not technical debt. Ward Cunningham explains Debt Metaphor","text":"While we're at it, let's talk about what Technical Debt really means. A mess is not technical debt. Ward Cunningham explains Debt Metaphor [VIDEO] transcript at [LINK]"},{"id":27531435,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2021-06","story":"Tech debt is not a burden, it's a strategic lever for success","stance":"rec","why":"Corrects common misunderstandings of technical debt","quote":"so I refer people to Ward Cunningham's explanation","text":"It's kind of a pet peeve of mine around technical debt. Some programmers and places I've worked get it wrong, so I refer people to Ward Cunningham's explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8637283,"author":"datashovel","month":"2014-11","story":"Your developers aren’t slow","stance":"rec","why":"Explains technical debt to non-technical people","quote":"resources I have used in the past to help explain to non-technical folks","text":"Here are a few of the resources I have used in the past to help explain to non-technical folks some of what a developer needs to deal with when involved in a large project. 1) Ward Cunningham explains \"technical debt\": [VIDEO] 2) comic strip - \"How Projects Really Work\": [LINK]"},{"id":38714941,"author":"avereveard","month":"2023-12","story":"All code is technical debt","stance":"reference","why":"Debt has a specific defined scope, not poor programming","quote":"heed Ward words directly, poor programming is not debt","text":"No sorry that is just bad encapsulation and goes under poor programming.Technical debt occurs when you write a good program to your current understanding of the solution knowing that the full understanding of the solution will come later, and that you need to do something now to get to that learning point later, and so you have to repay your partial understanding at the beginning with refactoring later. Here, heed Ward words directly, poor programming is not debt, and debt doesn't cover \"whatever reason programmer may have\" but has a specific well defined scope: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31835955,"author":"reedlaw","month":"2022-06","story":"The business impact of code quality (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Cunningham did not mean debt as poor code","quote":"Ward Cunningham, who coined the term, did not mean it in this way [1].","text":"The paper uses the term \"technical debt\" without defining it. From the context the term is used in its popular sense in which the primary source of debt is writing code poorly. Ward Cunningham, who coined the term, did not mean it in this way [1]. Wikipedia defines it as \"the implied cost of additional rework caused by choosing an easy (limited) solution now instead of using a better approach that would take longer\" [2] which is more aligned with Ward's intention. The problem with the current understanding of the technical debt metaphor is that it justifies poor code quality. Apparently it's like taking out a loan to make a sound investment. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK]"},{"id":30158964,"author":"crdrost","month":"2022-02","story":"How to approach and prioritize technical debt","stance":"reference","why":"Cunningham's original account of coining the debt metaphor","quote":"I coined the debt metaphor to explain the refactoring that we were doing on the WyCash product.","text":"Original sources by Ward Cunningham are not too hard to come by... > I became interested in the way metaphors influence how we think, after reading George Lakoff and Mark Johnson's Metaphors We Live By. An important idea is that we reason by analogy with the metaphors that have entered our language. > I coined the debt metaphor to explain the refactoring that we were doing on the WyCash product. This was an early product done in DigiTalk Smalltalk, and it was important to me that we accumulate the learnings we did about the application over time by modifying the program to to look as if we had known what we're doing all along, and to look as if it had been easy to do in Smalltalk. > The explanation I gave to my boss, and this was financial software, was a financial analogy I called the debt metaphor. And that said that, if we fail to make our program align with what we then understood to be the proper way to think, uh, about our financial objects—then we were going to continually stumble over that disagreement: and that would slow us down, which is like paying interest on a loan! With borrowed money, you can do something sooner than you might otherwise, but then until you pay back that money, you'll be paying interest. > I, uh, I thought borrowing money was a good idea, I thought that rushing software out the door to get some experience with it was a good idea. But that of course you would eventually go back, and as you learn things about that software, you would repay that l"},{"id":28008482,"author":"paphillips","month":"2021-07","story":"Technical debt is not debt; it’s not even technical","stance":"reference","why":"Cunningham's statements on aligning code with understanding","quote":"accumulate the learnings we did about the application over time by modifying the program to look as though we knew what we were doing all along","text":"'Technical Debt' in contemporary terms has certainly become a container holding far more than originally intended by Cunningham. The fundamental debt metaphor was about the need for ongoing re-factoring of past code and systems to match today's understanding , and the accumulating cost of failing to have the discipline and taking the time to do so. Cunningham's specific quotes from the video [0] linked in the article: \"...accumulate the learnings we did about the application over time by modifying the program to look as though we knew what we were doing all along, and to look as if it had been easy to do\" , and \"If we fail to make our program align with what we then understood to be the proper way to think about our financial objects then we were going to continually stumble over that disagreement, and that would slow us down\" . This problem is fundamental to software systems and how they change over time, and is not about making poor choices, tradeoffs, or generally poor engineering to ship code faster. A system or feature is initially built based on the requirements that were known at that point in time . Requirements are frequently not static though: that original set of requirements may continue to evolve over time, either as we better understand them after implementation, or when requirements are added, refined, or removed by the stakeholders or organization. Additionally, we can't view each feature or requirement in isolation over time: only when we see the overall set "},{"id":26950694,"author":"cratermoon","month":"2021-04","story":"Nobody cares about your clean code","stance":"reference","why":"Debt means best code for partial understanding, later improved","quote":"write the best code possible given a partial understanding of the problem so later when you do understand it better you improve the solution","text":"Except that technical debt is not about writing bad code, and the author of this article gets it completely wrong. \"The metaphor of debt is sometimes used to justify neglecting internal quality... Teams who do this end up maxing out all their credit cards, but still delivering later than they would have done had they put the effort into higher internal quality.\"[1] \"the whole debt metaphor, let's say, the ability to pay back debt, and make the debt metaphor work for your advantage depends upon your writing code that is clean enough to be able to refactor as you come to understand your problem\"[2] \"write the best code possible given a partial understanding of the problem so later when you do understand it better you improve the solution\"[3] 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] 3. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10928767,"author":"ColinDabritz","month":"2016-01","story":"How to Leverage Technical Debt","stance":"reference","why":"Original definition: gap between implementation and understanding","quote":"Wards definition is much more about the differences between the current implementation of the software and your understanding of the problem","text":"I enjoyed the article, although I disagreed on some minor points, some of the broad strokes seem solid to me. Very thought provoking. I think it's interesting to contrast the thrust of the article with Ward Cunningham's original definition of technical debt: [VIDEO] Wards definition is much more about the differences between the current implementation of the software and your understanding of the problem, particularly as your understanding changes over time. He explicitly says that \"tehcnical debt\" is NOT an excuse to write known-to-be-poor code, which is why I disagree with the //TOBECLEANED approach. This change in perspective shifts the point of view quite a bit. In some ways this problem defines a startup, your biggest problem is that you don't understand your problem. It's somewhat likely that you won't even have the same problem you'll successfully solve later. The first points are salient here, focus your engineering efforts on the things you don't understand yet, and the minimum support it needs to be viable. Shorten your feedback loop as you navigate the things you do not yet understand. In this light, a startup is actively SEEKING technical debt, because it represents gaining understanding about a problem, and exploring that space quickly. This doesn't mean you have to \"cut corners\" and do things you know to be flawed, but it suggests focusing on small 'forays' into the unknown, gaining understanding quickly, and being willing to throw away your exploration for a ne"},{"id":10627746,"author":"LoSboccacc","month":"2015-11","story":"Technical Debt Is a Good Thing","stance":"reference","why":"Cunningham's view on shortcuts versus debt","quote":"let Ward Cunningham itself explain what he thinks about taking shortucts and calling it technical debt","text":">Knowing when to release a feature and go into technical debt is one part art and one part science. let Ward Cunningham itself explain what he thinks about taking shortucts and calling it technical debt [VIDEO]"},{"id":9950164,"author":"6d0debc071","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: How often does technical debt lead to the failure of a startup?","stance":"reference","why":"The original debt metaphor explained","quote":"Essentially how well you can write a program to reflect your understanding of a problem.","text":"It's a metaphor: [VIDEO] Essentially how well you can write a program to reflect your understanding of a problem. Though it has come to more generally mean writing crap in a lot of cases."},{"id":8357808,"author":"RKoutnik","month":"2014-09","story":"It's called “Ship” not “Shit”","stance":"reference","why":"Debt is ok when you do not know direction","quote":"taking on this technical debt is ok when you don't know which direction to go","text":"This reminds me of the Humbug in The Phantom Tollbooth, who is described as \"[someone] who always managed to be the first with the wrong answer\" and later tries to argue that it was the question itself, not the answer, that was wrong. It's a shockingly good lesson that I seem to re-learn every six months or so. Being first is never a virtue in and of itself. MVPs that sacrifice quality for speed (instead of sacrificing scope) always end up as painstaking rewrites at best, and slow slogs to failure at worst. Something I think most miss is that taking on this technical debt is ok when you don't know which direction to go [0]. Get something to market to learn , not to be \"first\" or \"right\". [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4499039,"author":"adrianhoward","month":"2012-09","story":"Everything Gets Painful & Slow as You Scale","stance":"reference","why":"Cunningham on debt as something noticed later and refactored","quote":"Here's a video of Ward talking about technical debt (referenced in Fowler's definition)","text":"Interesting that this repeats Martin Fowler's definition of technical debt ( [LINK] ). While Fowler references Ward Cunningham as the originator of the term I think he gives an interestingly different definition. Fowler says \"You have a piece of functionality that you need to add to your system. You see two ways to do it, one is quick to do but is messy - you are sure that it will make further changes harder in the future. The other results in a cleaner design, but will take longer to put in place. Technical Debt is a wonderful metaphor developed by Ward Cunningham to help us think about this problem. In this metaphor, doing things the quick and dirty way sets us up with a technical debt, which is similar to a financial debt. \" Here the decision to take on technical debt is a deliberate one. You knowingly taking the quick-n-dirty route because of time constraints, not because you don't understand the \"good\" design. Here's a video of Ward talking about technical debt (referenced in Fowler's definition) [VIDEO] Here he talks about debt being something that you notice later. You learn more about what the solution should be, and need to go back and refactor your code so it reflects your new best understanding of the code. Debt is something that builds up over time, and you need to pay it off regularly so that the debt doesn't become disabling. At about 3:20 he talks explicitly about the idea of knowingly writing poor code: \"A lot of bloggers at least have explained the debt metap"},{"id":2888752,"author":"zupatol","month":"2011-08","story":"A Mess is not a Technical Debt","stance":"reference","why":"Cunningham explains debt as code written before understanding the problem","quote":"He explains that technical debt is code written before you have a complete understanding of the problem to solve.","text":"Ward Cunningham disagrees with your interpretation of his metaphor. [VIDEO] He explains that technical debt is code written before you have a complete understanding of the problem to solve. You repay the debt by refactoring it when you have a better understanding. This only works if the code is clean enough to be refactored."},{"id":730101,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2009-07","story":"Embrace technical debt","stance":"reference","why":"Ward Cunningham on technical debt","quote":"(Video: Ward Cunningham on technical debt)","text":"We (or at least I) also think of it as leverage. We also have a very strong 80/20 ethic, so we don't have particular qualms about using the leverage that this debt can provide. We don't do anything magical; there is no secret sauce, noe no spreadsheets with formulae or anything fancy; it's literally just a series of wiki articles and Jira tickets with issue type \"Debt\" so we can easily find the documented debt and make plans when/whether to retire it. So the concrete formal mechanism is \"use your bug tracking system and flag them somehow so you can filter/search for them.\" I will add some additional links that our top-level debt page references as useful: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] (Video: Ward Cunningham on technical debt)"}],"r":[["oV9rvDllKEg",1],["aAb7hSCtvGw",1]]},"v0YEaeIClKY":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a quick, visual way to understand Euler's identity.","q":{"x":"3blue1brown explains it better than I could","i":25277479,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25652591,"author":"davidwf","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What YouTube channels do you subscribe to?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great first 3Blue1Brown video on how e^pi*i works.","quote":"3Blue1Brown: Amazing math visualizations","text":"I keep a Google Document of these to share just in case people ask about my YouTube interests. I Actually Watched All of Them I have actually watched ALL of the videos on these channels and I don’t even feel bad about it :-) I actually like new videos from some of the ones below (like NativLang, for example) but they maybe don’t hold up in the early videos. - Space Time: PBS Physics, sooo good -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Lindsay Ellis: Really, really intelligent video essays about film and culture -- [LINK] -- Great First Video: [LINK] - Periodic Videos: Chemistry, including a video for every element -- [LINK] -- Great first video (obviously): [LINK] Other Math and Science - 3Blue1Brown: Amazing math visualizations -- [LINK] -- Great first video, how e^pi*i works: [VIDEO] - NumberPhile: Fun math videos -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Eons: Biology! -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Infinite Series: Math math math, really really hard math -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - SciShow: Science! More poppy but fun -- [LINK] -- Silly first show: [LINK] -- Actual physics: [LINK] - VSauce: Science-y? -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Practical Engineering: -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] Other Humanities - Alliterative: Connections via language -- [LINK] -- Great first video about gin and gimlets: [LINK] - Bon Appetit: Such fun food stuff! -- Gourmet Makes: [LINK] -- It’s Alive: [LINK] - Masaman: Genetics and human migration (an"},{"id":27351197,"author":"adrusi","month":"2021-06","story":"Show HN: Kalk, A calculator with math syntax, complex numbers","stance":"rec","why":"Builds intuition for Euler's identity, though glosses over rotation by i.","quote":"This video [1] kinda helps, although it glosses over the critical fact","text":"You actually have to understand calculus for that to actually prove it to you, but, you can show that it seems to work out numerically with a bit of programming knowledge, which I imagine is more common on Hacker News. I don't think either the numerical or symbolic proofs really help build intuition though. This video [1] kinda helps, although it glosses over the critical fact \"multiplying by i rotates counterclockwise 90°.\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25277479,"author":"kortex","month":"2020-12","story":"Alternative notation for exponents, logs and roots? (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Geometric understanding of e to the pi i.","quote":"3blue1brown explains it better than I could","text":"IMHO, the \"deeper truth\" is addition is translation and multiplication is rotation/stretching (at least when it comes to rings and fields). It involves getting a geometric understanding of e to the pi i, and 3blue1brown explains it better than I could: [LINK] [6.28 min] [VIDEO] [3.14 min] [LINK] [25 min]"},{"id":23107685,"author":"dgritsko","month":"2020-05","story":"Why is e^(pi I) = -1?","stance":"rec","why":"Short visual explanation of Euler's identity.","quote":"is a nice compliment to this post.","text":"3Blue1Brown's video \"Understanding e to the i pi in 3.14 minutes\" is a nice compliment to this post. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["mvmuCPvRoWQ",1],["C1KT8PS6Zs4",1],["EMKLeS-Uq_8",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["Nwew5gHoh3E",1],["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["K8gV05nS7mc",1],["0olpSN6_TCc",1],["6Af6b_wyiwI",1]]},"5HbYScltf1c":{"wy":"Commenters bring it up in threads on phones, boredom, attention and children's screen use as a funny but resonant take.","q":{"x":"arrived at the same conclusion much more concisely nearly a decade ago","i":27371886,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27371886,"author":"Apocryphon","month":"2021-06","story":"Stop overstimulating your mind with digital entertainment","stance":"rec","why":"Reaches same conclusion as the article more concisely.","quote":"arrived at the same conclusion much more concisely nearly a decade ago","text":"The best interpretation of this post is in its warnings against digital submersion, but when it veers into cultural critique it itself is being very middlebrow and dismissive (\"just listen to classical music! Modern music sucks!\") There is a good post in here about how externalizing negative emotion to the internet is indeed addictive behavior, an emotional crutch. Unfortunately this one ain't it. Not to mention it's hardly breaking new ground with this thesis- pre-cancellation Louis C.K. arrived at the same conclusion much more concisely nearly a decade ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27493219,"author":"jonplackett","month":"2021-06","story":"Doing something is better than doing nothing for most people: study (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Clip on losing the ability to sit with your thoughts.","quote":"a clip of Louis CK talking about losing the ability to just sit with your own thoughts and be really sad.","text":"This reminds me of a clip of Louis CK talking about losing the ability to just sit with your own thoughts and be really sad. [VIDEO] Shame he turned out to be a sexual deviant because he said some interesting things."},{"id":26837029,"author":"whitepaint","month":"2021-04","story":"Dear Zuckerberg, We're writing to urge you to cancel Instagram for children [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Great comments on raising children and phones.","quote":"Louis CK had great comments about this","text":"Everyone's insane so I must be as well. Come on man. I totally agree with the OP, take personal responsibility. You raise your children not Zuck. Louis CK had great comments about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11658335,"author":"itchynosedev","month":"2016-05","story":"Why I Deleted My Social Media Accounts","stance":"rec","why":"Smartphone bit that rings true despite being jokey.","quote":"I love this bit Louis CK did about smartphones, even though a bit jokish but rings true","text":"Sometimes people coerce me into using services, eg development team found a cool service to manage the project issues or designers want to check out the tool to manage the assets. There's no way around it. I found password manager tools to be invaluable in maintaining the accounts, \"fire and forget\". They usually opt you in for the marketing emails, but unsubscribing is just one click away in 99% of the cases. You are spot on about the mobile devices enabling addictive behavior. I used to have an old Nokia phone, with physical buttons during the smartphones craze and wasn't able to consume entertainment media while away from my computer. I though I'd upgrade to be able to interact better with friends through WhatsApp and whatnot and realized how much more time I fill my brain with junk media, because the phone is always there hence everything is at the tip of the finger. Although, I found playing chess on my phone to be very convenient, it's been ages since I wanted to try to learn playing chess. Technology is a double-edged sword and it definitely requires to exert more self control. It's easy not to be bored, to be somewhere in between of being satisfied and not achieving or progressing much. I love this bit Louis CK did about smartphones, even though a bit jokish but rings true: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7663437,"author":"Smudge","month":"2014-04","story":"A Gossip App Brought My High School to a Halt","stance":"rec","why":"Louis C.K.'s view on kids and phones.","quote":"I think Louis C.K. has a pretty smart perspective","text":"It's no surprise that, when left unchecked, teenagers (whose brains actually lack some capacity for empathy) would generate extremely un-empathetic content. And it is certainly not limited to children -- adults can be just as bad or worse. This is a topic that has come up in different forms many times as the Internet has grown. One way to reign in the vitriol would be to force everyone to use their real names (see Google+'s 'real names' policy), but I don't feel this should ever be a requirement for participation in an online service. For the service itself to require it is one thing, but if the government ever tried to enforce this I'd consider it a human rights violation. (The debate over real names vs. pseudonymity vs. anonymity is a topic I could go into at much length). In the case of Yik Yak, rather than shifting blame onto the app, I instead wonder how/why any parents would allow their children to freely install such apps on their phones. Sure, kids will be kids (and will always find covert ways of misbehaving), but there should be some form of supervision available for parents of smartphone users, don't you think? I think Louis C.K. has a pretty smart perspective: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18173141,"author":"Alterlife","month":"2018-10","story":"Shutting Down Google+ for Consumers","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK's interview quote on phones and feeling nothing fully.","quote":"You never feel completely sad or completely happy, you just feel kinda satisfied with your product... and then you die.","text":"> \"so that you're never too satisfied or unsatisfied, just constantly craving more.\" That quote reminds me of Louis CK's interview with Conan ( [VIDEO] ) where he says about mobile phones: \"You never feel completely sad or completely happy, you just feel kinda satisfied with your product... and then you die.\""},{"id":11806445,"author":"ericzawo","month":"2016-05","story":"The attention economy","stance":"reference","why":"Louis CK on just being a person.","quote":"","text":"I think there's reason to be optimistic. For example, social media and the internet have gave way to many new friendships and communities that simply wouldn't have been possible otherwise. While I understand and, too, struggle with the collective feeling of modern loneliness, I think ultimately there's more avenues than ever for people to bond over ideas. However, you are right that even more increased demand for our headspace in exchange for moments of reflection and, as Louis CK[1] says it, \"just being a person,\" are cause for concern. 1 [VIDEO]"},{"id":10305710,"author":"copsarebastards","month":"2015-09","story":"Boredom is not a problem to be solved, it's the privilege of a free mind","stance":"reference","why":"Louis C.K. on being alone with your thoughts.","quote":"Louis C.K. also talks about this","text":"This article is basically just a different definition of the word \"boredom\" than is being used by most people. It's trying to communicate something insightful (that we should be okay with being alone and undistracted with our own thoughts). But I don't think that's what most people mean when they say \"boredom\", and there's plenty of deeper discussion on this topic in literature about mindfulness. Louis C.K. also talks about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9836082,"author":"chuckcode","month":"2015-07","story":"This Industry is Fucked","stance":"reference","why":"Routine on why kids lack empathy without seeing the other person.","quote":"It reminds me of Louis CK's routine about why he doesn't give his kids cell phones","text":"The level of abuse the author has endured is ridiculous and she certainly has my sympathies. It reminds me of Louis CK's routine about why he doesn't give his kids cell phones ( [VIDEO] ) which boils down to without seeing the other person they can't empathize about the effect their words will have on other people. I can't help but wonder how these pseudo-anonymous people will feel when they find out that they aren't all that anonymous in the long run. When gawker unmasked \"Reddits biggest troll\" Violentacrez he certainly had a change of feelings when he had to stand behind his words and actions[1]. Freedom of speech is one of our most important values and anonymity is an important source for social change and equality for all. Using these gifts to rain down insults on others rarely works out well though in person and likely won't work out well on the internet in the long run either. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":7047203,"author":"adnzzzzZ","month":"2014-01","story":"How to stop feeling lonely","stance":"reference","why":"Loosely related Louis C.K. bit on being alone.","quote":"Kinda related","text":"My experience with curing loneliness is very contrary to what the article states in the sense that I found a way to not feel lonely by being more disconnected from other people. I realized that I only felt lonely when my expectations as to how other people should give me their time/attention were not met, so I purposefully decided to decrease the number and intensity of interactions with other people. I haven't felt that soul crushing loneliness that cripples you in about a year now, and I like to think that that only happened because of lowered expectations (over time as you interact less intensely with people you'll naturally come to expect less of them) and because of learning how to be alone (being alone and being lonely are not the same thing). The article states you should be around people you know every day and I find that pretty weird. I think the best way to learn how to not feel lonely is to learn to be content with yourself, otherwise you'll always rely on other people and so your feelings of loneliness will fail to go away as soon as other people fail you. Kinda related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6537112,"author":"hawkharris","month":"2013-10","story":"Why I Silence Calls Even When I'm Free","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Louis makes some insightful points that go along with the author's argument.","text":"We should all turn off our phones until a Bruce Springsteen song makes us burst out crying: [VIDEO] I'm joking, of course, but Louis makes some insightful points that go along with the author's argument."}],"r":[["uTRUQ-RKfUs",1],["xzYgiOC9cj4",1],["K1WrHH-WtaA",1],["2nBPN-MKefA",1]]},"6BhV-o77RqQ":{"wy":"HN commenters point people to it as a cheap, simple, effective way to kill mosquitoes, and some report trying it successfully.","q":{"x":"attach a screen (or net) to a high powered fan and catch/kill insane amount of mosquitoes.","i":45893748,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45893748,"author":"al_borland","month":"2025-11","story":"Ask HN: Effective way to deal with mosquitoes?","stance":"rec","why":"Screen on high-powered fan catches many mosquitoes.","quote":"attach a screen (or net) to a high powered fan and catch/kill insane amount of mosquitoes.","text":"There are some YouTube videos out there where guys attach a screen (or net) to a high powered fan and catch/kill insane amount of mosquitoes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44736861,"author":"johnthedebs","month":"2025-07","story":"Try the Mosquito Bucket of Death","stance":"rec","why":"Fan with screen traps mosquitoes; user tried it successfully.","quote":"Another technique I've tried which works (observably) well is described in this video","text":"The comments saying that other sources of water need to be removed are spot on. Another technique I've tried which works (observably) well is described in this video: [VIDEO] tl;dw: Get a big drum fan with a screen on the back, attached with small/powerful magnets. Mosquitos are such poor flyers that they get pulled against the screen and can't escape, and they pretty quickly desiccate and die. Most other flying insects don't get caught, although there is a bit of collateral (some moths and lacewings, unfortunately). Another benefit of the fan is that you can hang out in front of it and mosquitos mostly won't bother you there either. I did this in our shared backyard space in Brooklyn and would catch hundreds/thousands of mosquitos per week. Despite that, there were still a ton of mosquitos in the area so it's best combined with other methods of control. edit: better/updated video link"},{"id":32536629,"author":"rainbowzootsuit","month":"2022-08","story":"Scientists discover how mosquitoes can ‘sniff out’ humans","stance":"rec","why":"Window screen on a fan to kill mosquitoes.","quote":"the window screen on a fan one is a personal favorite","text":"There's a lot of tricks out there for killing mosquitoes but the window screen on a fan one is a personal favorite: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16973119,"author":"ryanseys","month":"2018-05","story":"Illnesses from Mosquito, Tick, and Flea Bites Increasing in the US","stance":"rec","why":"Simple fan-based mosquito trap.","quote":"Do it!","text":"Do it! I've seen videos floating around E.g. [0] that are as simple as a giant fan that sucks in mosquitoes. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29754394,"author":"drited","month":"2022-01","story":"Predict 3D position of flying bugs and pest to shoot down them with a laser","stance":"reference","why":"Fan trap using carbonated water as attractant.","quote":"He uses an open bottle of carbonated water to attract them to the fan/screen trap","text":"Ya I saw this Dan Rojas video linked on HN a while back when the laser mosquito idea came up. He uses an open bottle of carbonated water to attract them to the fan/screen trap [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AD5aAd8Oy84",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["aIHj3qMRqqE",1]]},"LWGJA9i18Co":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it as a creative music video shot entirely in zero gravity, citing it in threads about parabolic flights and space.","q":{"x":"One of my preferred video clip ever was filmed entirely during a parabolic flight","i":32823675,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32823675,"author":"bjkchoy","month":"2022-09","story":"Weightless: Parabolic flight on an A310","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Music video shot entirely during a parabolic flight.","quote":"One of my preferred video clip ever was filmed entirely during a parabolic flight","text":"One of my preferred video clip ever was filmed entirely during a parabolic flight: OK Go, Upside Down & Inside Out [VIDEO]"},{"id":18675600,"author":"hathawsh","month":"2018-12","story":"Virgin Galactic successfully reaches space","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Music video filmed on a zero-g parabolic flight.","quote":"You owe it to yourself to see these amazing videos filmed on planes that simulate zero G","text":"They fly the plane downward, matching the vertical acceleration with the acceleration caused by gravity. You owe it to yourself to see these amazing videos filmed on planes that simulate zero G: OK Go - [VIDEO] Physics Girl - [LINK] Veritasium - [LINK]"},{"id":48098741,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2026-05","story":"The Greatest Shot in Television: James Burke Had One Chance to Nail This Scene","stance":"rec","why":"Creative OK Go video filmed in a zero-g plane.","quote":"A number of their music videos are quite creative","text":"A number of their music videos are quite creative: * in a zero-G plane: [VIDEO] * one-take (?) on electric unicycles: [LINK] * 4.2s real-time scene with the footage slowed down to 4 minutes: [LINK] * Chevrolet-sponsored video where they use a moving car as an instrument: [LINK]"}],"r":[["zT58pzY41wA",1],["j4H9LL7A-nQ",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["MejbOFk7H6c",1],["qWKz3psejb0",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["QvW61K2s0tA",1],["o1Fyn_h6LKU",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["-VsmF9m_Nt8",1]]},"ZD_3_gsgsnk":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up in geothermal, greenhouse and vertical farming threads as a low-tech example of growing citrus in a cold climate.","q":{"x":"Really interesting story/concept and I'm hoping to replicate on a small scale","i":34280154,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48741713,"author":"culi","month":"2026-07","story":"Forestiere Underground Gardens","stance":"rec","why":"Citrus grown in Nebraska using trenches, from a fascinating channel.","quote":"Almost all the videos on this channel are fascinating btw.","text":"Almost all the videos on this channel are fascinating btw. Greenhouses enveloping entire houses/villages[0][1] in northern Europe to earthships[2] to someone growing citrus in Nebraska using trenches[3] to entire villages underground in Australia[4] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK] I'd also recommend the channels FLORB and Happen Films though they're admittedly a little corny. [LINK] [LINK] Crime Pays But Botany Doesn't recently did a tour of an interesting alt home as well. [LINK]"},{"id":34280154,"author":"kingsloi","month":"2023-01","story":"Sunken Greenhouse","stance":"rec","why":"Sunken greenhouse growing citrus in Nebraska snow, heated and cooled geothermally.","quote":"Really interesting story/concept and I'm hoping to replicate on a small scale","text":"If this interests you, I recommend checking out this video by Russ Finch, who grows citruses (orange, lemons, etc), figs, pomegranates in his sunken greenhouse in the middle of Nebraska in the snow/20F, and uses geothermal heating (long tubes buried underground) to heat it during the winter months, and cool in the summer months. Really interesting story/concept and I'm hoping to replicate on a small scale when I renovate my back yard in the coming years. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29007857,"author":"mythrwy","month":"2021-10","story":"Geothermal's path to relevance: cheap drilling","stance":"rec","why":"Oranges in Nebraska with underground tubes for heating and cooling.","quote":"Check out this guy, he grows oranges in Nebraska in thermal heated greenhouses.","text":"Yes I think that would work. Check out this guy, he grows oranges in Nebraska in thermal heated greenhouses. As I recall he talks about cooling as well. He has tubes around 8 feet under the ground running around the yard. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20246933,"author":"johnohara","month":"2019-06","story":"Swedish Couple Builds Greenhouse Around Home","stance":"rec","why":"Russ Finch grows citrus in Nebraska using geothermal energy.","quote":"One of my favorite Kirsten Dirksen videos","text":"Completely agree. One of my favorite Kirsten Dirksen videos is about Russ Finch using geothermal energy to grow citrus in a greenhouse located near Alliance, NE. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39927538,"author":"westurner","month":"2024-04","story":"Engineers develop 90-95% efficient electricity storage with piles of gravel","stance":"reference","why":"Passive geothermal half-buried greenhouse producing winter citrus at about $25k.","quote":"Passive , Net Zero, Carbon Sink solar geothermal half-buried greenhouse that produces citrus in the winter at a colder northern latitude","text":"Very competitively efficient, but you don't need 932F for greenhouses? /? oranges in Nebraska; [LINK] - \"Nebraska retiree uses earths's heat to grow oranges in snow\" in Alliance, NE, USA at -40F sometimes [VIDEO] : Passive , Net Zero, Carbon Sink solar geothermal half-buried greenhouse that produces citrus in the winter at a colder northern latitude Geothermal air with 6\" tubes, 8ft down , out 230ft (75 3) into the yard, blower, reflective sheeting on the south sloped back wall; est $25k build cost. 30F interval with no fans on in the winter. ; Net zero greenhouses probably win UN SDG Indicator 13.2.2 Total greenhouse gas emissions per year ; UN SDG Goal 13. Take urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts* A New Zealand word for an [excavated] [semi-] underground greenhouse is \"Walipini\". FWIU from YouTube, there's a new style of Northern Chinese greenhouse now in Canada: they build a big wall of clay/soil on one long side and lower a blanket over the entire greenhouse with a TODO HP motor in the evening to keep heat in the greenhouse at night. The wing shape makes it's easy to capture rainwater in a trough on just the one side. Geodesic domes are very resilient, easy to assemble, and can also be semi-underground with vents at the top to actuate. An aquarium heater can heat a tank of water to heat an arctic greenhouse. Geodesic domes get so hot in the summer that it's necessary to hang cloth to shade and cool. Also learned from YT South Korea: (JADAM JWA (Castille"},{"id":35968684,"author":"beembeem","month":"2023-05","story":"Renewables supplied 65% of new US utility-scale generating capacity in Q1 2023","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks for linking those sources. I think this is the key part of the article: > increased demand over the coming years, including data centres, cryptocurrency miners and greenhouses. Price them out. Don't give sweetheart deals to uses that aren't beneficial to the local economy. Additionally, these uses don't necessitate baseload. Crypto should be using cheap, renewable variable power sources. I'm genuinely curious what greenhouses means in this case. Heating indoor crops in the winter? Geothermal would be more effective for this. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34961381,"author":"julienchastang","month":"2023-02","story":"The vertical farming bubble is finally popping","stance":"reference","why":"Ingeniously designed earth-tube geothermal greenhouse for citrus.","quote":"a retired postal worker in Nebraska who grows citrus fruit in an ingeniously designed greenhouse heated with geothermal.","text":"Slightly off topic, but I saw this video [0] about a retired postal worker in Nebraska who grows citrus fruit in an ingeniously designed greenhouse heated with geothermal. (Not magma geothermal, but earth tubes geothermal that treats the ground as an infinite thermodynamic reservoir. Works in the same way as a refrigerator.) Can anyone here comment on if this could be done at scale? Could the economics be feasible? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33163469,"author":"mountaintimefrm","month":"2022-10","story":"Geothermal may beat batteries for energy storage","stance":"reference","why":"Example of useful low-grade geothermal.","quote":"Even low grade geothermal is pretty awesome.","text":"Even low grade geothermal is pretty awesome. Example: \"Nebraska retiree uses earths's heat to grow oranges in snow\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29220568,"author":"pengaru","month":"2021-11","story":"Colorado 'solar garden' is a farm under solar panels","stance":"reference","why":"Earth tubes with partially buried, oriented greenhouses.","quote":"This guy used \"Earth Tubes\" in combination with partially buried and appropriately oriented greenhouses","text":"> My family grew strawberries in eastern Colorado for a few years and one of the big problems was the extreme sun. In order to extend the growing season we used low tunnels to shield to plants from frost. However, the low tunnels during the peak summer months would act like a lens melting irrigation lines and even damaging produce. Sounds like problems more ground-coupling of the thermals would help mitigate. This guy used \"Earth Tubes\" in combination with partially buried and appropriately oriented greenhouses: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25852084,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-01","story":"Vertical farms grow veggies on site at restaurants and grocery stores","stance":"reference","why":"Small-scale example of an orange grower in a cold zone.","quote":"Here is a small scale example","text":"Agreed and this has been proven already in greenhouse farming. There are many greenhouse farmers that grow crops that would otherwise be impossible to grow in their zone. One example is a orange tree grower in a very cold zone that can sell oranges to his local community at a tiny fraction of what it costs to ship oranges from California. He still makes a profit and the produce is fresh. Here is a small scale example [1] but I can't fault this guy for keeping it small given his advanced age. I hope I have that much energy when I am older. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":20783111,"author":"mythrwy","month":"2019-08","story":"Cooling a house without air conditioning","stance":"reference","why":"Grows oranges in Nebraska with little electricity; same principles can cool.","quote":"This guy grows oranges in Nebraska using very little electricity.","text":"This guy grows oranges in Nebraska using very little electricity. He mentions in the video the same principals can be used for cooling. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["86gyW0vUmVs",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["akVL7QY0S8A",1],["ZSPkcpGmflE",1]]},"giQ8xEWjnBs":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear explanation of Starlink's purpose and the case that it can beat fiber on latency for long routes.","q":{"x":"the whole video is worth watching","i":24291471,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24346734,"author":"hangonhn","month":"2020-09","story":"Amazon drivers are hanging smartphones in trees to get more work","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Starlink is useful and why traders would pay for it","quote":"It also does a much better job of explaining it than I have","text":"Right, so if you can't go through the crust then going up would be another option. Over long enough of a distance, say NYC to London (or even farther), the overhead of going up first and then a \"straight\" line horizontally (it's not completely straight because it has relay between the satellites via LOS laser but each arc would be fairly long) and back down would be shorter than a line following the curvature. (Edited: This is wrong. I had conflated the lower latency with shorter distance as others have pointed out.) BTW, I want to give full credit to my source: [VIDEO] It also does a much better job of explaining it than I have but it is a convincing argument for how Starlink can be useful and why traders would be willing to pay a lot for it."},{"id":24291471,"author":"PopeDotNinja","month":"2020-08","story":"SpaceX Starlink average speedtest is 42.3Mbps down / 9.7 Mbps up","stance":"rec","why":"Explains light speed slower in fiber than vacuum","quote":"the whole video is worth watching","text":"Light travels 47% slower in a fiber optic cable than it does in the vacuum of space. [1] [1] [VIDEO] (the whole video is worth watching, but that timestamp answers your specific question."},{"id":21325111,"author":"bearcobra","month":"2019-10","story":"Elon Musk tweets using SpaceX’s Starlink satellite internet","stance":"rec","why":"Why Starlink matters for international trading firms","quote":"Real Engineering has an interesting video on why it would be useful for firms trading internationally","text":"Real Engineering has an interesting video on why it would be useful for firms trading internationally [VIDEO]"},{"id":20366785,"author":"Nican","month":"2019-07","story":"Amazon Seeks Permission to Launch 3k Internet Satellites","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of Starlink's purpose","quote":"I recommend watching. The channel does really good explanations.","text":"Real engineering made a video on it: [VIDEO] I recommend watching. The channel does really good explanations."},{"id":24905589,"author":"coolandsmartrr","month":"2020-10","story":"Invites sent for Starlink beta","stance":"reference","why":"Speculates Starlink latency beats fibre, valuable to finance","quote":"This video[1] speculates that Starlink will achieve lower latency than fibre Internet.","text":"This video[1] speculates that Starlink will achieve lower latency than fibre Internet. Latency-sensitive industries like finance are very willing to pay a lot for improved latency. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24161821,"author":"hangonhn","month":"2020-08","story":"Beta users of Starlink get downloads of 11 to 60 Mbps","stance":"reference","why":"Starlink may beat fiber latency over long distances","quote":"it may be possible to achieve a lower latency using Star Link than over fiber if the points are far enough","text":"[VIDEO] According to this Engineering Explained video, it may be possible to achieve a lower latency using Star Link than over fiber if the points are far enough. People like high frequency traders might be willing to pay a ton for such a thing."},{"id":20504012,"author":"PopeDotNinja","month":"2019-07","story":"Google’s Equiano Cable Will Extend to the Remote Island of Saint Helena","stance":"reference","why":"Argues satellites can be faster than cables in some cases","quote":"satellites can be faster than cables in certain cases","text":"Someone smarter than I made this, which you may or may not agree with... \"Why SpaceX is Making Starlink\" [VIDEO] TL;DR -- satellites can be faster than cables in certain cases"}],"r":[["6RhtiPefVzM",1],["66YRCjkxIcg",1],["7gtog_gOaGQ",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1]]},"6VOQnK7O2To":{"wy":"HN recommends it in startup video lists for its stories and Sacca's candid background.","q":{"x":"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis","i":7665375,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7665375,"author":"i4i","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: What are your favorite videos relevant to entrepreneurs or startups? ","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full of great stories from Sacca interviewed by Calacanis.","quote":"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis","text":"My favorite, because it's so full of great stories, is the Chris Sacca interview with Jason Calacanis on This Week In Startups. Pt.1 [VIDEO] Pt.2 [LINK]"},{"id":14204173,"author":"dmitri1981","month":"2017-04","story":"Hanging up my spurs","stance":"rec","why":"Sacca's little-known background, losing millions at university and recovering.","quote":"The part about him losing millions while still at university is as shocking as his recovery from the setback is amazing.","text":"Chris Sacca's background story is little known and he talks about it at length with Jason Calacanis. The part about him losing millions while still at university is as shocking as his recovery from the setback is amazing. Check out [VIDEO]"},{"id":9270764,"author":"knappador","month":"2015-03","story":"In Silicon Valley, stories about failure are a form of social currency","stance":"rec","why":"Sacca's interesting personal story of risk and recovery.","quote":"Chris Sacca has a pretty damned interesting tale","text":"Yeah, I recently heard someone talking about taking huge personal financial risk to keep a startup going and suggested maybe it's time to live and learn and start over with a hell of a lot better product-market fit or spend that money on Treehouse and an apartment for a few months to learn how to build something, which could just be their first successful product. There are too many people willing to seed a decent product to go all-in with anything except time and soul. Chris Sacca has a pretty damned interesting tale: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4802123,"author":"jonathanjaeger","month":"2012-11","story":"Ask HN: How did Treehouse get 5M in funding without wanting an \"Exit\"?","stance":"reference","why":"Sacca discusses investing in Kickstarter whose founders refuse to sell.","quote":"Chris Sacca talks about his investment in Kickstarter and how the founders don't want to sell the company","text":"Chris Sacca talks about his investment in Kickstarter and how the founders don't want to sell the company: [VIDEO] You have to get your investors on board even if it's not the \"typical\" setup."}],"r":[["SK4ezQrTqFw",1]]},"KgzQuE1pR1w":{"wy":"Offered as a pick-me-up and calming perspective when people feel lost or stressed.","q":{"x":"Whenever I find myself feeling like you do right now, I watch this video from Bill Hicks","i":30232894,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30232894,"author":"dudeinjapan","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: How do you deal with getting old and feeling lost?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bill Hicks monologue as a pick-me-up when feeling lost","quote":"Whenever I find myself feeling like you do right now, I watch this video from Bill Hicks","text":"Whenever I find myself feeling like you do right now, I watch this video from Bill Hicks: \"It's just a ride\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":45536407,"author":"0xDEAFBEAD","month":"2025-10","story":"Python 3.14 is here. How fast is it?","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as calming advice to take a deep breath","quote":"Take a deep breath and watch this video","text":">evolved Yes, it evolved. It wasn't designed. >Version numbers? Without version numbers, it has to be backwards-compatible, making it difficult to remove cruft. What would programming be like if all the code you wrote needed to work as IBM mainframe assembly? Tau is a good case study. Everyone seems to agree tau is better than pi. How much adoption has it seen? Is this what \"heavy optimization\" looks like? It took hundreds of years for Arabic numerals to replace Roman numerals in Europe. A medieval mathematician could have truthfully said: \"We've been using Roman numerals for hundreds of years; they work fine.\" That would've been stockholm syndrome. I get the same sense from your comment. Take a deep breath and watch this video: [VIDEO] >You're being facetious, right? I'm being provocative. Not facetious. \"Strong opinions, weakly held.\""},{"id":11477840,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-04","story":"The rituals we perform and the games we play","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"One last thing...","text":"You're welcome. :-) Thank you for your answer, I think I understand your reasoning a little better. With the example of the mathematician, you can read it a number of different ways, but one of the things I took from it was the difference between a healthy curiosity and an unhealthy curiosity. It sounds to me that you have a healthy curiosity, and enjoy the mental stimulation of exploring the big questions. As long as it remains something you enjoy and you're still open to enjoying other things then you can't really go wrong. If you'd like to find kindred spirits that also enjoy thinking about the big questions, you can find plenty in the world of philosophy. I can recommend The School of Life as an enjoyable way to explore philosophy. As some examples, here are a couple of my favourites from TSoL: [LINK] [LINK] Learning can be done for your own enjoyment, but what I would suggest is the mindset that many of us used to get us through school isn't likely to help with that. I think this video puts it well: [LINK] Related to this, here's a discussion about deschooling you may find interesting: [LINK] One last thing... [VIDEO] Best of luck!"}],"r":[["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1]]},"O61WihMRdjM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the clearest hands-on explanation of how Toyota hybrids work.","q":{"x":"I understood after I watched the brilliant Weber Auto video on it, published 5 years ago[0].","i":48208015,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48208015,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2026-05","story":"Nobody understands the point of hybrid cars [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made the point of hybrids click","quote":"I understood after I watched the brilliant Weber Auto video on it, published 5 years ago[0].","text":"I understood after I watched the brilliant Weber Auto video on it, published 5 years ago[0]. I liked it so much I \"gently\" encouraged my partner to get a Yaris hybrid, which gets about 69mpg average. It's not unusual to see 80+ on individual trips. She was a little sceptical about having an auto at first, because us Europeans still think it's 1980 and that manuals are superior, but she came around after a few minutes of driving. To be fair, though, classic \"slushbox\" autos actually were/are garbage, as are chain actuated CVTs. The hybrid system gets you immediate acceleration like an electric, none of this awful lag you get with a slushbox. It's hilarious to me that 34mpg is considered remarkably good, though. I know it's a \"minivan\" but my Golf GTI gets 42mpg and is actually fun and arguably cool. I can also fit 5 people, furniture, and 2.4m lengths of timber in it comfortably. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46283329,"author":"hedgehog","month":"2025-12","story":"Ford kills the All-Electric F-150","stance":"rec","why":"Educational walkthrough of planetary gear hybrid drivetrains","quote":"long educational video from Weber State University that gives a good walkthrough","text":"The planetary gear \"eCVT\" systems that Toyota and Ford use in many models are mechanically a lot simpler than a traditional automatic or sequential manual transmission. Few moving parts and no clutches at all. I don't know what the long term reliability of those drivetrains is is but I wouldn't be surprised if it's measurably measurably better than a traditional transmission + engine. There's a long educational video from Weber State University that gives a good walkthrough of what's going on in those things. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36623081,"author":"somerandomqaguy","month":"2023-07","story":"Toyota Is Right: We Need More Hybrid Cars and Fewer EVs. Here’s Why","stance":"rec","why":"Extensive hands-on explanation of Toyota hybrid system","quote":"Weber Auto giving an extensive hands on explanation.","text":"Toyota's eCVT? Not at all. The gas engine can drive the front wheels directly. [LINK] Conceptual explanation of the system. [VIDEO] Weber Auto giving an extensive hands on explanation."},{"id":45803923,"author":"Merad","month":"2025-11","story":"Tiny electric motor can produce more than 1,000 horsepower","stance":"reference","why":"Shows RAV4 hybrid motor","quote":"You can see the main hybrid motor of a RAV4 at [0].","text":"> Toyota-style hybrid drives could be a lot lighter The hybrid electric motor in a Toyota is already pretty comparable in weight to the motor in TFA, but obviously much less powerful. You can see the main hybrid motor of a RAV4 at [0]. If memory serves both the Camry and RAV4 hybrid models are only 2-300 lbs heavier than their gas counterparts. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42294682,"author":"CRConrad","month":"2024-12","story":"First Geared CVT [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Long explanation of Toyota eCVT","quote":"Long, 39 min!","text":"A quick Web search seems to indicate that's either a) not actually a CVT at all, or at best b) a planetary gear used as a power-splitting device to connect electric and ICE power sources to a conventional CVT. So this compares rather favourably, by actually being a geared CVT, which Toyota's \"eCVT\" apparently isn't at all. Sources: [LINK] (From: [LINK] ) [VIDEO] (Long, 39 min!) Ooh, aah, now I get it (I think): Yes, the output RPM of the \"eCVT\" is indeed continuously variable. But that's just how all planetary gearboxes with two power inputs (as here, ICE and e-motor) work: The output RPM is a function of both the input RPMs, a (weighted) average of them if you will. So you can vary the output RPM while holding one of the input RPMs constant, simply by varying the other input RPM. Here Toyota presumably wants to keep the ICE working at the most economical RPM, while allowing the vehicle to move at varying speeds, which they achieve by varying the RPM of the electric motor. (In practice, Toyota uses two electric motors, but AFAICS the principle also applies to just one. TBH I'm not sure why they need two, but then I'm only halfway through that long video.) So yeah, the \"eCVT\" is kind of \"a CVT\", but only if you have two power sources simultaneously. It can't apply a continuously varying gear ratio from just one power input to a power output, which is what is usually meant by \"power transmission\". TL;DR: This can vary the gear ratio from a single power source to your wheels; To"},{"id":39762445,"author":"brendoelfrendo","month":"2024-03","story":"Dual-Clutch Transmission: Wet or Dry?","stance":"reference","why":"How Toyota eCVT works with planetary gearsets","quote":"uses the electric motors with planetary gearsets to keep the engine operating as close to its optimal RPM as possible","text":"The \"eCVT\" in the Toyota hybrid system isn't a CVT at all, but rather a system that uses the electric motors with planetary gearsets to keep the engine operating as close to its optimal RPM as possible: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38469263,"author":"hedgehog","month":"2023-11","story":"Who makes the most reliable new cars?","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown showing hybrid unit's simple internals","quote":"Find a teardown of the hybrid unit and compare to a teardown of any automatic","text":"Most of the images there are broken, all I see are some conceptual block diagram type things. Find a teardown of the hybrid unit and compare to a teardown of any automatic, look at the actual internal complexity and moving parts. I was surprised. Here's a video of the P710: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["gSKTfG1GXYQ",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1],["pkCLMl0e_0k",1],["meHYBhcpdvQ",1]]},"cdiD-9MMpb0":{"wy":"Cited for Karpathy's first-hand explanations of camera-only self-driving, transformers and ML trends.","q":{"x":"Anyone seriously interested in this question should listen to the Lex Friedman interview of Andrej Karpathy","i":34453756,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34453756,"author":"tdalaa","month":"2023-01","story":"Will Tesla Ever “Solve” Full Self Driving? (2022)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Essential listening on self-driving","quote":"Anyone seriously interested in this question should listen to the Lex Friedman interview of Andrej Karpathy","text":"Anyone seriously interested in this question should listen to the Lex Friedman interview of Andrej Karpathy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34089074,"author":"meken","month":"2022-12","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Explanation of transformer layers as message passing","quote":"this was the takeaway I got from this explanation (starts at 36:21)","text":"My understanding is the transformer layer in the LLM is basically doing something akin to message passing, it’s like a mini computer. In predicting the next word, it has to understand a lot about a lot of different kinds of topics My understanding is kinda fuzzy because I haven’t coded it up myself, but this was the takeaway I got from this explanation (starts at 36:21) [VIDEO]"},{"id":33457273,"author":"jcims","month":"2022-11","story":"Study urges caution when comparing neural networks to the brain","stance":"rec","why":"Karpathy expands on comparing neural nets to brain, finding physics 0-days","quote":"Great listen overall IMHO","text":"Andrej Karpathy was recently on Lex Fridman's podcast and covered this to some extent. He has the same perspective on this topic and expanded on it quite a bit. Great listen overall IMHO - [VIDEO] I like his idea of finding 0-days in physics. :)"},{"id":44625444,"author":"kamranjon","month":"2025-07","story":"How Tesla is proving doubters right on why its robotaxi service cannot scale","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy discussing camera versus lidar trade-offs","quote":"Here is a link to Karpathy discussing the trade-offs","text":"The part that I always found difficult to square was not that Elon Musk towed this “no need for LiDAR” line so hard, but that Andrej Karpathy, who I generally consider a very reliable voice in this space, was also in strong agreement that cameras were all that was needed. Does anyone know if he still believes cameras is all you need? Edit: Here is a link to Karpathy discussing the trade-offs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43017193,"author":"nomel","month":"2025-02","story":"Tesla Cybertruck Drives Itself into a Pole, Owner Says 'Thank You Tesla'","stance":"reference","why":"Discussion of Tesla AI data collection goals","quote":"There's some talk about it in the Lex Friedman podcast(s)","text":"This, obviously, is one of the ongoing efforts and goals of Tesla AI, and the reason they collect so much data. There's some talk about it in the Lex Friedman podcast(s)[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37255843,"author":"yreg","month":"2023-08","story":"Camera Crushes Lidar, Claims Startup","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy explains the camera-only reasoning","quote":"Karpathy explains the reasoning on Lex's podcast","text":"Karpathy explains the reasoning on Lex's podcast[1]. This was after he left Tesla, but of course he's hardly impartial. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34981189,"author":"Tepix","month":"2023-03","story":"Microsoft Kosmos-1: A Multimodal Large Language Model","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy discusses OpenAI's World of Bits project","quote":"Karpathy talks about the OpenAI project","text":"Yeah, check out the Lex Friedman podcast episode #333 around minute 52 where Andrew Karpathy talks about the OpenAI project \"World of Bits\" that did this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35196944,"author":"1970-01-01","month":"2023-03","story":"GPT-4 System Card [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy on humor as an AI benchmark","quote":"Humor was one of the tests Karpathy would use as a benchmark for AI","text":">your boyfriend’s only in a wheelchair because he doesn’t want to kneel five times a day Within the roast context, this is a good line. Humor was one of the tests Karpathy would use as a benchmark for AI: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33650406,"author":"nverno","month":"2022-11","story":"Twitter to employees: all office buildings closed, badge access suspended","stance":"reference","why":"Engineer's account of working with Elon","quote":"Andrej Karpathy talked a bit about working with Elon recently","text":"If you want to get an idea, why not listen to some engineers that have worked with him? There are plenty of those around, for example, Andrej Karpathy talked a bit about working with Elon recently - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33729520,"author":"nuccy","month":"2022-11","story":"Stable Diffusion 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy discusses ML following physics' path to big collaborations","quote":"it seems machine learning takes the same path as physics already did.","text":"As pointed out in [1], it seems machine learning takes the same path as physics already did. In the mid-20th century there was a \"break\" in physics, before individuals were making ground breaking discoveries in their private/personal labs (think Newton, Maxwell, Curie, Roentgen, Planck, Einstein, and many others) later huge collaborations (LHC/CERN, Icecube, EHT, et al.) are required, since the machinery, simulations, models are so complex, that groups of people are needed to create, comprehend and use them. 1. [VIDEO] Lex Fridman podcast with Andrej Karpathy P.S. To counteract that (unintentionally actually, likely because of a simple optimization of instruments' duty cycle) in astronomy people come up with a concept of \"observatory\" (Like Hubble, JWST) instead of \"experiment\" (like LHC, HESS telescopes) where outside people can submit their proposals, and if selected get observational time. Along with raw data authors of the proposals get required expertise from the collaboration to process and analyze that data."},{"id":33388476,"author":"jstx1","month":"2022-10","story":"Andrej Karpathy: Tesla AI, Self-Driving, Optimus, Aliens, and AGI","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy on scope for a better search engine","quote":"There's absolutely scope to build a better search engine based on these tools.","text":"At 1:03:30 - [VIDEO] > I think there's definitely scope in building a better search engine today. Google have all the tools, all the people, (...) all the data, it's just not obvious that they're capable as an organization to innovate on their search engine right now, and if they don't, someone else will. There's absolutely scope to build a better search engine based on these tools."}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["Ip3X9LOh2dk",1]]},"q0RX59G2jaA":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it for the movers' rigor and discipline, which they find oddly satisfying to watch.","q":{"x":"Seeing their rigour and discipline is like a visual ASMR video.","i":27313323,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43962733,"author":"nervousvarun","month":"2025-05","story":"A Typical Workday at a Japanese Hardware Tool Store [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Movers far better than American ones; part of worthwhile series.","quote":"Honestly if you like any of these you'll likely find they're all worth watching.","text":"Longtime Paolo fan, he does a great job with his \"typical workday\" series. Not sure of the exact number he's done, but I personally estimate I've watched at least 20-25 of these across a wide range of industries (majority are in food preparation/service but he also covers construction, tech, animation, airlines, education, politics, and many more) and if there's one overriding takeaway it's the Japanese as a culture are extremely committed to maximizing product quality. It could be selection bias (maybe he only films exemplary people) but it doesn't seem to matter if you're dealing with someone preparing food, fighting fires, or delivering packages these people seem to really care about the overall quality of the service or product they provide. A few others worth watching: [VIDEO] (movers...as someone who only has experience with American movers this is ridiculously better than what I experienced) [LINK] (package delivery worker) [LINK] (politician) [LINK] (fire fighter) [LINK] (anime director) [LINK] (car repair shop worker) Honestly if you like any of these you'll likely find they're all worth watching."},{"id":27313323,"author":"AndrewOMartin","month":"2021-05","story":"Americans are on the move, but their stuff doesn’t always follow","stance":"rec","why":"Rigorous, disciplined Japanese movers, like visual ASMR.","quote":"Seeing their rigour and discipline is like a visual ASMR video.","text":"Maybe if you used the Tokyo based Art Moving Center also known as Art Hikkoshi Center ( アート引越センター ) [VIDEO] Seeing their rigour and discipline is like a visual ASMR video."},{"id":22992262,"author":"bane","month":"2020-04","story":"An average family in Tokyo can own a new house for $850/month","stance":"rec","why":"Young couple's moving company experience in Tokyo.","quote":"","text":"Decent family houses in Tokyo are definitely more expensive than this, but not hugely (also Kyoto surprisingly). There's some interesting things about the Japanese housing market, for example, many homes become \"worthless\" after the end of the mortgage -- so they depreciate and are more like buying cars. Tokyo is also an absolutely enormous city with even more massive metro area. There's very few examples on the planet that come close. Tokyo is sometimes called \"the infinite city\" for good reason. This means that yes, you can pay huge money for a coffin in some areas, and little money for an entire house elsewhere in the same city. New York is kind of the same way in that respect, but is actually still smaller and less diverse. I could write a ton more, but I think this series from \"Life Where I'm From\" which is pointed out in the article is a fantastic overview of housing for a nuclear family in Tokyo. How an Average Family in Tokyo Can Buy a New Home - [LINK] Brand New Japanese House Tour - [LINK] Are Japanese Homes Really Worthless After 30 Years? - [LINK] Old Japanese Apartment vs. New House - [LINK] What a Very Old Japanese House is Like - [LINK] Living in Traditional Japanese Townhouses: Kyo-machiya - [LINK] Checking Out New Japanese House Design and Technology - [LINK] I'd also point out this young couple who are expecting their first child (Paolo from Tokyo) What a Japanese Moving Company in Tokyo is like - [VIDEO] $1500 Japanese Apartment Tour in Tokyo Shibuya - [LIN"}],"r":[["ouAG4vXFORc",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["S4gU2Tsv6hY",1],["iGbC5j4pG9w",1],["ZXJWO2FQ16c",1],["9KABcmczPdg",1],["03p2CADwGF8",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["s_fkpZSnz2I",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["yReDbeY7ZMU",1],["dpw9EHDh2bM",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1]]},"rrkrvAUbU9Y":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever money as an incentive comes up, as evidence that rewards can backfire for creative white-collar work.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend taking a look at the work of Daniel Pink related to money as an incentive.","i":46834602,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46834602,"author":"Ey7NFZ3P0nzAe","month":"2026-01","story":"Antirender: remove the glossy shine on architectural renderings","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues money is an ineffective incentive for creative work","quote":"I highly recommend taking a look at the work of Daniel Pink related to money as an incentive.","text":"> The idea that money is not an effective incentive to drive behavior is wishful thinking It is obviously an incentive. But I think it's not an effective one and has many morally bad side effects. I highly recommend taking a look at the work of Daniel Pink related to money as an incentive. See The Puzzle Of Motivation (~20min) [VIDEO]"},{"id":7928303,"author":"arkitaip","month":"2014-06","story":"Employees That Stay In Companies Longer Get Paid Less","stance":"rec","why":"Motivators in business beyond pay","quote":"It might inspire you to focus on other incentives than just pay salary increases.","text":"Have you seen any of Daniel Pink's work on what motivates people in business? It might inspire you to focus on other incentives than just pay salary increases. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1813668,"author":"hoop","month":"2010-10","story":"Does business school teach people how to assert power before they're ready?","stance":"rec","why":"Concise summary of Pink's Drive","quote":"There's also a Youtube/TED video that sums up","text":"I'll read them all over the next couple of hours. (I am sick in bed anyhow :)) Readings that I recommend are \"The Great Jackass Fallacy\" (I got my copy <$10 on Amazon) and \"Drive\" by Daniel Pink. There's also a Youtube/TED video that sums up \"Drive\" very well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46960956,"author":"ismailmaj","month":"2026-02","story":"Ask HN: What Are You Working On? (February 2026)","stance":"reference","why":"Incentives backfire for creative white collar work","quote":"incentives are often counterproductive or insulting for white collar workers","text":"How do you scale a more human management system when both the manager or the managee can be incompetent or malicious. I'm aware that incentives are often counterproductive or insulting for white collar workers where there are elements of creativity in the job, but I feel like to get it to work you need trust which IMO cannot be scaled to big companies like Google. FYI, I'm paraphrasing, perhaps badly \"The puzzle of motivation | Dan Pink | TED\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":36655280,"author":"gretch","month":"2023-07","story":"California needs real math education, not gimmicks","stance":"reference","why":"Data showing money hurts creative problem solving","quote":"shows that motivation via money hurts creative problem solving.","text":"This Ted talk (includes a lot of data) shows that motivation via money hurts creative problem solving. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29219321,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2021-11","story":"Ways to improve mental health for software developers","stance":"reference","why":"Pink mentions a company with 20% time","quote":"Atlassian; not Google.","text":"I remember a TED talk that Dan Pink gave (I think this might be it[0]), in which he mentioned a company that did \"20% time.\" Atlassian; not Google. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5001994,"author":"DeepDuh","month":"2013-01","story":"Why did a Train Carrying Biofuel Cross the Border 24 Times and Never Unload?","stance":"reference","why":"Traditional incentives are less effective for creative work","quote":"Traditional incentives are often not as effective as you think","text":"Traditional incentives are often not as effective as you think: [VIDEO] The best way to have motivated employees for creative tasks is to give them a high base salary, good working conditions and hire suiting ones in the first place. For repetitive jobs the incentives might work, but these are also the jobs where you should think about automation."},{"id":1209535,"author":"nreece","month":"2010-03","story":"Functional Fixedness - The Candle Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Pink uses the candle problem to illustrate motivation","quote":"The Candle Problem was used as an insightful example by Daniel Pink","text":"The Candle Problem was used as an insightful example by Daniel Pink in his TED talk on the surprising science of motivation - [VIDEO]"}]},"y-AXTx4PcKI":{"wy":"HN points to it as the one scene worth watching from the movie, a classic about sales and entrepreneurship.","q":{"x":"Just watch this classic clip from Glengarry Glen Ross","i":3413856,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3413856,"author":"richardburton","month":"2012-01","story":"How to date a supermodel (or get dealflow or find cofounders)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic clip on sales being about numbers.","quote":"Just watch this classic clip from Glengarry Glen Ross","text":"Sales. It is all about the numbers. Just watch this classic clip from Glengarry Glen Ross: [VIDEO] Pure. Gold."},{"id":3220513,"author":"ims","month":"2011-11","story":"Lesser Known Ways to Persuade People","stance":"rec","why":"Brilliant Always Be Closing scene giving a feel for the movie.","quote":"Brilliant (but NSFW, language) \"Always Be Closing\" scene to give you a feel for it","text":"If you liked this movie, may I suggest the David Mamet movie that inspired it: Glengarry Glen Ross ( [LINK] ). Brilliant (but NSFW, language) \"Always Be Closing\" scene to give you a feel for it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1208745,"author":"thomaspaine","month":"2010-03","story":"Must-See Movies for Entrepreneurs","stance":"rec","why":"The one scene worth watching from the movie.","quote":"Save yourself 90 minutes and just watch that scene","text":"That's really the only part of the movie worth watching to be honest. Save yourself 90 minutes and just watch that scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1208544,"author":"jorisvoorn","month":"2010-03","story":"Ask HN: How to sell?","stance":"reference","why":"Always Be Closing scene linked as sales motivation.","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Always Be Closing \"The hardest thing in life is sell\" [LINK] * * * The biggest step in my opinion is the psychological one. After that, everything comes in much easier than before."},{"id":480113,"author":"gojomo","month":"2009-02","story":"Y Combinator putting on special event called AngelConf for would-be investors","stance":"reference","why":"The Glengarry Glen Ross monologue scene identifying the memorable line.","quote":"It's a memorable line from a monologue delivered by the Alec Baldwin character in the movie 'Glengarry Glen Ross'.","text":"It's a memorable line from a monologue delivered by the Alec Baldwin character in the movie 'Glengarry Glen Ross'. [scene] [VIDEO] [transcript] [LINK] (Language is NSFW... unless of course your workplace is like the one in the movie.)"},{"id":2234228,"author":"ludicast","month":"2011-02","story":"Ask HN: Would you fire this developer?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Probably not the best \"formal warning\", but fun all the same.","text":"\"Nice guy I don't give a f &k. Good father, f &k you, go home and play with your kids. You want to work here close!\" [VIDEO] Probably not the best \"formal warning\", but fun all the same."}]},"AxJubaijQbI":{"wy":"HN points to it as a good, anecdote-filled introduction to the math of shuffling from the expert himself.","q":{"x":"It gives a lot of insight and anectdotes in addition to the central theorem","i":48590585,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48590585,"author":"fisian","month":"2026-06","story":"Seven Perfect Shuffles Randomize a Deck of Cards. But How Many Sloppy Ones?","stance":"rec","why":"Diaconis on seven perfect shuffles, with anecdotes beyond the theorem","quote":"It gives a lot of insight and anectdotes in addition to the central theorem","text":"Numberphile has a video with Persi Diaconis about the seven perfect shuffles. It gives a lot of insight and anectdotes in addition to the central theorem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17333823,"author":"evanb","month":"2018-06","story":"The Math of Card Shuffling","stance":"rec","why":"Numberphile with Persi Diaconis on shuffling, with good extra footage","quote":"There's a great numberphile with Persi Diaconis all about shuffling","text":"There's a great numberphile with Persi Diaconis all about shuffling: [VIDEO] The extra footage is fantastic, too."},{"id":9915498,"author":"shuriu","month":"2015-07","story":"The Coin Flip: A Fundamentally Unfair Proposition? (2009)","stance":"rec","why":"Persi Diaconis on the research behind shuffling randomness","quote":"He also has done research on the randomness of shuffling cards","text":"This video from Numberphile goes in depth about this: [LINK] . The guy talking, is Persi Diaconis, is the main author of the paper this article is based upon. He also has done research on the randomness of shuffling cards: [VIDEO] . Pretty cool stuff!"},{"id":26019473,"author":"trcollinson","month":"2021-02","story":"The mathematics of shuffling","stance":"reference","why":"Persi Diaconis appearance on Numberphile","quote":"Persi on Numberphile","text":"One of my absolute favorite mathematicians is Persi Diaconis who is mentioned in this paper and was the original basis for this area of research. Well worth learning about. He was a runaway. Then a magician, trained by Dai Vernon and worked along side people like the late Ricky Jay and Richard Turner. He went on to get PhD from Harvard and got a MacArthur Fellowship. He is a legend. Ricky Jay talking about his childhood friend Persi: [LINK] Persi on Numberphile: [VIDEO] A great lecture: [LINK]"}],"r":[["msN87y-iEx0",1],["p5_tpq5ejFQ",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1]]},"NfizT369g60":{"wy":"HN points people to it as a rare, concrete look at how a AAA studio uses procedural tools to fill huge worlds with natural-looking environments.","q":{"x":"You may enjoy this talk, the devs for Far Cry 5 procedurally generated quite a lot of their world","i":21307995,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":30212678,"author":"boarnoah","month":"2022-02","story":"GTA 6 Confirmed","stance":"rec","why":"How Ubisoft leverages tools to build large worlds.","quote":"There is an interesting talk from Ubisoft which talks about how they leverage tools for building their large worlds","text":"It has its applications in a different way, tool assisted level design. Software suites like Houdini, which is something of an industry standard in VFX and gamedev can potentially leverage ML techniques. AFAIK the folks who make Houdini have talked in the past about using ML to improve their terrain heightmap modelling, improving the quality of the erosion etc... The real value add with using procedural tools to automate creating parts of large worlds (eroded terrain, placement of foliage according to rules) is that it frees up artist time to focus on hand crafting individual locations and detail into the map. There is an interesting talk from Ubisoft which talks about how they leverage tools for building their large worlds [VIDEO] (slides: [LINK] )"},{"id":21307995,"author":"fenwick67","month":"2019-10","story":"The Grove 8 – Growing Trees in Blender","stance":"rec","why":"How Far Cry 5 procedurally placed vegetation by terrain rules.","quote":"You may enjoy this talk, the devs for Far Cry 5 procedurally generated quite a lot of their world","text":"You may enjoy this talk, the devs for Far Cry 5 procedurally generated quite a lot of their world, placing plants based on the ground slope, sun, whether they were under power lines, elevation etc. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21369017,"author":"rayalez","month":"2019-10","story":"One Page Dungeon Generator","stance":"rec","why":"Far Cry procedural world generation talk.","quote":"awesome talks about modern procedural generation techniques","text":"Looks amazing! Note that the author also has a fantasy city generator: [LINK] And mansion generator: [LINK] If you're into that kind of stuff, there's a world map generator(by different author): [LINK] and this amazing tutorial on generating maps: [LINK] ---- Also, kinda relevant - awesome talks about modern procedural generation techniques(with AI, Houdini, etc): How AI will change 3D industry [LINK] Procedural world generation for Far Cry [VIDEO] Procedurally generating Manhattan for Spider Man [LINK] Mindblowing talks by Anastasia Opara (using cool math, deep learning, and a lot of creativity to create crazy cool stuff) [LINK] She is the author of the most impressive procedural generation project I've ever seen: [LINK] ---- Also, I myself have made a little tutorial about procedurally generating a 3D landscape in Houdini: [LINK]"},{"id":20150963,"author":"rayalez","month":"2019-06","story":"Procedural content generation: creating a universe (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Procedural world generation for Far Cry 5.","quote":"awesome talks about modern procedural generation techniques","text":"Somewhat related(okay, not really, but still fun) - awesome talks about modern procedural generation techniques(with AI, Houdini, etc): How AI will change 3D industry [LINK] Procedural world generation for Far Cry [VIDEO] Procedurally generating Manhattan for Spider Man [LINK] Mindblowing talks by Anastasia Opara (using cool math, deep learning, and a lot of creativity to create crazy cool stuff) [LINK] Procedurally generating 3D landscape in Houdini (a little tutorial I have made, can't resist sharing) [LINK]"}],"r":[["X2wLP0izeJE",1],["sBzRwzY7G-k",1],["4aw9uyj9MAE",1],["RxabLA7UQ9k",1],["uDqjIdI4bF4",1]]},"O45LaFsaqMA":{"wy":"Recommended as a thought-provoking lecture from a Fields medalist on the foundations of mathematics and formal proof verification.","q":{"x":"I recommend this video","i":20572779,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20572779,"author":"ukj","month":"2019-07","story":"A Programmer’s Regret: Neglecting Math at University","stance":"rec","why":"Explains univalent foundations and shift toward structuralism","quote":"I recommend this video","text":"Where Hilbert erred was looking for foundations in first-order logic. Univalent foundations are laid on high-order logic. It's the distinction between top-down and bottom-up thinking. If you are into philosophy/epistemology - it's somewhat of an attempt to shift Mathematics from foundationalism towards structuralism. I recommend this video: [VIDEO] Hilbert's dream of Metamathematics has sort of come true in the form of homoiconicity in programming languages."},{"id":18391325,"author":"IngoBlechschmid","month":"2018-11","story":"Mystery Math Whiz and Novelist Advance Permutation Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Voevodsky's vision for computer-checked proofs","quote":"You can learn more about Voevodsky's vision in slides of him and in a recorded lecture","text":"> And indeed, this will likely always be the case, as the true purpose of proving a mathematical result is almost never to actually get the boolean true/false output, but to illustrate how to solve the problem in the first place and what methods were helpful for that. As a working mathematician, I wholeheartedly agree with the second part of the quoted sentence! But I think that the first part isn't quite right: The function of proofs to determine truth is extremely important, especially considering that, especially in pure mathematics, we often can't check our results in any other manner (like conducting physical experiments or checking many cases with the computer). Bad experiences with proofs which later turned out to be faulty led Voevodsky to initiate his univalent foundations program (also called homotopy type theory ), where all results should be checked by the computer. (For the particular area he was working on, no existing theorem prover turned out to be sufficient. He made a breakthrough in finding a new foundation to base theorem provers on, which made it possible to formalize results in his area of mathematics not only in principle, but also in practice.) The hope is also that with computer-checked proofs, we can advance to new areas of mathematics which are currently inprenetable to our puny human minds. I'm very much looking forward to this future! You can learn more about Voevodsky's vision in slides of him and in a recorded lecture: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15600504,"author":"Ericson2314","month":"2017-11","story":"How a destructive idea paved the way for modern math","stance":"rec","why":"Voevodsky's talk on possible inconsistency of mathematical foundations","quote":"by the late great Voedvodvosky is more interesting.","text":"Yes we must take saxioms on faith alone. Embrace pluralism and move on. [VIDEO] by the late great Voedvodvosky is more interesting."}],"r":[["1wAaI_6b9JE",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1]]},"gvZSpET11ZY":{"wy":"HN links it in personal finance threads as an entertaining explanation of how fees erode retirement savings.","q":{"x":"John Oliver had a great bit on retirement plans [1] if you're interested in passive investing.","i":29057681,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30137336,"author":"giorgioz","month":"2022-01","story":"What I learned from a year of watching bad financial advice on TikTok","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how pension fund fees eat into retirement savings.","quote":"Watch this video from Last Week Tonight with John Oliver that explains the last concept very well","text":"ETF aren't rocket science. They are not super easy either, maybe 2-4 weeks of research. It's quite probably they will literally DOUBLE your savings when you will retired. And you will be able to retire when you say so not when the governments tells you that you can. There are 2 types of pensions funds essentially. 1) The ones that do not save money. You pay pensions taxes now that just go to pay the people retired today. Believe it or not the vast majority of governments pension funds are like this. In Italy it's like this. You IRPEF money doesn't go in an investment, it pays the retirees of today. Now just wait and see what we'll happen with life expectancy increasing and population reduction. 2) The ones that buy things for you. And guess what they buy: usually 3 bonds and 2 ETFs. And they will take 1-2% for that. And you might think: \"oh but 1-2% I can give awayt if I do not have to care about anything\". In practice though ETFs return 5-8% per year when kept for decades and the earnings are averaged. So the pension fund takes 1-2% from 5-8% which is 20-30%. On top of that there is compounding interest. When you will have retired it's likely you will have given away half of your savings to the company managing the pension fund/retiring plan. Watch this video from Last Week Tonight with John Oliver that explains the last concept very well: [VIDEO] AS USUAL, DO NOT JUST TRUST ME, RESEARCH WHAT I SAID (AND NOT ON TIK TOK)"},{"id":29057681,"author":"alecst","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: How to start learning about investments?","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver on retirement plans and passive investing.","quote":"John Oliver had a great bit on retirement plans [1] if you're interested in passive investing.","text":"John Oliver had a great bit on retirement plans [1] if you're interested in passive investing. I've pretty much only followed this advice since 2016, with minor changes accounting for my personal situation. My Vanguard portfolio has tracked the market, which means my annualized rate of return is about 12%, at an expense ratio of .1% (how much I pay in fees of that original 12%.) Of course that won't last forever, but it's a good balance of risk and return. A bank account usually pays around .25-1% a year. Active investors, on average, do as well as passive investors, but incur greater costs. [2] I only \"actively invest\" for fun, and as a way of connecting with my friends and family who also enjoy the stock market casino. [1] [VIDEO] [2] Warren Buffett's side of [LINK]"},{"id":12026482,"author":"whamlastxmas","month":"2016-07","story":"Ask HN: How do you manage you money?","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver segment on retirement plans and fees.","quote":"You might like this","text":"You might like this: [VIDEO] My personal method is to have a standard of living high enough that I don't have to really think about it much. A small amount of discomfort, but not much. I'm a naturally frugal person so it's not so bad. I have a single credit card, a single debit card, and various Vanguard accounts (IRA, Roth IRA, Brokerage). I keep a few months of cash liquid in the bank account, whenever it gets a couple thousand over that I transfer it to my Vanguard accounts. Vanguard is very easy to manage. About an 80/20 mix between US and international, and an 80/20 mix between stock index and bond index for both US and international. So only 4 index symbols to deal with and rebalance every 6 months or so. Increase the size of your bond holdings as you get older. I log into my bank's website to pay my balance every month, and to view my transactions for that month to make sure there's no fraud/stolen card. I look at my net worth in Mint every few months just out of curiosity. I used to use Mint for tracking budgets for stuff like food, but I found that my happiness went down drastically if I tried to artificially limit my spending because I feel like I'm already really frugal. This is enough for 95%+ of people assuming they're saving enough and making enough to save enough."},{"id":48135291,"author":"elevation","month":"2026-05","story":"New York, California pension leaders oppose 'extreme' SpaceX control structure","stance":"reference","why":"John Oliver episode on predatory 401k plans with high fees.","quote":"It was so bad that John Oliver made an episode[0] about it!","text":"> Social Security replaced with an index Yes please! I learned just how bad of a deal Social Security is when an employer (a bootstrapped startup) offered a predatory 401k plan. It was free for my employer to setup but the employees were stuck with extremely high fees. It was so bad that John Oliver made an episode[0] about it! Yet, even for the worst 401K plan in America, the projected retirement returns were 1600% of the Social Security returns. Even America's worst 401K would be better for the average consumer than the federal debacle Uncle Sam should sunset SSI and allow citizens to select from and move freely between a number of accredited funds. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31750519,"author":"papito","month":"2022-06","story":"Major crypto lender Celsius freezes withdrawals as markets tumble","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for distrust of fee-charging money managers.","quote":"","text":"That's why I have a problem with someone else managing my money (outside of direct fund managers, of course, who take a fixed percentage fee and are incentivized to have the fund grow, not to fleece me). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oFrTqQw0_3c",1]]},"lgivCGdmFrw":{"wy":"Used as the primary source for Apple's stated plan to make Swift its successor to C++ and for how interop supports gradual transition.","q":{"x":"Finaly a recent gem from CppNow 2023","i":37479190,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40651792,"author":"glhaynes","month":"2024-06","story":"Swift Static Linux SDK","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among relevant resources on Swift C++ interop","quote":"A few hopefully relevant resources, first a couple of videos","text":"I'm not a (direct) user of the C++ interop, but everything I've heard about it has been quite positive. A few hopefully relevant resources, first a couple of videos: - \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" (John McCall; CppNow 2023) [VIDEO] - \"Swift as C++ Successor in FoundationDB\" (Konrad Malawski; Strange Loop 2023) [LINK] And then a series of blog posts by Doug Gregor: - \"Swift for C++ Practitioners\" [LINK] And finally: - \"Mixing Swift and C++\" (Swift.org) [LINK]"},{"id":38742594,"author":"mannuch","month":"2023-12","story":"Memory safety is necessary, not sufficient","stance":"rec","why":"One of a couple great talks on Swift's C++ migration story","quote":"There’s a couple great talks on this by folks on the Swift team.","text":"I wonder what the author thinks about Swift’s new C++ interop story? Since the Swift compiler includes Clang, and can thus compile both your C++ and Swift into LLVM IR, without the need for an FFI later between the two, couldn’t this be the “Typescript for C++” that the author points out a space for? The Swift folks are very much thinking about Swift as a C++ successor that can be incrementally migrated to so I’m a bit surprised the author didn’t discuss it further — especially given Swift’s spiritual similarities to Rust. There’s a couple great talks on this by folks on the Swift team. John McCall at CppNow [VIDEO] Konrad Malawski at StrangeLoop 2023 [LINK]"},{"id":37479190,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-09","story":"Show HN: C-ocoa, Write iOS/macOS apps in any language, with a generated C API","stance":"rec","why":"Recent talk on Swift as a memory-safe successor to C++","quote":"Finaly a recent gem from CppNow 2023","text":"Java also compiles to native code on Android. Try to write an iOS application using only C and C++, instead of either Objective-C or Swift. \"Swift was designed from the outset to be safer than C-based languages, and eliminates entire classes of unsafe code\" -- [LINK] \"Swift is a successor to the C, C++, and Objective-C languages. It includes low-level primitives such as types, flow control, and operators. It also provides object-oriented features such as classes, protocols, and generics, giving Cocoa and Cocoa Touch developers the performance and power they demand.\" -- [LINK] Some of the information related their Safe C dialect, [LINK] \"2023 EuroLLVM - Keynote: “-fbounds-safety”: Enforcing bounds safety for production C code\" [LINK] Finaly a recent gem from CppNow 2023, \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37610172,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-09","story":"Interoperability: Swift’s Super Power","stance":"rec","why":"Apple's talk on Swift as successor to C++","quote":"watch Apple's talk at CppCon 2023","text":"On the same note, watch Apple's talk at CppCon 2023, \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":43609503,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-04","story":"Apple’s Darwin OS and XNU Kernel Deep Dive","stance":"reference","why":"Apple's own talk stating Swift is its successor language","quote":"obviously at Apple our sucessor language is Swift","text":"Yes, Apple themselves, apparently folks wanting Apple to use Rust don't read Apple's documentation or watch talks done by Apple compiler developers. > Swift was designed from the outset to be safer than C-based languages, and eliminates entire classes of unsafe code. -- [LINK] > Swift is a successor to the C, C++, and Objective-C languages. It includes low-level primitives such as types, flow control, and operators. It also provides object-oriented features such as classes, protocols, and generics. -- [LINK] \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO] \"So we feel pretty strongly, obviously at Apple our sucessor language is Swift and I am here to talk about features of Swift, both to try to sell you on to it, but also to talk about the things we think are pretty necessary and the ways in which a programming language can support, you know, clear code, safer and more correct code.\" \"Like I said before, Apple has always intended for Swift to be a sucessor language for all of our predecessors, from the top to the bottom of our stack, accessible to novices, powerful enough for experts, it is real a tall order. From [VIDEO] to [VIDEO] \"Swift as C++ Successor in FoundationDB\" [LINK]"},{"id":42137721,"author":"pornel","month":"2024-11","story":"Safe and efficient C++ interoperability via non-escapable types and lifetimes","stance":"reference","why":"Apple wants to migrate from C++ to a safe low-overhead Swift","quote":"Apple wants to migrate away from C++, including code in the kernel","text":"Apple wants to migrate away from C++, including code in the kernel, so they need a safe, low-overhead language. Swift has originally been designed as an ObjC replacement, mostly for UI glue code, with conveniences more like a scripting language. So now they're working on making Swift support more low-level low-overhead code. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41572398,"author":"myko","month":"2024-09","story":"Swift 6","stance":"reference","why":"Swift team member's talk on Swift replacing C++","quote":"John McCall (Swift team) gave a talk on Swift replacing C++ earlier this year","text":"John McCall (Swift team) gave a talk on Swift replacing C++ earlier this year: [VIDEO] Selfishly I wish mixed C targets were a priority before C++ but I get why this was more important to them"},{"id":40862112,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2024-07","story":"Ladybird Web Browser becomes a non-profit with $1M from GitHub Founder","stance":"reference","why":"Swift's C++ interop is designed for migrating C++ projects","quote":"it’s designed for exactly this [1]","text":"I wonder if they are thinking Swift - their C++ interop is still pretty new and has a way to mature, but it’s designed for exactly this [1] (we should expect Apple to start doing it with WebKit and other big C++ projects, even perhaps LLVM). 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38978587,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2024-01","story":"Apple hiring compiler developers for improving Swift / C++ interoperability","stance":"reference","why":"Presenter explains writing new C++ project features in Swift","quote":"the presenter at the C++ Now 2023 talk [1] goes into this","text":"They definitely have opened some things up, like a full C++ interface to Metal. I think the idea with this current effort is kind of the opposite though - not to encourage more use of C++, but to start writing new features of C++ projects in Swift (the presenter at the C++ Now 2023 talk [1] goes into this). I expect WebKit is a big target for them, I think they eventually want to replace all their C++ with Swift gradually (instead of trying to do ground-up rewrites, where you dedicate a team to doing that for years and you have a replacement at the end with fewer features than the old project). 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39087510,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2024-01","story":"The Case for Rust in the base system","stance":"reference","why":"Pictures where Apple is going with Swift","quote":"Apple's John McCall makes a picture of where they are going with Swift","text":"Apple's John McCall makes a picture of where they are going with Swift in \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":39087511,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2024-01","story":"The Case for Rust in the base system","stance":"reference","why":"Pictures where Apple is going with Swift","quote":"Apple's John McCall makes a picture of where they are going with Swift","text":"Apple's John McCall makes a picture of where they are going with Swift in \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":39091305,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2024-01","story":"The Case for Rust in the base system","stance":"reference","why":"Talk has no Rust references; about replacing C and C++ with Swift","quote":"There are zero references to Rust on that talk.","text":"There are zero references to Rust on that talk. It is all about C, C++, Objective-C and how Apple is replacing them with Swift. Rust isn't even shown on languages in use at Apple, [VIDEO] As for tracking down the exact minutes of each Swift reference, maybe I will try to find some time on my side to prove me right on the Internet."},{"id":38663554,"author":"nrr","month":"2023-12","story":"Swift was always going to be part of the OS","stance":"reference","why":"Source that Apple adopts Swift as successor to C and C++","quote":"Apple has explicitly set out to adopt Swift as a successor language to C, Objective-C, C++, and Objective-C++[0][1].","text":"The raisons d'être between the CLR (and C#) and Swift are entirely different. Apple has explicitly set out to adopt Swift as a successor language to C, Objective-C, C++, and Objective-C++[0][1]. This stands in stark contrast to Microsoft's vision for the CLR, which was… to be a better Java, more or less? (Does anyone actually know what the .NET initiative was all about? Microsoft went absolutely ham on it in their branding, but the momentum unceremoniously fizzled out before Server 2003 went RTM.) That said, apropos .NET fitting into Windows, the work on Singularity[2] by MSR had a lasting impact. I'm told that there is in use a System C# dialect that generates native object code via a more production grade version of Bartok[3]. 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK] . 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK]"},{"id":38789910,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2023-12","story":"Operation Triangulation: What you get when attack iPhones of researchers","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Apple's plan to incrementally replace C++ with Swift","quote":"they have recently come out with C++ interoperability [1]","text":"That is exactly their plan. Swift could always link into C applications, and they have recently come out with C++ interoperability [1] so things like WebKit etc. can start having parts re-written or new parts written from the start in Swift so they can gradually replace C and C++ codebases instead of trying to rewrite everything (which sucks because even for things much, much less complex than WebKit, you can have a team working for three years working on a replacement and it’ll have less features than the original had when you started). They’re even working on an embedded subset for embedded devices so things like microcontrollers like battery management, the Secure Enclave etc. can run it. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37825401,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-10","story":"Bare-metal Rust in Android","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder that Apple's successor language is Swift","quote":"Apparently people keep forgetting Swift exists.","text":"Apparently people keep forgetting Swift exists. \"CppNow 2023:Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37952722,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-10","story":"Apple's use of Swift and SwiftUI in iOS 17","stance":"reference","why":"Apple talks on Swift as a C++ successor","quote":"some talks from Apple folks","text":"On the same subject, some talks from Apple folks, \"Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO] \"Swift as C++ Successor in FoundationDB\" [LINK]"},{"id":37953867,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2023-10","story":"Apple's use of Swift and SwiftUI in iOS 17","stance":"reference","why":"Apple intends Swift for nearly all first-party code, with subsets for low levels","quote":"the presenter from Apple says that they intend to eventually use Swift for basically all their first-party code","text":"In the recent talk about the new Swift to C++ bidirectional bridge [1] the presenter from Apple says that they intend to eventually use Swift for basically all their first-party code from embedded code on microcontrollers, to the kernel, libraries and apps. There will be language subsets for embedded and kernel development, not all features would be able to be used at the lower levels. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37477613,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2023-09","story":"Show HN: C-ocoa, Write iOS/macOS apps in any language, with a generated C API","stance":"reference","why":"Apple talk noting C bindings are mostly C++ with extern C","quote":"Apple would rather write less of them than more of.","text":"As mentioned on the Swift talk at CppNow 2023, most of the C bindings are actually C++ with extern \"C\", and Apple would rather write less of them than more of. \"CppNow 2023:Introducing a Memory-Safe Successor Language in Large C++ Code Bases\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":37610521,"author":"pohl","month":"2023-09","story":"Interoperability: Swift’s Super Power","stance":"reference","why":"Supports point that interop enables gradual transition to Swift","quote":"One can imagine a Swift LLVM/Clang now.","text":"Don’t forget that this interoperability was put in place to support a specific engineering goal: to make it possible to slowly (over decades, probably) transition from 4 different languages (C, ObjC, C++, ObjC++) at every level of Apple’s stack towards a single successor language. Would you recommend they wait for some weird Java-esque 100% Pure implementation within the compiler itself before they can begin that work? The ideal you’re suggesting is actually made more possible by this work. One can imagine a Swift LLVM/Clang now. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37653808,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2023-09","story":"PostScript’s Sudden Death in Sonoma","stance":"reference","why":"Details Apple's transition layers from C, ObjC and C++ to Swift","quote":"See this talk for the details on that","text":"Apple have chosen to go all in on Swift for their first-party code, from the kernel to the libraries to the apps. Even things running on microcontrollers (but there’s no official or standard embedded Swift subset yet). It’s quite interesting actually that they’ve very intentionally focused on building transition layers so they can start gradually integrating Swift into C, Objective-C, and recently new support for C++ and Objective-C++. See this talk for the details on that - [VIDEO] So don’t expect to see any Rust code from Apple, although of course they might ship third party things written in it at some point."}],"r":[["ZQc9-seU-5k",2],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"mHtdZgou0qU":{"wy":"Recommended as a classic JavaScript performance talk whose fundamentals about reflow and efficient code still apply.","q":{"x":"the fundamental ideas are still the same.","i":44654180,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44654180,"author":"RumourRider","month":"2025-07","story":"Don't animate height","stance":"rec","why":"Old talk with still-valid fundamentals on reflow and performance.","quote":"the fundamental ideas are still the same.","text":"You need to think about what the browser is actually doing when it displays a web page. You need to think about what happens when you type the URL in and what the browser is doing conceptually. If you have any experience programming 2D graphics with SDL this will help you to understand what is happening as well. There is an old school talk by Nikolas Zakas on JavaScript Performance from 2009 which while some of it is out of date now due to improvements in Browser engines, the fundamental ideas are still the same. [VIDEO] If you skip to the 35 minute mark he speaks about reflow specifically. Generally I still use many of these techniques when writing vanilla JS (which is unfortunately rare these days). There are other talks where he talks about many other performance specific topics. Obviously none of this is as good as real world experience of dealing with a thorny issue."},{"id":7014188,"author":"tangue","month":"2014-01","story":"Wonderful Javascript Talks?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among classic JavaScript talks.","quote":"Speed up your Javascript","text":"Classics John Resig : The Dom is a mess [LINK] Javascript, The Good Parts [LINK] Speed up your Javascript [VIDEO] Brendan Eich : Fluent 2013: Brendan Eich, \"JavaScript at 18: Legal to Gamble\" [LINK] Paul Irish : 10 things I learned from the Jquery source [LINK] Less known but very interesting Building High-Performing JavaScript for Modern Engines [LINK]"},{"id":3551071,"author":"malandrew","month":"2012-02","story":"Ask HN: JavaScript Dev Tools","stance":"rec","why":"Google Tech Talk on JavaScript performance.","quote":"this Google Tech Talk by Nicholas Zakas about javascript performance.","text":"The only other video that sticks out in mine head is this Google Tech Talk by Nicholas Zakas about javascript performance. [VIDEO] Besides videos, check out Javascript Garden: [LINK] Javascript Weblog: [LINK] Here are some links over on Quora: [LINK] The three books worth getting for someone who is already a programmer are: Crockford's \"Javascript: The Good Parts\", Stoyan Stefanov's \"Javascript Patterns\", and Resigs's \"Secrets of the Javascript Ninja\" IMHO, there are few videos worthwhile."}],"r":[["LLCF7vPanrY",1],["hQVTIJBZook",1]]},"ra_9zU-mnl8":{"wy":"HN points people to it as the original explanation of what Gridfinity is and why it exists, in threads about the project and its catalogs.","q":{"x":"Watching Zack's original video on the concept really helps to cement why this exists.","i":33035840,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33931962,"author":"lathiat","month":"2022-12","story":"Gridfinity: Unofficial Wiki","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to the Gridfinity project by its creator","quote":"its an open and free expandable 3D printable modular storage system with modules to fit everything from screws to soldering irons and SD cards","text":"For an introduction to the project see this video from the original creator Zach Freedman: [VIDEO] (Short version its an open and free expandable 3D printable modular storage system with modules to fit everything from screws to soldering irons and SD cards)"},{"id":33735518,"author":"blamazon","month":"2022-11","story":"A 3D-printed ethernet RJ45 clip to secure/repair/fix broken tab (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Gridfinity's creator and his channel","quote":"gridfinity is a project kicked off by Zack Freedman, who makes awesome YouTube videos","text":"For the unfamiliar, gridfinity is a project kicked off by Zack Freedman, who makes awesome YouTube videos with his life partner: [VIDEO] (Yes, that is a monocular HUD on his face, it's his teleprompter. There are a bunch of videos on the channel chronicling the various iterations of it)"},{"id":33035840,"author":"jgoewert","month":"2022-09","story":"Gridfinity 3D Printed Storage and Organization Catalog","stance":"rec","why":"Original video explaining why Gridfinity exists","quote":"Watching Zack's original video on the concept really helps to cement why this exists.","text":"Cool! I was hoping someone would make a better catalog for these. Searching thangs for specific things got really murky. I made a ton of the 2 grid 3 bay windowed bins to hold M3 and M4 screws and nuts and small but numerous lego pieces. Watching Zack's original video on the concept really helps to cement why this exists. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40431237,"author":"philsnow","month":"2024-05","story":"Bento3D","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I about had a stroke when I got to 11m54s in [VIDEO] ; I think this is the correct transcription of that sentence: > I did a whole episode on how more printers' printers print prints for printers' printers than printers print prints for printed projects"}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"0M7IINwTFVw":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a showcase of the deep reverse-engineering and glitch knowledge behind speedrunning, and cite it for the wrongwarp trick.","q":{"x":"The level of reverse-engineering is impressive.","i":8890491,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":6628082,"author":"jal278","month":"2013-10","story":"Discoveries Ten Years Later in Zelda Speedrun","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Skilled runner explains a complex exploit skipping much of the game.","quote":"here's the important youtube link","text":"In case my server gets too sluggish, here's the important youtube link: [VIDEO] Basically there's a speedrunner who is technically adept at the game, is a good communicator, and explains as he plays a complex exploit that facilitates skipping a large part of the game"},{"id":8890491,"author":"stolio","month":"2015-01","story":"Devs Play – Hacking a Legend of Zelda ROM [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Speed-run where runner explains the impressive reverse-engineering involved.","quote":"The level of reverse-engineering is impressive.","text":"In a similar vein are videos from the speed-running community. Linked below is a video of an Ocarina Of Time speed-run where the guy explains a bit of what it's all about. The level of reverse-engineering is impressive. [VIDEO] But this is the first time I've heard of somebody hacking the game to highlight different aspects of the gamelplay, which is pretty cool."},{"id":9530410,"author":"zyx321","month":"2015-05","story":"Gamer beats both Mega Man X and Mega Man X2 using the same controller","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates the Ocarina of Time wrongwarp trick.","quote":"Ocarina Of Time wrongwarp trick","text":"Before that was the Ocarina Of Time wrongwarp trick [1] that became so ubiquitous in real-time runs that real-time and TAS are going head-to-head nowadays (The TAS times are actually slightly slower, due to including the title screen/outro sequence in their measurements). [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JBgG_VSP7f8",1],["jw8K460vx1c",1]]},"7LqaotiGWjQ":{"wy":"Recommended to people interested in propaganda, misinformation and how to improve public discourse.","q":{"x":"It may change the way you think about people that question the narrative.","i":27691668,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27595975,"author":"cdstyh","month":"2021-06","story":"Types of propaganda, propaganda techniques, and propaganda strategies (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on propaganda in the social media era.","quote":"I highly recommend it.","text":"The War on Sensemaking is a great series of videos on propaganda and the information ecology in the modern social media driven world. I highly recommend it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27691668,"author":"cdstyh","month":"2021-06","story":"Conspiracy: In Theory and Practice","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture that may change how you view people questioning narratives.","quote":"It may change the way you think about people that question the narrative.","text":"I recommend you watch this lecture. It may change the way you think about people that question the narrative. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21296426,"author":"undershirt","month":"2019-10","story":"Reason Won't Save Us","stance":"rec","why":"Schmachtenberger on how information ecology and debate optimize for bias.","quote":"some recent hope came from Daniel Schmachtenberger","text":"> “Courage is the solution to despair. Reason can provide no answers.” –Ernst Toller (First Reformed, 2017) I’ve felt the despair of this article for a time (and the quote above gave me some peace), but some recent hope came from Daniel Schmachtenberger[1]. He postulates that our conception of debate in our current information ecology selects and optimizes for bias. He refers to the Hegelian dialectic as an alternative to debate—of an earnest synthesis from understanding both a thesis and its antithesis, resolving paradox with a higher order model. I’ve heard this described as steel-manning[1] another’s argument to find its signal (as opposed to straw-manning the noise). But Schmachtenberger, as well as Bret Weinstein I believe, proposes that the game-theoretic win/lose dynamic of our current system is so entrenched that it really selects for this type of narrative warfare which doesn’t promote this type of open and honest information ecology for this to really work yet. So there’s something to their post-game-theoretic frameworks (Bret’s “Game B”) that make me a lot less fatalistic and anarchist about the whole complexity game. [1] The War on Sensemaking: [VIDEO] [2] a.k.a purva paksha: [LINK]"},{"id":37714206,"author":"cf141q5325","month":"2023-09","story":"As We May Think (1945)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"First off, thanks for mentioning Karl Popper and the media literacy theory. Will have to look into that. I wish you the best here. Have been thinking about the issue as well and have been drifting more and more in the direction dealing with human error / aiding human thinking by just raising the bar abit without trying to find completeness and focusing on acceptability. Fixing the stuff that should be easiest. So looking at the description of errors and and how to communicate them in a sensible way. A minimum consensus based safety focused approach with capsuled complexity seems to be the way to go. A few perspectives you may or may not have heard of and find interesting in this context. In the spirit of capsuled complexity and accessibility with good podcasts / videos: Example for the concept and power of capsuled complexity with the 5 level explanation series from WIRED [LINK] You already mentioned information pollution so you might be familiar with the whole military perspective, from operations in the information environment / cognitive warfare, Diss/Miss Information and missing context, psychographic advertisement, ODAA Loop and the research coming out of IBMs Watson by Juliane Gallinas with her Ted Talk about \"(General)Patton in a Box\" [LINK] [LINK] If things shift into a game theoretical perspective its also always worth while reminding people that even psychopaths like drinkable tap water. There is some win-win stuff. (Air) Accident investigations and crew resource ma"},{"id":33950493,"author":"cf141q5325","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Best books read in 2022","stance":"reference","why":"Schmachtenberger's proposals for improving discourse.","quote":"","text":"Thanks for the resources, especially the Bonhoeffer theory and the nice feedback. I do appreciate it. I can also only return the favor, being able to come to an agreement on such points is really good news and i found this quite beneficial as well. The difficulty of it (conflict in speech is just so much more easy and enjoyable) is why i have so high hopes for technology. Because you wont just help with understanding but being more easy to understand. Which means getting more control about how you frame stuff without just trying to influence your conversation partner. I am not so optimistic about education beyond a base level though, getting a grip of framing stuff, and not just reacting, is just very difficult no matter how much you meditate, at least for idiots like myself. Schmachtenberger [0] is proposing that same approach with the stuff around \"rule omega\", but as you can take from the close to 10 hours of videos and not making much visible progress since, you are kind of trying to build a new non-stupid human. When it comes to possible solutions, cooperative minimum is key imho. Which likely means the solution cant be aimed at gaining political influence or monetary benefits. Because people react to such attempts of influence as well. And as as with every tool, there will be attempts of weaponization. Which gets us to conflict again, which makes the experience everywhere more shit. In case anyone is wondering about the poverty point, its so people get the option to not"}],"r":[["2qsmF8HHskg",1]]},"LS3GQk9ETRU":{"wy":"It comes up as a great explainer on magnetohydrodynamic drives and pumps in threads about silent submarine propulsion.","q":{"x":"There's a great video from one of my favorite YouTube channels about Magnetohydrodynamic drives.","i":21976109,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":21976109,"author":"bloudermilk","month":"2020-01","story":"The propeller is giving way to the pump-jet propulsor on larger submarines","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great explainer on magnetohydrodynamic drives from a favorite channel.","quote":"There's a great video from one of my favorite YouTube channels about Magnetohydrodynamic drives.","text":"There's a great video from one of my favorite YouTube channels about Magnetohydrodynamic drives. Really fascinating drive design and channel for anyone interested in engineering. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36171606,"author":"elihu","month":"2023-06","story":"Taking a New Look at Fundamental Tech for Quiet Undersea Propulsion","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how magnetohydrodynamic pumps work.","quote":"there's a video on the Tech Ingredients channel that explains magnetohydrodynamic pumps","text":"Interesting. If I understand correctly, they need a electrode that's in physical contact with the sea water. I'm not sure I understand it entirely, but there's a video on the Tech Ingredients channel that explains magnetohydrodynamic pumps: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18106319,"author":"macromaniac","month":"2018-09","story":"Magnetic Field Record Set with a Bang: 1,200 Tesla","stance":"reference","why":"Explains magnetohydrodynamic propulsion.","quote":"you could do something akin to magnetohydrodynamics","text":"If can get to 4 billion tesla I /think/ that's enough to generate electrons from the vacuum of space (see:schwinger magnetic induction) and you could do something akin to magnetohydrodynamics ( see: [VIDEO] ) which would use the magnetic field to accelerate the electrons away from the ship, I could be incorrect here though"}],"r":[["JtKAScORevQ",1],["jA1C9VFqJKo",1],["thOifuHs6eY",1],["ljOoGyCso8s",1]]},"UOHURuAf5iY":{"wy":"Commenters link it as an approachable explanation of concrete basics in threads about Roman concrete, construction and sand supply.","q":{"x":"You're going to love this playlist from Practical Engineering [1].","i":34286206,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34286206,"author":"walty","month":"2023-01","story":"Riddle solved: Why was Roman concrete so durable?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Playlist explaining reinforcement and pre-stressing well","quote":"You're going to love this playlist from Practical Engineering [1].","text":"You're going to love this playlist from Practical Engineering [1]. Grady explains all of this much better than I could. The second video explains reinforcement and the fifth explains pre-stressing. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32116807,"author":"zen_of_prog","month":"2022-07","story":"How the Colosseum was built and why it was an architectural marvel","stance":"rec","why":"Explains concrete versus cement and reinforcement","quote":"This might help too","text":"Concrete is made up of both cement (the CONtinuous part) and aggregate/stone (the disCRETE part), so cement vs concrete is kind of like broth vs soup, and concrete and stone like soup vs vegetables. > So the main difference is that you can shape it without chiselling it. I guess, yeah. Both are really good at withstanding compressive forces. Unlike Roman times, concrete today is usually reinforced with steel rebar, which gives it good tensile strength as well. This makes it useful for a lot of things that stone is not. Disclaimer, take everything here with a grain of salt. Continuous-discrete is not the etymology of concrete. This might help too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17921820,"author":"CGamesPlay","month":"2018-09","story":"Sand scarcity is about more than “running out” of sand","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates different concrete ingredients","quote":"Practical Engineering did a good demonstration of different concrete ingredients","text":"I think that's basically plaster of paris? Practical Engineering did a good demonstration of different concrete ingredients: [VIDEO]"}]},"WB2-2j9e4co":{"wy":"Held up as the ultimate example of small-space design and a beautiful compact home.","q":{"x":"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes","i":20246851,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20246851,"author":"cwkoss","month":"2019-06","story":"Swedish Couple Builds Greenhouse Around Home","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gary Chang's 24-rooms-in-1 transformer apartment in Hong Kong.","quote":"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes","text":"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes, 'green' construction, etc. Literally hundreds of hours of high-quality content - I'm really curious how she is able to find all of these interesting people who are doing such cool things. Channel: [LINK] Some other favorites: Baubotanik shapes living tree branches into building facades - [LINK] Boeing retiree finds meaning inventing micro homes & high speed trikes - [LINK] Extreme transformer home in Hong Kong: Gary Chang's 24 rooms in 1 - [VIDEO] Earth-cooled, shipping container underground CA home for 30K - [LINK] Yokohama narrow tiny house \"breathes\" & attracts local nature - [LINK]"},{"id":40360548,"author":"Pet_Ant","month":"2024-05","story":"Ford trims EV battery orders as losses swell to over $100k per electric car","stance":"rec","why":"Example of a beautiful luxurious small apartment.","quote":"Actually this is just under 350 sq ft and just beautiful and luxurious","text":"> The environment is a nice side effect. So you don't really care about the environment, not really strongly at least, at least you are honest about it. I respect that. > why not just take the bus? I do take the bus, and have resisted getting a car, managing with Uber Green when necessary. > why not just walk? I do when I can, it's not practical for time and/or safety reasons, but it is ideal. > Don't buy a house because it's an inefficient waste of space, resources, and energy. This!!! God are single family dwellings the worst blight on the earth. They just shit suburban sprawl on the green earth. So much waste. Don't get my started on lawns. No reuse of infrastructure for heating or cooling. Just bad. > Live in a studio apartment under 300sq ft, enough for a bed and a desk. Actually this is just under 350 sq ft and just beautiful and luxurious: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25100712,"author":"dotBen","month":"2020-11","story":"Ori Pocket Office","stance":"reference","why":"Transformable apartment predating Ori.","quote":"The ultimate instantiation of this concept is architect Gary Chang's childhood HK apartment","text":"The ultimate instantiation of this concept is architect Gary Chang's childhood HK apartment that he transformed with this kind of concept throughout. This also pre-dates Ori, I believe as this is from 10-15 years ago. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4Z75A_JMBx4",1],["Z0oFJ2jbkDI",1],["0olpSN6_TCc",1]]},"YcUycQoz0zg":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it for the remarkable remote reverse engineering done on decades-old hardware with no simulator.","q":{"x":"Blows my mind of the reverse engineering done to achieve this","i":49218987,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49218987,"author":"wanderingmind","month":"2026-08","story":"NASA to keep its 48-year-old Voyager 2 probe running for yet another year","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Technical deep dive on fixing Voyager 1 remotely.","quote":"Blows my mind of the reverse engineering done to achieve this","text":"A slight tangent here is a technical deep dive on Voyager 1 fixing a memory corruption error in 2023 by hand 15 billion miles away. Blows my mind of the reverse engineering done to achieve this [VIDEO]"},{"id":47823974,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2026-04","story":"NASA Shuts Off Instrument on Voyager 1 to Keep Spacecraft Operating","stance":"rec","why":"Walkthrough of the flight computer and the memory chip fix.","quote":"It goes over how the flight computer works and he walks through a few assembly routines.","text":"Here's a talk about how the Voyager team fixed the flight data computer on Voyager 1 when a memory chip went bad on it a few years ago. It goes over how the flight computer works and he walks through a few assembly routines. [VIDEO] Some of the challenges they had to deal with while developing the fix: - The only source code they had for the flight data software was an OCR'd Microsoft Word document (with typos) that was likely scanned from a hard copy assembler listing printout. - The processor runs a custom instruction set developed by JPL for the Voyager mission. The documentation they had on the processor was incomplete. - Everybody who had designed the flight software was dead. - They had no assembler, no debugger, and no processor simulator. They had no testbed, the only two FDS processors were in space."},{"id":47567982,"author":"zerd","month":"2026-03","story":"Voyager 1 runs on 69 KB of memory and an 8-track tape recorder","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on fixing Voyager without simulator, tests, or complete source.","quote":"","text":"Based on the communication fix, they also didn't have a simulator, or tests, or complete source code, on a custom instruction set that wasn't well documented, so they had to reverse engineer how it worked. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ispyUPqqL1c",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["3ZTGwcHQfLY",1]]},"bM0PmwOlifE":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a compelling demonstration that metadata identifies people, and as a great watch on sloppy CIA tradecraft.","q":{"x":"This is one of the best videos I have ever seen countering this argument.","i":8490527,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8490527,"author":"_nullandnull_","month":"2014-10","story":"Troubling Arguments from the Government in Smith v. Obama","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Counters the claim that call records do not identify people","quote":"This is one of the best videos I have ever seen countering this argument.","text":"> Call Detail Records Don’t Actually Identify People This is one of the best videos I have ever seen countering this argument. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20802265,"author":"guitarbill","month":"2019-08","story":"Why the CIA Doesn't Spy on the UAE","stance":"rec","why":"Investigation of the Abu Omar case and sloppy CIA tradecraft","quote":"the Black Hat 2013 talk on this is a great watch","text":"who could forget the Abu Omar Case, where the CIA kidnapped an individual in Milan. but they left evidence due to sloppy OPSEC. so we know for sure they used to do much worse. I guess most spying activities don't warrant such a detailed investigation though, so we never find out. the Black Hat 2013 talk on this is a great watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21005236,"author":"keehun","month":"2019-09","story":"Facebook launches Portal TV, a $149 video chat set-top box","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what leaked metadata reveals about you","quote":"Check out what leaking even just the metadata can tell about you.","text":"What none of the privacy-related prose defense by Facebook addresses is the abundant value of the metadata that Facebook will be able to harvest. Check out what leaking even just the metadata can tell about you.[0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10429172,"author":"godgod","month":"2015-10","story":"CIA Director John Brennan emails","stance":"reference","why":"Shows CIA OPSEC failures using cell technology","quote":"this video is semi-related and showing OPSEC failures by CIA using cell technology","text":"While not a talk by Applebaum, this video is semi-related and showing OPSEC failures by CIA using cell technology: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["MsbiO8EAsGw",1],["S-3wEC6Fj_8",1]]},"lqJUIqZNzP8":{"wy":"HN recommends Robert Miles' channel as a clear starting point for people curious about AI safety.","q":{"x":"For those curious about AI safety I heavily recommend Robert Miles channel on YouTube.","i":34459007,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34459007,"author":"jononor","month":"2023-01","story":"Not worried about AI that passes Turing test, but AI that fails it on purpose","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Starting point on Robert Miles' AI safety content.","quote":"For those curious about AI safety I heavily recommend Robert Miles channel on YouTube.","text":"For those curious about AI safety I heavily recommend Robert Miles channel on YouTube. He has lot of good content describing out the challenges we face when trying to create safe AI. Here is one potential starting point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17147271,"author":"nmca","month":"2018-05","story":"Uber Self-Driving Car That Struck Pedestrian Wasn’t Set to Stop in an Emergency","stance":"rec","why":"Covers lack of safe fallback policies in AI safety.","quote":"is covered nicely in this youtube video, as well as the paper referenced there","text":"This highlights an interesting general point - in many situations, there is no simple safe fallback policy. On a highway, an emergency stop is not safe. This is a general problem in AI safety and is covered nicely in this youtube video, as well as the paper referenced there - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18501516,"author":"pas","month":"2018-11","story":"Superintelligence: The Idea That Eats Smart People (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Concrete AI safety problems with code and simulations.","quote":"See also the AI safety problems for concrete problems that people are currently working on","text":"Re: alignment, see the \"Where are we\" part of this talk: [LINK] See also the AI safety problems for concrete problems that people are currently working on - as in with code and real simulations, not \"just\" math and thinking: [VIDEO] Re: scaling up. That's called intelligence explosion, there are a lot of write ups about it, but the gist of it is just that by simply increasing working memory size, thinking speed, prediction precision, pattern matching, and so on, you get to unimaginably high levels. Furthermore, if you can make a human level AI, it naturally follows that it's only a matter of time to make it slightly better. At first simply faster, simply more eloquent, a bit more emotional depth, it gets better at speaking, thinking. At first it's a child that asks questions, later it's a clever guy who spots your errors as you code, and in no time, I mean no time, it does whatever it wants, because by the time you try to confront it about something, you lost, because you are bogged down in an argument, while it does whatever it wants. (Unless of course it's contained. For a while at least.)"}],"r":[["MYucYon2-lk",1],["9dKiLclupUM",1],["r0plv_nIzsQ",1]]},"sJj9y4t9UnU":{"wy":"Shared as an example of Artemyev, called the Russian Vangelis, and of great Soviet electronic film music.","q":{"x":"it hit me back hard how great this music is, even genius-like, I would say.","i":30995891,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30995891,"author":"paganel","month":"2022-04","story":"Eduard Artemyev, Soviet and Russian composer of electronic music and film scores","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Theme that hits hard, even genius-like","quote":"it hit me back hard how great this music is, even genius-like, I would say.","text":"Just stumbled on his Siberiade theme [1] recently, I hadn't listened to it in a couple of years, it hit me back hard how great this music is, even genius-like, I would say. There's a slightly re-mastered version by PPK [2] that manages to \"increase\" that genius a little. Amazing stuff. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":1871559,"author":"hackermom","month":"2010-11","story":"Soviet Image Editing Tool from 1987","stance":"rec","why":"Theme by the Russian Vangelis, defining sci-fi film music","quote":"It's Eduard Artemyev, by many considered \"the Russian Vangelis\".","text":"[VIDEO] It's Eduard Artemyev, by many considered \"the Russian Vangelis\". His work has been truly defining for a handful of Russian sci-fi masterpieces."},{"id":36436835,"author":"retrac","month":"2023-06","story":"The Mellotron, an electronic keyboard of recorded samples, changed pop music","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the ANS synthesizer in a film soundtrack","quote":"It features prominently in the soundtrack by Eduard Artemyev, for the historical film Siberiade from 1979.","text":"Conceptually similar, there was also the ANS synthesizer [1] designed by the Soviet engineer Evgeny Murzin. Unlike the Mellotron, it was opto-electronic, giving the composer more direct access and control over the \"samples\". Waveforms were drawn on glass plates, and pressing one of the keys would scan across the associated plate, feeding the generated signal through various filters, etc. It features prominently in the soundtrack by Eduard Artemyev, for the historical film Siberiade from 1979. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bwDrHqNZ9lo",1],["k7X7sZzSXYs",1],["uXdtafGdIVM",1]]},"uKfKtXYLG78":{"wy":"HN treats it as a classic that shows what Erlang was built for: robust, concurrent telecom systems with live upgrades.","q":{"x":"this is truly a gem","i":18656072,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18656072,"author":"jimbokun","month":"2018-12","story":"Twenty Years of Open Source Erlang: A Retrospective from the trenches","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great explanation of Erlang's benefits for robust systems","quote":"this is truly a gem","text":"I had never watched Erlang the Movie before, this is truly a gem: [VIDEO] This is a great way to explain the benefits of Erlang. Anyone who has tried to write robust, distributed systems should immediately see the benefits. Even all of these years later."},{"id":14424153,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2017-05","story":"Let's Encrypt OCSP and Issuance Outage Postmortem","stance":"rec","why":"Classic demo of hot code swapping","quote":"Their original video demo of hot code swapping is also a classic worth checking out","text":"Their original video demo of hot code swapping is also a classic worth checking out: [VIDEO] That got a lot of programmers interested in Erlang ~10yrs ago."},{"id":25801622,"author":"probst","month":"2021-01","story":"Why is signal servers using Java and not Erlang","stance":"reference","why":"Erlang showcased in a telecom setting","quote":"They made a video way back showcasing Erlang in the setting it was primarily used: telecom systems.","text":"Hehe, shame this got down voted so much. It's actually a pretty hilarious Erlang joke. They made a video way back showcasing Erlang in the setting it was primarily used: telecom systems. In this video they keep calling and greeting each other. The video can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24658386,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2020-10","story":"A Brief Guide to OTP in Elixir","stance":"reference","why":"Original video showing telecom hot code loading","quote":"This is the original video in its 90s glory","text":"This is the original video in its 90s glory: [VIDEO] Regardless of where the call is handled, you can't just deploy a new switch in a telecom network, but you still want to upgrade them. This is where you really want hot code loading."},{"id":21368986,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2019-10","story":"Show HN: Siproc – A primitive SIP client that spawns processes for each call","stance":"reference","why":"Erlang's process-per-call model","quote":"See also: Erlang The Movie","text":"Spawning a process for each call / endpoint is how almost every large phone switchboard does it. See also: Erlang The Movie: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20267058,"author":"darkhorn","month":"2019-06","story":"Dutch Telephone Outage Takes Out Nation's Emergency Number","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"They should have used Erlang [VIDEO] I wonder what coused this issue? Dependency issue in Maven? May be they cannot figure out what that Java error means?"}],"r":[["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["5Vt8zqhHe_c",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1]]},"7W33HRc1A6c":{"wy":"HN quotes it in environmental threads as a famous, darkly funny take that the planet will outlast humanity.","q":{"x":"I cannot explain it better than George Carlin [1]. Enjoy!","i":24472497,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24472497,"author":"kbr2000","month":"2020-09","story":"Sir David Attenborough makes stark warning about species extinction","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin explains the problem will solve itself.","quote":"I cannot explain it better than George Carlin [1]. Enjoy!","text":"Hey, you can be proud for having spent time thinking about it to start with. And for having tried! Feeling responsible for it yourself alone is not necessary, it's bigger than that. Here's a thought or two by Attenborough on overpopulation, one of the drivers of these problems [0] The problem will solve itself however (with certainty), and I cannot explain it better than George Carlin [1]. Enjoy! [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18911203,"author":"radiorental","month":"2019-01","story":"Insect collapse","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin's saving-the-planet comedy as comfort on extinction.","quote":"It's never a bad time to listen to George Carlin's \"saving the planet","text":"I view our success as a product of evolution. It is the driving factor that got us down out of the trees, cultivating our food and to where we are today. Would we be putting a little less pressure on the environment without people like Edward Bernays, possibly. However, I think our collective consciousness can put itself a little at ease. As we enter the 6 mass extinction event, it's a natural cycle and if it wasn't us, some other species would dominate the planet. It's never a bad time to listen to George Carlin's \"saving the planet\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30683430,"author":"Gravityloss","month":"2022-03","story":"Antarctic sea ice hits lowest minimum on record","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses Carlin's skit and its punchline about people.","quote":"George Carlin has his famous skit.","text":"Now have time for a longer reply. There are a few different phenomena. First is that \"fear\" and \"anxiety\" are indeed used by the news media or politicians to get more attention. There I agree. It is cheap, unsustainable and harmful. But there's more to glaciers than news fearmongering. The second concept is \"the planet doesn't care\". George Carlin has his famous skit. [VIDEO] Of course life will continue on earth, after relatively large temperature swings or 10 kilometer asteroid hits etc. That doesn't mean we don't need to care about anything. His skit can be seen as a wake up call (which he might or might not have meant). Why would we do anything? Why would you recycle instead of dumping plastic bags in the river? Why would you put the safety on in your gun? The planet will be fine even if you blow your leg off. But you will be affected. It's the people that are f.... That's the punchline. But I also disagree about his \"don't meddle with nature, let the species die\". If we change something, let's say, dump cyanide or mercury in a river, it's not \"natural\" for animals to die downstream. It's not \"nature doing its thing\". It's our responsibility. It's an untenable philosophical position to do something and then act as if the consequences don't have anything to do with the deed. But that's not the core point of the skit. So about caring. If there was an asteroid headed towards earth, I'd care. Yes, it's happened before, and life has survived. But I still care. It would have gr"},{"id":24451879,"author":"34679","month":"2020-09","story":"How Big Oil Misled the Public into Believing Plastic Would Be Recycled","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's plastic bit quoted in full.","quote":"","text":"George Carlin: \"And if it’s true that plastic is not degradable, well, the planet will simply incorporate plastic into a new paradigm: the earth plus plastic. The earth doesn’t share our prejudice toward plastic. Plastic came out of the earth. The earth probably sees plastic as just another one of its children. Could be the only reason the earth allowed us to be spawned from it in the first place. It wanted plastic for itself. Didn’t know how to make it. Needed us. Could be the answer to our age-old egocentric philosophical question, “Why are we here?” Plastic… asshole.” Full text: [LINK] Video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18249261,"author":"subcosmos","month":"2018-10","story":"Microplastics found in 90 percent of table salt","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin cited as having been right about pollution.","quote":"Carlin had it right ....","text":"This is getting absolutely rediculous ... Epoxy resins may very well have created the Diabetes epidemic, with genetic data backing it. [LINK] And the chemical used to make Teflon was dumped into the Mississippi river valley for years, leading to a spate of birth defects and other more common medical problems. Dupont paid hundreds of millions : [LINK] Carlin had it right .... [VIDEO]"},{"id":16439480,"author":"cornholio","month":"2018-02","story":"Taiwan ban single-use plastic straws, plastic bags, disposable utensils by 2030","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin: the planet is fine, the people are not.","quote":"the planet is just fine. It's the people who are f*ed","text":"By that metric, what is the environment and why is it important to protect it? On an abstract enough scale, we and our plastic are simply evolutionary products and the fact that we are wiping out species and cataclysmically heating the planet is just a geological event that might be called The Great Human Extinction by some distant inteligent race. As George Carlin used to say, the planet is just fine. It's the people who are f*ed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15699563,"author":"badosu","month":"2017-11","story":"Scientists have signed the largest-ever warning about Earth’s destruction","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin routine saying the planet will outlast humanity.","quote":"Could not resist mentioning \"Save the planet\" from Geroge Carlin","text":"Could not resist mentioning \"Save the planet\" from Geroge Carlin: The planet isn’t going anywhere. We are! We’re goin’ away. Pack your shit, Folks, we’re goin’ away. We won’t leave much of a trace either, thank god for that. Maybe a little styrofoam, maybe, little styrofoam. Planet’ll be here and we’ll be long gone. Just another failed mutation. Just another closed-end biological mistake, an evolutionary cul de sac. The planet will shake us off like a bad case of fleas, a surface nuisance. You wanna know how the planet’s doin’? Ask those people at Pompeii, who were frozen into position from volcanic ash. How the planet’s doin’. Wanna know if the planet’s alright, ask those people in Mexico City or Armenia, or a hundred other places buried under thousands of tons of earthquake rubble if they feel like a threat to the planet this week. How about those people in Kilauea, Hawaii who built their homes right next to an active volcano and then wonder why they have lava in the living room. The planet will be here for a long, long, long time after we’re gone and it will heal itself, it will cleanse itself ’cuz that’s what it does. It’s a self-correcting system. The air and the water will recover, the earth will be renewed, and if it’s true that plastic is not degradable well, the planet will simply incorporate plastic into a new paradigm: the earth plus plastic. The earth doesn’t share our prejudice towards plastic. Plastic came out of the earth. The earth probably sees plastic as jus"},{"id":7794482,"author":"pavanred","month":"2014-05","story":"Trillions of plastic pieces found in Arctic ice","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's famous planet-will-be-fine argument.","quote":"it's a very famous argument made by George Carlin","text":"Not sure if you based it on this, but it's a very famous argument made by George Carlin [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42005498,"author":"latexr","month":"2024-10","story":"Cooking with black plastic is particularly crucial to avoid","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It will solve itself, nature adapts fast Indeed. The first step is for the planet to get rid of the pesky pollutants, perhaps by way of launching several “natural disasters” such as mighty strong winds, excessive floods, and particularly pesky deathly organisms. Then it can deal with plastic at its leisure. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16349297,"author":"rasengan0","month":"2018-02","story":"There Might Be No Way to Live Comfortably Without Also Ruining the Planet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's nice to know we're all gonna die with generational suffering down the road. I'd like to know what Vietnam is doing and how might that model be improved. Data --> ML --> policy, etc With all this bickering on who's right or wrong, it is no wonder that nothing will change and humanity will go the way of the cynical comedian: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14742608,"author":"mdekkers","month":"2017-07","story":"Earth's sixth mass extinction event already under way, scientists warn","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"When it will come down to the last 1000 people on this planet, 400 will be arguing about whether or not humans really have a bad impact on this planet; 50 will be trying to lord it over the rest, using another gullible 150 as muscle; 200 will be looking for ways to turn a profit out of the situation; and the rest will weeping somewhere in a corner. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10763466,"author":"codeshaman","month":"2015-12","story":"Wild bees are recycling plastic, study finds (2014)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The planet will be here a long long time after we're gone.. And it will heal itself, it will cleanse itself, because that's what it does. It's a self correcting system. The air and the water will recover, the earth will be renewed and if it's true that plastic is not degradable, well, the planet will simply incorporate plastic into a new paradigm - the Earth plus Plastic. The Earth doesn't share our prejudice towards plastic. Plastic came out of the Earth. The Earth probably sees plastic as just another one of it's children. Could be the only reason the Earth allowed us to be spawned from it in the first place. It wanted plastic for itself. Didn't know how to make it. Needed us.. Could be the answer to the age old philosophical question \"Why are we here ?\".. Plastic, assholes! :) --- George Carlin [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Dddbe9OuJLU",1],["xMP_AfiNlX4",1],["Ki_Af_o9Q9s",1],["A2zKARkpDW4",1]]},"GLQyj-kBRdo":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining first-hand engineer account of what went wrong with healthcare.gov and how it was fixed, including that no one owned the whole site.","q":{"x":"A couple of my colleagues have given talks on healthcare.gov from the perspective of the engineers who worked around the clock to help get the site running again","i":8112488,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":10248103,"author":"sylvinus","month":"2015-09","story":"How Healthcare.gov Botched $600M Worth of Contracts","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk by a Google engineer who helped fix the site","quote":"You can watch a very entertaining talk from one of the Google engineers who went to fix the website at the time","text":"You can watch a very entertaining talk from one of the Google engineers who went to fix the website at the time: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8112488,"author":"brandonb","month":"2014-07","story":"Poorly Managed HealthCare.gov Construction Cost $840M, Watchdog Finds","stance":"rec","why":"Engineers' account of fixing healthcare.gov","quote":"A couple of my colleagues have given talks on healthcare.gov from the perspective of the engineers who worked around the clock to help get the site running again","text":"I was part of the \"tech surge\" brought in to help patch up healthcare.gov in December. The GAO report gives a decent idea of what went wrong in 2010-2013 from a high-level contracting and policy perspective, but it doesn't really give much technical detail or describe what was broken about the organization, software architecture, and culture. A couple of my colleagues have given talks on healthcare.gov from the perspective of the engineers who worked around the clock to help get the site running again: [LINK] [VIDEO] A small team is also re-writing the site, starting with the most troublesome components for the next open enrollment period. Wired covered some of what is going on here: [LINK] If any of you out there want to help, please email jobs@hcgov.us!"},{"id":8114869,"author":"angersock","month":"2014-07","story":"“Agile” methodologies blamed for healthcare.gov failure","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how contractors owned pieces but nobody owned the website","quote":"Worth watching","text":"Worth watching: [VIDEO] I like the way the presenter sums it up...it seems like each contractor was responsible for their own little piece of the stack, and nobody was actually responsible for delivering a website . That should've been the gating criteria."},{"id":8165506,"author":"sylvinus","month":"2014-08","story":"White House launches “U.S. Digital Service,” with HealthCare.gov fixer at helm","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on what went wrong technically","quote":"There was a talk at dotScale about the healthcare.gov website and what went bad on a technical level","text":"There was a talk at dotScale about the healthcare.gov website and what went bad on a technical level: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8098300,"author":"sylvinus","month":"2014-07","story":"The first Swift conference worldwide (Feb 6th, Paris)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a talk filmed in a theater","quote":"You can check this as an example","text":"I think it has to do mostly with the image they want to have in the developer community. Plus, watching a HD video shot in a Theater like ours is arguably more enjoyable and impactful than something shot in a studio. You can check this as an example: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LNjJTgXujno",1]]},"Gs790JOeN3Y":{"wy":"HN commenters point people dealing with concussions or brain injuries to it as a hopeful first-hand story of recovery.","q":{"x":"he had a concussion and seems to be handling it quite well, even recovering","i":36536604,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36505265,"author":"jchw","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: How to navigate the world as a brain-damaged 17 year old?","stance":"rec","why":"YouTuber's head injury story and bad medical advice","quote":"maybe you'll find this video from Strange Parts interesting","text":"Hey, sorry to hear about the bullies, I don't understand why everyone tolerates the tragically broken school systems we have (I'm in the U.S., and my experience with school wasn't great either. Nobody cares here either.) I can't give much in terms of life advice, but maybe you'll find this video from Strange Parts interesting, a YouTuber who almost lost everything due to a head injury and got terrible advice from well-meaning medical professionals. I have No idea if this could possibly be useful, but I hate the idea that someone might be able to recover still but never gets the right help. [VIDEO] There's so many different kinds of head injuries, though, and I'm sure they are all very different. But, I still wish you best of luck in recovery."},{"id":36505308,"author":"trebligdivad","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: How to navigate the world as a brain-damaged 17 year old?","stance":"rec","why":"Video about recovery from brain injury","quote":"The guy who runs the Strange Parts channel has a video about his recovery;","text":"I've read about a few people who've recovered from brain injuries - but it does seem to take ages - so hang in there! The guy who runs the Strange Parts channel has a video about his recovery; [VIDEO]"},{"id":36536604,"author":"vilvadot","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: How to navigate the world as a brain-damaged 17 year old?","stance":"rec","why":"Creator handling concussion and recovering","quote":"he had a concussion and seems to be handling it quite well, even recovering","text":"Sorry to hear about your attack. It reminded me of Cory from Strangeparts, he had a concussion and seems to be handling it quite well, even recovering: [VIDEO] . Hope it can help you somehow. Hope you best of luck with whatever you decide to pursue."},{"id":34917030,"author":"iamflimflam1","month":"2023-02","story":"Implant lets those with severe paralysis send texts with just their minds","stance":"rec","why":"YouTuber's recovery from a severe concussion","quote":"Might be worth having a look at the Strange Parts YouTuber","text":"Might be worth having a look at the Strange Parts YouTuber - he suffered severe concussion after an accident with serious brain fog and has managed to come out the other side. I believe he used these guys: [LINK] or maybe these guys: [LINK] No idea if this would be covered by your health insurance (assuming you're USA). [VIDEO]"},{"id":39751093,"author":"evilai","month":"2024-03","story":"We've Been Treating Concussions All Wrong","stance":"reference","why":"Similar experience with incorrect treatment of a concussion","quote":"It's incredible the harmful effect that incorrect medical treatment can have.","text":"Very similar to the experience recounted by this engineer YouTuber[1], who is partly known for modifying his iPhone to add a headphone jack[2]. It's incredible the harmful effect that incorrect medical treatment can have. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["KrksBdWcZgQ",1]]},"dfY5ZQDzC5s":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a visual illustration of wingtip vortices and wake turbulence when discussing lift and aircraft wakes.","q":{"x":"the spiraling wingtip vortices which can be observed in this neat video","i":29027864,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29027864,"author":"ertemplin","month":"2021-10","story":"Incorrect Lift Theory","stance":"rec","why":"Shows spiraling wingtip vortices, the hazardous part of wake turbulence.","quote":"the spiraling wingtip vortices which can be observed in this neat video","text":"Regarding your 2nd answer, taking off quickly after another aircraft has departed is difficult because of wake turbulence. The most hazardous part of wake turbulence is the spiraling wingtip vortices which can be observed in this neat video: [VIDEO] One notable wake turbulence incident was in 2006 at Boeing Field in Seattle where a Cessna 172 and Boeing 747 were both on final approach for parallel runways. The Cessna 172 got flipped almost upside down and recovered 150 feet over the terrain: [LINK]"},{"id":13890865,"author":"phumbe","month":"2017-03","story":"Enroute Airbus A380 wake flips Challenger business jet upside down","stance":"rec","why":"Shows wingtip vortices created by various aircraft.","quote":"there are several great youtube videos[1-4] that show the wakes (the wingtip vortices in particular) created by various aircraft.","text":"For anyone interested, there are several great youtube videos[1-4] that show the wakes (the wingtip vortices in particular) created by various aircraft. This incident brings the Reduced Vertical Separation Minima (RVSM)[5] into question. Strategic Lateral Offset Procedure (SLOP)[6] can be used used to avoid such incidents. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"},{"id":46724373,"author":"mncharity","month":"2026-01","story":"Show HN: Interactive physics simulations I built while teaching my daughter","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a moment of cloud deck disturbance by a plane.","quote":"","text":"Two quick errata: In Sound / Biology, a control inversion: low pitch (slider and sound) animates blue short hairs, and high pitch animates long red. In the windtunnel > Wing, lift is reported but the airstream remains undeflected.[1] [1] randomly, fwiw, I've used cloud deck slicing to illustrate downdraft, eg [LINK] [LINK] . Or perhaps moments of \"yoink\" like... err, [VIDEO] ."},{"id":22995495,"author":"mncharity","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What scientific phenomenon do you wish someone would explain better?","stance":"reference","why":"Visual aid for how flight throws air downward.","quote":"Visuals help","text":"One concept is \"heavier than air\" flight is throwing air downward. Visuals help: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"}]},"ssZ_8cqfBlE":{"wy":"Cited in automation and robotics threads as the example of a warehouse built robot-first.","q":{"x":"I thought this was an interesting insight into what a fully-automated grocery store looks like.","i":29988275,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29988275,"author":"cecilpl2","month":"2022-01","story":"Automation is reaching more companies","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into a fully automated grocery store designed for automation.","quote":"I thought this was an interesting insight into what a fully-automated grocery store looks like.","text":"I thought this was an interesting insight into what a fully-automated grocery store looks like. And also wild to think about how different things are when you design them for automation from the ground up. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27935066,"author":"cbm-vic-20","month":"2021-07","story":"Intrinsic, a new Alphabet company","stance":"rec","why":"Tour of a grocery packing warehouse with a sophisticated picking network.","quote":"Tom Scott had a good video a couple of weeks ago about a grocery packing warehouse","text":"Tom Scott had a good video a couple of weeks ago about a grocery packing warehouse that has a sophisticated picking network: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49059697,"author":"pj_mukh","month":"2026-07","story":"Park by Robot at London Gatwick Airport","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Great, now let's (gradually) get rid of all surface parking lots and build hyper-optimized multi-floor parking setups not made for human driving. A sort of Ocado Grocery Warehouse [1] for cars. [1]: [VIDEO] As always, yes I know this already exists in Japan."},{"id":46601316,"author":"shagie","month":"2026-01","story":"Postal Arbitrage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"grocery fulfillment centers are different than Amazon","text":"> With online shopping and delivery, the warehouse can be a dark, cramped, hot, robot-filled pandemonium in the worst part of town. Tom Scott - How many robots does it take to run a grocery store? [VIDEO] (grocery fulfillment centers are different than Amazon) ... It's also coldish. [LINK]"},{"id":44137413,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-05","story":"The ‘white-collar bloodbath’ is all part of the AI hype machine","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> or a waiter serving food at a restaurant, Over the last few years, I've seen a few in use here in Berlin: [LINK] > or someone who restocks shelves at grocery stores For physical retail, or home delivery? People are working on this for traditional stores, but I can't tell which news stories are real and which are hype — after around a decade of Musk promising FSD within a year or so, I know not to simply trust press releases even when they have a video of the thing apparently working. For home delivery, this is mostly kinda solved: [VIDEO] > Plus, in the case of service work like being a waiter, I imagine some customers will always be willing to pay for a human face. Sure… if they have the money. But can we make an economy where all the stuff is free, and we're \"working\" n-hours a day smiling at bad jokes and manners of people we don't like, so we can earn money to spend to convince someone else who doesn't like us to spend m-hours a day smiling at our bad jokes and manners?"},{"id":43623199,"author":"0cf8612b2e1e","month":"2025-04","story":"Hyundai to buy 'thousands' of Boston Dynamics robots","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"here is a video of an automated grocery warehouse","text":"Not heavy industry, but here is a video of an automated grocery warehouse: [VIDEO] If you stick with the human first design, you miss out on enormous potential efficiency gains."},{"id":42449846,"author":"ben_w","month":"2024-12","story":"Advanced Expressive Humanoid Whole-Body Control","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"For shopping, surely the best robotic option are the various things the huge warehouses use, rather than havingthan a humanoid going around a store meant for humans? Both the things that look like Roombas and all these: [VIDEO] No single consumer would buy all that, but the retailers can."},{"id":42464323,"author":"Symbiote","month":"2024-12","story":"Amazon workers to strike at multiple US warehouses during busy holiday season","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There are robot grocery warehouses in England","text":"There are robot grocery warehouses in England: [VIDEO] (5 minutes, Tom Scott video). Lots of Amazon's stock would be easier, but Amazon also has a greater range of sizes."},{"id":35362283,"author":"pen2l","month":"2023-03","story":"What is really going on at Amazon Fresh?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not quite on-topic, but I have to wonder: why go and develop these long-winded solutions when we can come up with obvious better ones? -- order on your phone (which is nice and easy because you can add to your grocery list with a smart home speaker/smartphone list throughout the day) and then it's automatically packaged (in an automated warehouse: [VIDEO] ) and delivered? And for folks who drive to work, have them shipped to their place of work. Places of work make accommodations like have parking for electric cars and cafeterias -- having a place for delivery storage strikes me as an obvious perk that has tremendous benefits both for employers, employees, and the environment."},{"id":33922264,"author":"pdpi","month":"2022-12","story":"Amazon Is Running Out of Warehouse Workers. Cue the Robots","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Or maybe just do both. I do most of my groceries through Ocado, and their warehouse operations are almost fully automated[0], but then on the delivery front I can only assume they staff much more generously than amazon, because their delivery drivers are always much more relaxed than Amazon drivers, they're quite happy to have a chat while unpacking, and often call saying they're running early and ask whether it's ok to arrive before the delivery window. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33546226,"author":"francis-io","month":"2022-11","story":"Why everything looks the same","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"please look them up on Youtube[0]","text":"These days I try to avoid physical shops as much as possible. Here in the UK, we have a \"supermarket\" called Ocado. If you haven't heard of them, please look them up on Youtube[0]. They don't have any physical shops I know of, but have recently started stocking M&S products. They are known in the UK to usually be more expensive, but good quality. It might be the Whole Foods of the UK. They run warehouses with a grid of rails and seem to be on a mission to automate as much of the logistics and delivery of products as possible. The website allows me to set up a regular weekly order, I can edit it up until the evening of the day before to add any last minute items. I think I pay £10 a month for unlimited deliveries over £40. Having someone bring a weekly food shop to your door for £2.50 is a great deal. Not only is this a super easy to get food shopping done each week, I can do it from the comfort of my desk (great for when Retros get a little boring!). It also stops me impulse buying things. I know I already have a December 23rd order coming full of Christmas food and I get to avoid the packed Christmas food shoppers and the depressing Christmas decorations and music in store (I'm a scrooge). I don't want a better shopping experience, I want the best food, at the cheapest prices, delivered to me in a reliable way. I don't want to think about buying food any longer than I have to each week. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29742362,"author":"AussieWog93","month":"2021-12","story":"Boston Dynamics’ Stretch robot can move 800 heavy boxes per hour","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of a Tom Scott video where he checks out the Ocado warehouse in the UK","text":">rethinking the whole process instead yields a much bigger boost. Reminds me of a Tom Scott video where he checks out the Ocado warehouse in the UK: [VIDEO] It's crazy; they've rethought the entire system to have it navigable by machine."},{"id":29746457,"author":"ehnto","month":"2021-12","story":"Boston Dynamics’ Stretch robot can move 800 heavy boxes per hour","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There's a great example of a facility built from the ground up with a robot-first approach in Ocado","text":"There's a great example of a facility built from the ground up with a robot-first approach in Ocado, the grocery packing/delivery company, it looks very different to warehouses that have humans in mind: [VIDEO] It let them do a few novel things I think, most obvious is the dense packing grid, no need for humans to get around the area so they really pack in the functional units. Next is the precision they can pull off without having to worry about wiggle-room or interruptions from people, the robots move very close to eachother which would be a big no-no if you had to worry about people."},{"id":28798752,"author":"SideburnsOfDoom","month":"2021-10","story":"What’s the Point of 15-Minute Grocery Delivery?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I don't think Ocado has a dense enough network of local warehouses. yes, AFAIK, Ocado relies on a few large highly automated warehouses. (1) The main issue seems to be that the level of automation is so high that a fire is not noticed soon enough. This has happened twice. (2) 1) [VIDEO] 2) [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":29052731,"author":"BingoAhoy","month":"2021-10","story":"Pre-industrial workers had a shorter workweek than today's (1991)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Just to showcase some of the automation prototypes that makes me a hopeful believer","text":"I think your vision makes sense. It might not be optimal currently but as A.I., automation, and other technologies advance (Fusion fingers-crossed) I can see it becoming plausible and even desirable over the \"pure-ish\" capitalist economy we currently have. Just to showcase some of the automation prototypes that makes me a hopeful believer: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] 3-d printed homes too"},{"id":28242818,"author":"handmodel","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla Announces a Humanoid Robot “Tesla Bot”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I feel like a lot of factories are going to go in this direction.","text":"[VIDEO] I feel like a lot of factories are going to go in this direction. Lots of complex automation but ultimately very simple mechanisms with a strong framework to make sure nothing goes wrong."},{"id":28243274,"author":"handmodel","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla Announces a Humanoid Robot “Tesla Bot”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've been wrong many times before but still have trouble believing this would be more efficient than just having two separate buildings. Even in places where land is very expensive. How many dark stores would you have to run in a city like Boston to get the same efficiency you could get from a signle warehouse with a similar system to this?: [VIDEO] And if you insist on doing that multi-use concept - it still seems like a robot on wheels and with much different than human arms would be more efficient."},{"id":27746575,"author":"PeterisP","month":"2021-07","story":"How many robots does it take to run a grocery store? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes Xbox Kinect components visible in the production setup.","quote":"the production setup includes Xbox Kinect components","text":"Ha - the production setup includes Xbox Kinect components - right when the employee says \"3d cameras\" at 2:26 [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["LruaD7XhQ50",1]]},"xb_PyKuI7II":{"wy":"Remembered fondly as a fun, memorable animated explanation of how perpendicular disk storage works.","q":{"x":"It's a simple concept to explain, but animation sure makes it fun and lasting.","i":34344870,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48000201,"author":"staticshock","month":"2026-05","story":"What Is Z-Angle Memory and Why Is Intel Developing It?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"That song is etched into my memory and bring back a flood of great feelings.","text":"That song is etched into my memory and bring back a flood of great feelings. Daily Motion is giving me a playback error, but here's a copy from youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34344870,"author":"pcurve","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite illustration in Computer Science?","stance":"rec","why":"Animated explanation of perpendicular disc storage.","quote":"It's a simple concept to explain, but animation sure makes it fun and lasting.","text":"maybe not CS concepts, but wanted to re-share Perpendicular disc storage from Hitachi. [VIDEO] It's a simple concept to explain, but animation sure makes it fun and lasting."},{"id":22883356,"author":"basch","month":"2020-04","story":"Western Digital admits 2TB-6TB WD Red NAS drives use shingled magnetic recording","stance":"reference","why":"Hitachi's Schoolhouse Rock style perpendicular recording commercial.","quote":"Hitachi had such a great thing going with their Schoolhouse Rock meets Dino DNA commercial.","text":"That's what I'm wondering though. Nearly all the WD data center drives are marketed as Ultrastar. Is this marketing, or did the HGST entity supplant WD enterprise during \"integration\" in the last two years? [LINK] It looks like the only surviving product names are Ultrastar and CinemaStar. Nothing is labeled HGST anymore. Most of the HGST Helium disks were SMR, if I'm not mistaken. As of now, given the two HelioSeal series, one of the two is SMR. Side Note: Hitachi had such a great thing going with their Schoolhouse Rock meets Dino DNA commercial. I wish this would have continued. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2298896,"author":"jamesbressi","month":"2011-03","story":"Western digital acquires Hitachi Global Storage Tech for $4.3B","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Maybe you forgot this? Hitachi School House Rock video...","text":"Maybe you forgot this? Hitachi School House Rock video... [VIDEO] \"Get Perpendicular\" Hitachi Global Storage Technologies."},{"id":6861760,"author":"nly","month":"2013-12","story":"The Moore's Law blowout sale is ending","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminded me so much of this","text":"> or if we radically change the fabrication process and turn transistors sideways This reminded me so much of this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uF-GSj-Exms",1],["zDZFcDGpL4U",1]]},"8wZ1v4PIsYI":{"wy":"Cited as a favorite example of clever mechanical design and as a reference for CNC joinery devices.","q":{"x":"i think his youtube channel is really worth checking out.","i":8795622,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8795622,"author":"the_cat_kittles","month":"2014-12","story":"Jenga Pistol","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Matthias Wandel's pantorouter and his problem-solving approach","quote":"i think his youtube channel is really worth checking out.","text":"mattias' approach has really influenced how i think about software engineering. not entirely surprising considering he is also a software engineer. i think his youtube channel is really worth checking out. he has even invented several new, amazing tools, like the \"pantorouter\" for cutting perfect mortise and tennons (among other things) with a router. [VIDEO] the way he solves, and even identifies, the problems in the woodworking domain are, i think, very instructive for how to think about some aspects of software design."},{"id":25354765,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2020-12","story":"Exotic Programming Ideas","stance":"reference","why":"Pantorouter link in a tangential discussion","quote":"","text":"> \" If it's not too much to ask, can you better explain me your reasons? Is $500 a large amount of money for your budget? \" BitCoin has no associations in my mind except distasteful scams, hustle and the seedier side of life. The feeling is that you're trying to convince me to buy into Amway or an MLM scheme or a holiday time share. It wouldn't just be \"wasting $500\" it would be \"being scammed\". Wasting $500 would be mildly annoying, being scammed while watching it happen and thinking \"this is a scam\" and still going along with it would be infuriating . I could spare it. If I lost it, the loss would not inconvenience me. But you are acting as if BitCoin is doing something new here - I can already hold some high-risk/high-return investments, and they are some that I think have a chance of increasing in value for reasons that I understand at least at a high level. For one example, Segway had a very understandable business model: invent and patent a new vehicle, make and sell it. The risk was that nobody would buy, and they didn't. By comparison, I don't understand at any level why BitCoin's price is what it is, and have no confidence it won't collapse suddenly and completely instead of going up. I don't know what it would take to make the price go 50x higher to get any feeling if that's likely or unlikely. I don't know why it would happen to Bitcoin specifically instead of Litecoin, Namecoin, Peercoin, Dogecoin, xRipple, Nxt, Monero, Stellar, Ethereum, etc. Buying a cheap specu"},{"id":19979513,"author":"lqet","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows pantorouter, which works like old mechanical movements","quote":"This is basically how the pantarouter by Matthias Wandel [0] works","text":"This is so cool! [LINK] [LINK] This is basically how the pantarouter by Matthias Wandel [0] works: [LINK] Or take a look at this weird engine: [LINK] I am a bit nostalgic/sad about the fact that many young people who would've wanted to learn this craft a century ago are today choosing software engineering. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17895460,"author":"theothermkn","month":"2018-09","story":"MatchSticks: Portable CNC System for Automating Wood-Working Joinery","stance":"reference","why":"Pantorouter as comparison for a CNC joinery device","quote":"the device itself reads like a CNC Pantorouter","text":"The whole project smells of YouTube woodworking culture. That's not a bad thing! It's just that the device itself reads like a CNC Pantorouter ( [VIDEO] ). They show a table-top workbench. ( [LINK] ) And, the final example is of \"Korean Joinery\" ( [LINK] ). None of this is damning. It just gave me a heck of a sense of deja vu as I watched. I almost expected stop-motion clamps to squeak by, a la Frank Howarth!"},{"id":17698467,"author":"bachbach","month":"2018-08","story":"I’m a very slow thinker (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Pantorouter link in an unrelated discussion","quote":"","text":"I've always worried that it's worse than that. Even if there's plenty of deep thinkers (which is what slow thinking is in my opinion) - there should always be some people with good fortune and circumstances to push at the peripheral. I honestly do not see that today in Silicon Valley - I see a lot of useless bullshit and superficial replacements, there's only a handful of people out there keeping it together. However... poverty of Time is not likely to be the main constraint unless you live in the Middle Ages. Many intellectuals have taken jobs which enable them to kill two birds, merely being physically active and forced to be active by a job that requires it is a genuine cognitive advantage - not to mention the infinite distractions that can smear your available leisure time such that you produce nothing of value. Most aristocrats did not belong to the Royal Society. No - the real problem is that we're too synchronized. We think alike - we have a 'frame problem' of our own. Being here on HN or on the Internet in general is not an advantage. It at first appears to be valuable in the sense that you understand what exists and who is doing what, the context. That is I suspect the opposite of what we need for true innovation. For true innovation you need to connect up with 'the network' once or twice a year - and the rest of the time fuck off and do something good (ideally with a small close knit group). “[..]Although personally, I think cyberspace means the end of our species.\""}],"r":[["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1]]},"bX3EZCVj2XA":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a source for the demoralization and subversion idea, though one commenter dismisses it as alt-right material.","q":{"x":"Everybody should watch this video","i":14770707,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14770707,"author":"cmahler7","month":"2017-07","story":"The Decline of Marriage","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bezmenov interview on ideological subversion","quote":"Everybody should watch this video","text":"People have been brainwashed to hate their own culture, demorilization is the first step of ideological subversion. Western Civilization might not be perfect but there's nothing else in human history that has come close to it. Everybody should watch this video [VIDEO]"},{"id":42038484,"author":"ranger_danger","month":"2024-11","story":"Why Facts Don't Change Our Minds","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as relevant to why facts do not change minds","quote":"Also relevant","text":"\"As a rule, strong feelings about issues do not emerge from deep understanding.\" Mind = blown. Also relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35206098,"author":"recuter","month":"2023-03","story":"Costs of the U.S.-Led War in Iraq Since 2003","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on demoralization as a tactic","quote":"","text":"I have noticed a trend of \"America bad\" on this website. There is certainly a lot to criticize about this particular boondoggle, or indeed, any war. But taking notes from obvious Sino and Russophiles is not productive. On false equivalency: [LINK] On demoralization: [VIDEO] Dang, I suggest you close the thread, cheers."},{"id":35360195,"author":"tenpies","month":"2023-03","story":"The Teen Mental Illness Epidemic Is International, Part 1: The Anglosphere","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as original interview on ideological subversion","quote":"","text":"Also falls perfectly within what Yuri Bezmenov (the KGB defector who explained the Soviet's plan to take down the West through ideological subversion) laid out. At around that time, you would have the first batch of fully indoctrinated (trained by Socialists from childhood to adulthood, with no oversight or counterbalance) graduating. That is also the group that sets the stage for the demoralisation phase of the plan and allows for the next phase: destabilisation. Good summary here: [LINK] And the original interview: [VIDEO] I don't think this is purely a Soviet ploy working out, but you can't help but look at the Western education system and realise that's a big part of the problem."},{"id":27712064,"author":"baybal2","month":"2021-07","story":"Media blackout after key witness against Assange admits lying","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the demoralisation tactic","quote":"","text":"> I'm not sure you have to keep pointing to the boogie man of soviet infiltrators to explain power hungry bureaucrats. Those have existed in literally every society since at least the early bronze age. No, I'd say power hunger is one thing, but what you face now is unique to this poisonous \"Soviet Culture.\" A very special, aggressive brand of social nihilism at the apex of civil society, characteristic of one party/social class governments. Putin put his own spin on the timeless demoralisation tactic ( [VIDEO] ,) an even more aggressive targeting of both the new elites, and the establishment (see Biden Jr. adventures with in Ukraine.) Or plainly speaking playing both sides."},{"id":26313523,"author":"nix23","month":"2021-03","story":"Gab has been hacked and 70GB of data leaked","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for the demoralization claim","quote":"","text":"Isn't it beautiful how the American and western society's rips itself apart? Man vs Women, Woman vs Trans, Gay vs Lesbians, White vs Black..oh sorry i mean People of no Color vs People of Color, Democrats vs Republicans and so on...zero consensus on each side what our society should be. It's like the KGB wins the cold war after all...with a small delay. Yuri Bezmenov: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18918930,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-01","story":"Brexit Deal Fails in Parliament","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on Soviet subversion of education","quote":"","text":"Yes, the KGB defector Yuri [1] said as much. He describes a slow distruption they started of America by infiltrating the education system. This attacks the weakness of a democracy, which is the fact that it’s run by people for the behest of itself as a system and does not answer to an estate that would otherwise check its malfunctioning. However, that doesn’t mean the movements of dissent is caused by this. They could be, or they could be a symptom of the problems not solved by a long term malfunctioning Democratic system. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18918950,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-01","story":"Brexit Deal Fails in Parliament","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on manipulation of political movements","quote":"","text":"How do you know that other more major political stories is not the real product of manipulation [1] and these opposition movements are a symptom of problems not solved by a malfunctioning Democratic system? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18920466,"author":"asabjorn","month":"2019-01","story":"Brexit Deal Fails in Parliament","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source of 1984 interview on subversion","quote":"","text":"As I said elsewhere: Yes, the KGB defector Yuri [1] in 1984 said as much that there is manipulation. He describes a slow distruption they started of America by infiltrating the education system. This attacks the weakness of a democracy, which is the fact that it’s run by people for the behest of itself as a system and does not answer to an estate that would otherwise check its malfunctioning. However, that doesn’t mean the movements of dissent is caused by this. They could be, or they could be a symptom of the problems not solved by a long term malfunctioning Democratic system. And the malfunctioning could be some other more major political ideology. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20660284,"author":"hackermailman","month":"2019-08","story":"The coming automation of propaganda","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is pretty standard 'alt-right' materials","text":"The right wing has their own version of this, a KGB defector Yuri Bezmenov, who has powerful (meme) videos about the coming destruction of America and how it would take 3 decades to fulfill, all recorded in the early 80s. 'To change the perception of reality of every American, despite their abundence of information, nobody is able to come to sensible conclusions,... it's a great brainwashing process that takes decades' [VIDEO] Yuri also claims the Soviet Union never fell, it just reinvented itself. This is pretty standard 'alt-right' materials that people should pay attention to if they wish to understand that reactionary wing of politics"}]},"wg6cfsnmqyg":{"wy":"HN commenters point to Barkley's talks as a respected clinician's view of ADHD as a spectrum of executive function problems.","q":{"x":"I'd highly recommend listening to Dr. Russell Barkley talk about ADHD on this playlist","i":22921277,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22921277,"author":"dvcrn","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: How do I overcome mental laziness?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Barkley's playlist describes ADHD as spectrum of symptoms","quote":"I'd highly recommend listening to Dr. Russell Barkley talk about ADHD on this playlist","text":"When in doubt always best to talk to a doc. Best case you are completely fine, but doesn't hurt checking. (The irony is that people with ADHD are probably the least likely to get up by themselves and make an appointment given that there are no consequences of not doing it lol) ADHD is of course not just motivation but a spectrum of symptoms. I'd highly recommend listening to Dr. Russell Barkley talk about ADHD on this playlist - [VIDEO] Watch from 1B onwards and see if you feel like it describes you"},{"id":29679785,"author":"artdigital","month":"2021-12","story":"ADHD Accommodations Guide","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Inattentiveness is only a very small part of ADHD (and the name ADHD itself is very outdated and doesn't capture at all what this is about). It's more common in children to be inattentiveness (ADHD-PI) but goes away with adulthood. But ADHD is also a spectrum, so some people have it worse in X but very mild (or not at all) in Y. For me it's more emotional restless-ness, overthinking (esp. what people think about me and self-criticism), oversharing of whatever I am currently focused on (like writing this comment), being late because I constantly forget to account for something obvious like walking time, and a strong \"mental barrier\" of doing things that I don't want to do, or don't have consequences for not doing. Kind of like a self-drive that only exists when a) I really really want to do it, but then I have to do it right now because I can't focus on anything else. I want to drop everything I'm doing and only do that instead. b) there is some external consequence like a deadline driving me to do it. For example: My apartment has a stack of unopened amazon boxes that I only open when I need the item inside (no consequence of not doing it). But when a task has a deadline, or when I feel that my not-doing something would start to get people angry at me, I'll hyperfocus and rush it out in no time. Or dishes starting to get smelly (consequence), but a pile of washed clothes stays untouched (no consequence) until I need a shirt from that pile. When I was a child (untreated) I bar"},{"id":14297535,"author":"rtpg","month":"2017-05","story":"There is little research into the long-term effects of using antidepressants","stance":"reference","why":"Barkley talks on ADHD misnaming","quote":"Dr Charles Barkley [0] has a decent amount of talks on youtube about this misnaming","text":"Yeah, it's pretty interesting how many ADD/ADHD meds have completely different effects for those with or without the disorder. As I've read more into this, I've found that \"better\" indicators of the disorder is more around impulsive behavior (lack of concentration being such a difficult thing to quantify, nobody's concentrated!). Dr Charles Barkley [0] has a decent amount of talks on youtube about this misnaming (and other aspects like hereditary natures). Depending on where along the ASD spectrum your daughter might be, this might be harder to figure out though. I can understand being a bit skeptical about this. I hope you can work out what's best for your daughter (and hopefully keep her engaged and aware of what's out there). [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"2PCb9mnrU1g":{"wy":"HN refers to it as a chance to hear Shkreli's own side of the drug pricing story, including his claim that it is common practice.","q":{"x":"It was nice to see his side of the story.","i":14931071,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14931071,"author":"Clubber","month":"2017-08","story":"Martin Shkreli is found guilty of securities fraud","stance":"rec","why":"Shkreli's side of the story on price increases","quote":"It was nice to see his side of the story.","text":"I use to hate this guy, then I saw the Vice interview on him. It was nice to see his side of the story. I don't know much about this case in particular. It was kinda sleazy to see the congress question him about his price increases when they knew damn well it was perfectly legal and they haven't done anything to stop it. Shkreli seems to be trying to expose this hypocrisy, but the news loves their stories. Vice interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28971032,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2021-10","story":"Federal judges or their brokers traded stocks of litigants during cases","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on Shkreli's drug pricing revelations","quote":"","text":"You've got it backwards. Shkreli served time because of public opinion and the things he revealed about the medicare drug pricing [1,2,3] Shkreli's case shows the partiality, politics and bias embedded in our justice system. He was unable to even serve the remainder of his sentence under house arrest as very reasonable covid safety precautions despite his crime being entirely nonviolent. The justice system is in many ways the government's instrument of vengeance. You can clearly see this with O.J. Simpson's 33 year sentence in 2008 for armed robbery. Essentially, a new fluff case was used to dole out an improportionate sentence. as retribution for the murder of Nicole Brown and Ron Goldman. [4,5] The truth is that Shrkeli pissed off a lot of people in powerful places. None of the investors wanted him tried because he ended up making them all a lot of money. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":15830486,"author":"wpdev_63","month":"2017-12","story":"DOJ: We Want Martin Shkreli’s Enigma Machine, Wu-Tang Album, and $7.3M","stance":"reference","why":"Shkreli says price hikes are common practice","quote":"In an interview with Vice he goes over the fact that what he did is common practice[0].","text":"Whatever he is going to be convicted of, they're going to throw the book at him. He's a terrible person and an opportunist. The problem is he is not the only one like this. In an interview with Vice he goes over the fact that what he did is common practice[0]. Until there is some way to stop this price gauging there will be more people like him. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":11558714,"author":"rboyd","month":"2016-04","story":"For What It’s Worth: A Review of Wu-Tang Clan’s “Once Upon a Time in Shaolin”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's basically exactly what happened with Allie Conti in his Vice interview ( [VIDEO] ). Only it was red wine and not tea."},{"id":11354279,"author":"patrickk","month":"2016-03","story":"Startups that demoed at Y Combinator W16 Demo Day 2","stance":"reference","why":"Shkreli interview inspired a startup idea about buying approved drugs","quote":"I got this idea after watching the Vice interview with Martin Shkreli","text":"Perhaps it would be possible to attack this market using a different approach. The classic startup path is approximately --> geeks hammer out MVP product, get some funding and try to grow as quickly as possible, and either go big or fail. I got this idea after watching the Vice interview with Martin Shkreli (obviously not a role model)[1] Shkreli made the leap from hedge funds (betting against pharma companies) into becoming the CEO of a pharma company, which apparently does research in addition jacking up the prices of HIV drugs (Shkreli actually gives out chemistry lessons on youtube[2]). So maybe some geeks could try getting funding, buy up some underappreciated, undervalued but already FDA approved drugs, and using the cashflow to do further research, or develop cool new products or services in the healthcare space instead of the traditional, risky route. (I'm not advocating jacking up the prices of critical lifesaving drugs obviously). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["0UjpmT5noto",1]]},"5S7Bb0Qg-oE":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on Roman food and fish sauce as a hands-on demonstration of making garum.","q":{"x":"Worth a watch if you’re into cooking, or history, or both.","i":27312258,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27312258,"author":"el_benhameen","month":"2021-05","story":"Stuffed Dormouse and Fish Gut Sauce: The Flavors of Pompeii (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Channel cooking dishes from antiquity, including garum","quote":"Worth a watch if you’re into cooking, or history, or both.","text":"There’s a fellow on youtube who makes dishes from antiquity, including garum. Worth a watch if you’re into cooking, or history, or both. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27938601,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2021-07","story":"What did the ancient Romans eat?","stance":"reference","why":"Claims to know how to make garum from Byzantine sources","quote":"Max Miller on youtube says he knows how to make Garum","text":"Max Miller on youtube says he knows how to make Garum - the fish sauce that is often referenced. [VIDEO] Now sure if that is genuine, i previously read that the recipe for this stuff has been lost (but the Author says his receipe is based on Byzantine sources) More about the author here: [LINK] it says his historical cookbook will be published next year, in 2022."},{"id":23980732,"author":"eindiran","month":"2020-07","story":"Did fish sauce in Vietnam come from ancient Rome via the Silk Road?","stance":"reference","why":"Makes garum from a modernized surviving Roman recipe","quote":"This video covers how you can make garum using a modernized version of a surviving recipe from the Geoponica","text":"This video covers how you can make garum using a modernized version of a surviving recipe from the Geoponica: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23986348,"author":"virtue3","month":"2020-07","story":"Did fish sauce in Vietnam come from ancient Rome via the Silk Road?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows garum smells awful, so unlikely discovered casually","quote":"It smells like death, so it would be unlikely anyone would necessarily want to eat it in the first place","text":"I mean... sure, programming is just putting together 0 + 1 and calling it a day too. I think you're severely downplaying how unlikely it is that this was just \"done\". It smells like death, so it would be unlikely anyone would necessarily want to eat it in the first place: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxPdPpi5W4o",1]]},"Ae9EKCyI1xU":{"wy":"Cited as a fun, unconventional example of neural networks built from floating point error.","q":{"x":"A fun example of that (making a neural network using floating point error as a source of non-linearity)","i":40466099,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40466099,"author":"jcparkyn","month":"2024-05","story":"Your Transformer is Secretly Linear","stance":"rec","why":"Neural network using floating point error as source of non-linearity","quote":"A fun example of that (making a neural network using floating point error as a source of non-linearity)","text":"A fun example of that (making a neural network using floating point error as a source of non-linearity): [VIDEO]"},{"id":41736189,"author":"jcparkyn","month":"2024-10","story":"Were RNNs all we needed?","stance":"reference","why":"Silly example of an unconventional activation function","quote":"or for a sillier example","text":"> squash an output into a range This isn't the primary purpose of the activation function, and in fact it's not even necessary. For example see ReLU (probably the most common activation function), leaky ReLU, or for a sillier example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37844078,"author":"Uehreka","month":"2023-10","story":"\"We'll call it AI to sell it, Machine Learning to Build it\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don’t get what people are trying to say when they say these kinds of things about AI. That human-level writing is as simple as a linear regression? That we could’ve had computer programs capable of human-level writing decades ago? Have they not used these AIs enough to see how powerful they are? Are they seeing the bad outits and thinking that AIs are always doing that poorly? Like seriously, if you’re telling me that it was obvious that a “linear regression” could pass the LSAT I’ve got a macvlan to sell you. Edit: they’re also literally not linear regression! [VIDEO]"},{"id":35981257,"author":"superkuh","month":"2023-05","story":"Numbers every LLM Developer should know","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"as a joke, they should implement GPU fluint8 and get baked in non-linearity for the activation function","text":"Cool stuff. I looked at [LINK] and I noticed that that the fp8 support is only for the tensor cores and not the CUDA side. Does that mean training with H100 GPU in fp8 mode would use some software ecosystem that's not the existing vast existing CUDA one? Or am I just misunderstanding CUDA cores vs tensor cores? PS, as a joke, they should implement GPU fluint8 and get baked in non-linearity for the activation function without even using a non-linear function, [VIDEO] (\"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines\" by suckerpinch)"}]},"Bo3lUw9GUJA":{"wy":"Shared as a nostalgic tour of SGI hardware for people who remember that era.","q":{"x":"Fun vid that might bring back some memories :)","i":22004054,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22004054,"author":"amatecha","month":"2020-01","story":"BeOS: The Alternate Universe's Mac OS X","stance":"rec","why":"Fun nostalgic tour of SGI Onyx hardware.","quote":"Fun vid that might bring back some memories :)","text":"Fun vid that might bring back some memories :) [VIDEO] \"SGI's $250,000 Graphics Supercomputer from 1993 - Silicon Graphics Onyx RealityEngine²\""},{"id":39945052,"author":"latchkey","month":"2024-04","story":"The Rise and Fall of Silicon Graphics","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the SGI Onyx the commenter once owned.","quote":"Oh yea, found a video...","text":"Back in 1993, I was in college and working for the extended education department running their all their computer infrastructure. One day, someone wheeled this approx. 3x3 foot sized box to my door and asked me if I wanted it. It was a SGI Onyx with a giant monitor sitting on top, with a keyboard and mouse. I plugged it in and it sounded like an airplane taking off. It immediately heated up my entire tiny office. It was the 4th Unix I had ever played with (Ultrix, NeXT and A/UX were previous ones). It had some cool games on it, but beyond that, at the time, I had no use for it because A/UX on my Quadra950, was so much more fun to play with. I don't even think I ever opened it up to look at it. I don't know what I was thinking. lol. After realizing it did not have much going for it, I ended up just turning it on when the office was cold and using it as a foot rest. Oh yea, found a video... [VIDEO]"},{"id":23733049,"author":"pkphilip","month":"2020-07","story":"MaXX Interactive Desktop: A Re-Implementation of the IRIX Interactive Desktop","stance":"reference","why":"Shows SGI workstations in action.","quote":"","text":"SGI is Silicon Graphics - they were the pioneers in 3D animation especially for the movies, research etc. Jurassic Park was entirely rendered in SGI workstations. These workstations were incredibly powerful and extremely expensive (think > $250,000 for the higher end gear in the 1990s) but pretty amazing. Also, for the time it had very advanced user interfaces. SGI Irix was the name of the OS which ran on these machines. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17895922,"author":"jaysonelliot","month":"2018-09","story":"SGI's $250,000 Graphics Supercomputer from 1993 – Onyx RealityEngine² [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Contains a nostalgic VRML demo at 18 minutes.","quote":"The VRML demo at 18 minutes in really took me back","text":"The VRML demo at 18 minutes in really took me back: [VIDEO] I remember how convinced some people were that VRML was going to be the future of the Web. 3D graphics in your browser, all coded with a simple markup language! How could it fail? Ultimately it was just too complicated for the average web surfer to navigate, especially in the '90s. For a while, though, it really looked like the visions of virtual worlds that cyberpunk fiction loved might show up in your Netscape browser."}],"r":[["RaI_TuISSJE",1],["Lg_aUOSLuRo",1]]},"GctxvSPIfr8":{"wy":"HN points to it, including Gjengset himself, as a more digestible way into partially-stateful dataflow than the thesis text, mostly in caching and database threads.","q":{"x":"a shorter talk from a few years earlier at","i":33613911,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33613911,"author":"Jonhoo","month":"2022-11","story":"How PlanetScale Boost serves SQL queries faster","stance":"rec","why":"Author's thesis defense on partially-stateful dataflow, easier than the thesis","quote":"A recording of my thesis defense on this, which may be more digestible than the thesis itself, is also online at","text":":wave: Author of the paper this work is based on here. I'm so excited to see dynamic, partially-stateful data-flow for incremental materialized view maintenance becoming more wide-spread! I continue to think it's a _great_ idea, and the speed-ups (and complexity reduction) it can yield are pretty immense, so seeing more folks building on the idea makes me very happy. The PlanetScale blog post references my original \"Noria\" OSDI paper ( [LINK] ), but I'd actually recommend my PhD thesis instead ( [LINK] ), as it goes much deeper about some of the technical challenges and solutions involved. It also has a chapter (Appendix A) that covers how it all works by analogy, which the less-technical among the audience may appreciate :) A recording of my thesis defense on this, which may be more digestible than the thesis itself, is also online at [VIDEO] , as well as a shorter talk from a few years earlier at [LINK] . And the Noria research prototype (written in Rust) is on GitHub: [LINK] . As others have already mentioned in the comments, I co-founded ReadySet ( [LINK] ) shortly after graduating specifically to build off of Noria, and they're doing amazing work to provide these kinds of speed-ups for general-purpose relational databases. If you're using one of those, it's worth giving ReadySet a look to get these kinds of speedups there! It's also source-available @ [LINK] if you're curious."},{"id":39454023,"author":"dpcx","month":"2024-02","story":"Readyset: A MySQL and Postgres wire-compatible caching layer","stance":"reference","why":"Talk where Gjengset explains the underlying concept","quote":"Here's[1] a video where he talks about the concept","text":"Pretty sure that this is the database that Jon Gjengset[0] was working on as part of his thesis project. There have been several videos shared by him during talks about the system. It's a really interesting concept. edit: Here's[1] a video where he talks about the concept [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24872683,"author":"kondu","month":"2020-10","story":"Differential Dataflow","stance":"reference","why":"PhD thesis defense on Noria's partial state","quote":"","text":"this is also one of the main ideas behind Noria[1] (built by @jonhoo, who also gave his PhD dissertation[2] on this recently) [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"GwzN5YwMzv0":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as a clear explanation of the block universe and relativity of simultaneity.","q":{"x":"This video by Sabine Hossenfelder explains it nicely","i":36702885,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36702885,"author":"glennonymous","month":"2023-07","story":"Telling the Bees","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of the Block Universe","quote":"This video by Sabine Hossenfelder explains it nicely","text":"I haven’t heard anyone express this before, but when it comes to my death and the deaths of others, I take comfort in the idea of the Block Universe. On the Block Universe concept, time isn’t real in the way we think about it—everything that ever happened or will happen exists in a timeless, eternal block of spacetime. (This video by Sabine Hossenfelder explains it nicely: [VIDEO] .) If this is true, then our lives are eternal—not in the way envisioned by religious people, but eternal in a very real sense. It’s true that I am finite in time in exactly the same way that I’m finite in space, but just as I don’t lament the fact that my body isn’t infinitely large, I shouldn’t lament the fact that my life doesn’t extend through all of time. (I wouldn’t mind living for a lot longer , but that’s a different matter :-D.) I realize this conception of immortality will be too abstract to provide any consolation to many people. But ideas of an afterlife or reincarnation are abstract, too. This one at least has the advantage, to my mind, of being plausible. It’s not that I once was not, then I was, and after I die I will be no more. My life, my presence, this experience—it’s eternal and ever-present."},{"id":35198056,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2023-03","story":"A Growing Number of Scientists Are Convinced the Future Influences the Past","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses what the past is","quote":"discusses this a bit.","text":"To be fair there is some disagreement over what exactly \"the past\" _is_. [VIDEO] discusses this a bit."},{"id":32211965,"author":"ffwd","month":"2022-07","story":"Does the Past Still Exist?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains relativity of simultaneity and the nature of now","quote":"","text":"I feel like I must be missing something but in the part about the relativity of simultaneity [0] I feel like this is more about the relativity of the observers and the limitations of speed of light than it is about the ontology of time itself? The definition of \"now\" as when the light hits the mirror on each side is a \"constructed\" now, a false now if you may. It takes the light longer on the right side than the left side because she is moving through space, all that tells me is that there is no way to communicate a \"now\" between different observers because there can't be instantaneous communication between observers, not that time itself is relative or physical stuff itself doesn't have a constant time? She says in the video that we have to find a way to operationalize how to find a now, to measure it empirically but maybe that's not possible without running a complete simulation after the fact. But to me it seems not surprising that without instantaneous communication there is no way to create a \"now\" empirically but that doesn't mean it extends to the ontology of physical stuff itself or time itself. There would still be a time in there (you should be able to see it in a simulation?) Also for example, if you use sound instead of light, if I'm far away from a sound source (farther than the speed of sound), and another observer is closer to it, we would not be able to use the sound source as a source \"now\", because I would hear it later. Is this the same type of situation or"}]},"HjHMoNGqQTI":{"wy":"Mostly used as a critical source on how popular science channels may be shaped by billionaire funding.","q":{"x":"The following video is a good start","i":38402648,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38402648,"author":"brandnewideas","month":"2023-11","story":"YouTube is now blocking Ad Blockers – So I just make ads run 16x faster","stance":"rec","why":"Good start on billionaire propaganda methods in popular videos","quote":"The following video is a good start","text":"Propaganda for billionaires and the various methodologies they use to control and slowly reshape society. The following video is a good start: [VIDEO] The optimism you mention in your comment is key. Conditioning the public to perceive and blindly accept the ideas and procedures commonly discussed in many of their videos as something net-positive seems to be the primary goal. The art-style I think plays a very well-defined role in that: yes it's bland, but it's the most unlikely one to offend any one average viewer, irrespective of nationality or culture. It's just cute 2D flat birds after all."},{"id":35849597,"author":"oska","month":"2023-05","story":"How much do you “really” earn creating content on YouTube?","stance":"reference","why":"Unwatched but interesting related video on Kurzgesagt criticism","quote":"which I haven't watched but also look interesting","text":"(Can't edit my comment above now so adding on by reply) My observation above was just from personal experience. But after making it I searched 'Kurzgesagt' on YouTube (just wanted to see what videos they'd recently produced) and, as well as their recent videos, that search also turned up one called Kurzgesagt: billionaire propaganda, trusting science, and effective altruism [1] which I've now watched and found that my observation has been made by others and analysed in quite some detail. That video mentioned some others on the same subject [2-3], which I haven't watched but also look interesting. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":35807138,"author":"RoyGBivCap","month":"2023-05","story":"MSFT is forcing Outlook and Teams to open links in Edge and IT admins are angry","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Zoomers these days seem to think Bill is a benevolent do-gooder due to Gates foundation propaganda","text":"Zoomers these days seem to think Bill is a benevolent do-gooder due to Gates foundation propaganda: [VIDEO] Those of us who were computer nerds in the '90s know he and Microsoft were more akin to the Borg. Slashdot always used to feature Bill as Locutus on stories about him/Microsoft. This is completely on brand for microsoft. The \"reform\" is entirely made up. He's the same oligarch he always was, and so are they."},{"id":34053793,"author":"xenotize","month":"2022-12","story":"Discovery identifies Australia as birthplace of all modern mammals","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This video might give you some insight on why they use the research that they use","text":"This video might give you some insight on why they use the research that they use, or rather, the lack of research they use. [VIDEO]"}]},"KBMU6l6GsdM":{"wy":"HN cites it as striking evidence of long-standing kinetic interceptor and hover-control technology.","q":{"x":"the MKV thing is so rad","i":38237478,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38237478,"author":"ge96","month":"2023-11","story":"SpaceX will launch the X-37B on a Falcon Heavy rocket Dec. 7","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive kill vehicle hover demonstration.","quote":"the MKV thing is so rad","text":"Semi tangent, the MKV thing is so rad, I don't understand how it kills ballistic missiles but yeah -- I thought this thing was supposed to operate in space. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47507690,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2026-03","story":"Missile Defense Is NP-Complete","stance":"reference","why":"Interceptor test footage cited in argument about precision physics.","quote":"","text":"[LINK] This is what we had to build in the 60s to allow a missile to know where it physically existed precisely enough to allow it to 50% of the time hit within a circle of ~50 meters. When you get to certain points in physics, certain energy regimes, you no longer are building machines or tools or something mass market. You are building artisan scientific instruments, and then sometimes gluing explosives to them. Even modern laser ring gyros do not even share a dinner table with the precision and accuracy of the above singular component of the Peacekeeper ICBMs, and that was a long time ago. \"Tech\" and some of the developments of the past few decades have really confused people. The miniaturization of the transistor, and building billions of transistors on a small slice of silicon is an aberration, an anomaly. Most things don't get \"Better and better and cheaper and cheaper\" like that because shit just doesn't scale infinitely and in general materials science isn't that precise. For these ground based midcourse interceptors, they have to precisely loft the interceptor package at an incoming projectile. They have to shoot a bullet with a bullet, except the target bullet might even be moving around a bit, and your ability to precisely quantify the exact parameters of it's position and velocity is already limited. Is your position and velocity measurement an inch off? Two inches? Is that too much? How well have you quantified the thrust of your rocket engine? THIS specific rock"},{"id":40354607,"author":"datadrivenangel","month":"2024-05","story":"Disney's robots use rockets to stick the landing","stance":"reference","why":"Rocket-hover interceptor test, simple physics with impressive engineering.","quote":"simple physics, with impressive engineering implementation","text":"Reminds me of the MKV-L ballistic missile interceptor tests: simple physics, with impressive engineering implementation [0]. 0 - [VIDEO] (the rapid fire rockets are a little loud)"},{"id":37139859,"author":"freitzkriesler2","month":"2023-08","story":"China Stops Publishing Youth Unemployment Numbers Amid Faltering Economy","stance":"reference","why":"Lockheed multiple kill vehicle test from 2008.","quote":"Here's a video of a mkv defense tool made by Lockheed back in 2008","text":"Nukes these days are about strategic destruction of economic assets, not blanket salting the earth. MAD was only really a problem in the 60s and 70s when delivery systems were fuzzy and they were going for a wide berth. A nuclear exchange today would be much more precise. Not a complete volley to glass everything. Not to mention, anti missile tech has been black tech for years. Here's a video of a mkv defense tool made by Lockheed back in 2008 [VIDEO] This is the stuff they show off. Imagine the stuff they haven't shown yet. I am not worried at all."},{"id":34888390,"author":"ActorNightly","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: So what IS the end game for any WW3 scenario?","stance":"reference","why":"Older kinetic kill vehicle test as evidence of missile defense tech.","quote":"There was also stuff like this 14 years ago","text":"This is my personal belief, which I have no way to substantiate, and solely statistical, but it helps me sleep at night or so to speak. Nobody spends more on defense than US. Also, the new technology that you hear about today is essentially declassified tech from ~20 years ago. I remember reading in the past about Boeing doing experiments with a 747 carrying a giant laser that could lock on and fry ICBMs. There was also stuff like this 14 years ago: [VIDEO] Extrapolating to today, I firmly believe that US (and probably NATO and other countries who share intel) are watching Russian launch sites closely, ready to drop kinetic warheads from orbit at the first sign of activation, not to mention multiple layers of defense with kinetic kill vehicles, lasers, EMPS, jammers, e.t.c. That is assuming, we don't have stuxnet like malware hiding in computer systems that would just magically cause the missiles to fail. After all, Russian military was shown to be wildly incompetent (which US probably already knew), so that translates to easy infiltration/intel and creation of a safeguard plan to counter anything they have."},{"id":27767049,"author":"jcims","month":"2021-07","story":"The James Webb Space Telescope has passed the final mission analysis review","stance":"reference","why":"Ground hover test of a missile defense kill vehicle.","quote":"they tested missile defense 'kill vehicles' on the ground","text":"Sure the final mission configuration isn't exactly what they would test, but the differences in the sky crane maneuver on Mars and Earth would be relatively easy to factor in (ok, that might be an exaggeration, but its doable). There is a ton of control development that would need to be sorted out so they knew how to integrate it all. For example, I had heard that the primary indicator that the rover had touched down simply watching the throttle on the closed loop flight control system. When it throttled down it meant the rockets were no longer suspending the weight of the rover. If true, I would think you would want to test that quite a bit... FWIW they tested missile defense 'kill vehicles' on the ground and they will operate at or near orbital velocity in space. The main 'hover thruster' would likely be completely unnecessary in a live exercise: [VIDEO]"}]},"LZAeZBVAzVw":{"wy":"It is the go-to primary source whenever HN discusses posits versus IEEE floats, valued for Kahan's sharp critique despite the poor audio.","q":{"x":"Audio quality is not great but there are links to slides from Gustafson and Kahan","i":13991860,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13991860,"author":"celias","month":"2017-03","story":"Beyond Floating Point: Next-Generation Computer Arithmetic [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Debate between Gustafson and Kahan on unums versus floats","quote":"Audio quality is not great but there are links to slides from Gustafson and Kahan","text":"The Great Debate @ARITH23: John Gustafson and William Kahan [VIDEO] Audio quality is not great but there are links to slides from Gustafson and Kahan"},{"id":35589099,"author":"porkbrain","month":"2023-04","story":"Aztec monks with 1/55 HP no longer die when picking up or dropping a relic","stance":"reference","why":"Kahan explains disadvantages of interval math in debate","quote":"Kahan points out disadvantages of interval maths.","text":"A related discussion about number types: The Great Debate @ARITH23: John Gustafson and William Kahan [VIDEO] Kahan points out disadvantages of interval maths."},{"id":34405557,"author":"SideQuark","month":"2023-01","story":"Will Floating Point 8 Solve AI/ML Overhead?","stance":"reference","why":"Debate over posits cited as further reading","quote":"See also and","text":"Original posits are variable width, making them nearly useless for high performance parallel computations. Later versions don't add anything of use for low precision neural networks, and lack of hardware support anywhere make them too slow for anything other than toying around. See also [LINK] and [VIDEO]"},{"id":33006674,"author":"zasdffaa","month":"2022-09","story":"Posits, a New Kind of Number, Improves the Math of AI","stance":"reference","why":"Kahan criticizing Gustafson's unum work in a hostile debate","quote":"Kahan goes in like a wolf onto a lamb, much to gustafson's shock and hurt.","text":"I did some reading on unum a while back, inc. Kahan's criticisms. There's another side. There's a vid on youtube with Kahan and Gustafson talking over it. Kahan goes in like a wolf onto a lamb, much to gustafson's shock and hurt. Gustafson points out some of Kahan's criticisms are plain wrong, even citing the page in the book, then says something like \"we should be working together on this, why aren't we working together?\" Kahan doesn't respond. Kahan may be right or wrong but his attitude is weirdly hostile, and Gustafson is no n00b about floats. Edit: I think this is it (but it's been a while) [VIDEO]"}]},"PiGkzgfR_c0":{"wy":"HN points to it as a real example of certified emergency autoland, used in debates about autonomy and pilotless aircraft.","q":{"x":"If it activates, the plane is squawking emergency to ATC","i":41894817,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":21408303,"author":"cyberferret","month":"2019-10","story":"Piper Announces First GA Aircraft with Autoland Capability","stance":"rec","why":"Cirrus emergency autoland: one button reroutes and lands the aircraft.","quote":"Looks pretty impressive.","text":"Maybe a coincidence, but Cirrus Aircraft announced their SafeReturn Emergency Autoland on their Vision Jet series today as well [0]. Looks pretty impressive. A passenger can just press one button on the roof behind the pilot, and the aircraft will send a distress signal, re-route and autoland the aircraft at the nearest airport (including terrain and weather avoidance) and also convert all screens on board to inform the passengers how to use the radio for comms etc. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41894817,"author":"Animats","month":"2024-10","story":"US probes Tesla's Full Self-Driving software after fatal crash","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Safe Return squawking emergency and clearing runway.","quote":"If it activates, the plane is squawking emergency to ATC","text":"Ah. Few people realize how dumb aircraft autopilots really are. Even the fanciest ones just follow a series of waypoints. There is one exception - Garmin Safe Return. That's strictly an emergency system. If it activates, the plane is squawking emergency to ATC and and demanding that airspace and a runway be cleared for it.[1] This has been available since 2019 and does not seem to have yet been activated in an emergency. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38765225,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2023-12","story":"A cargo plane flew 50 miles with no pilot onboard using a semi-automated system","stance":"reference","why":"Cirrus Safe Return as model for airliner pilot-of-last-resort.","quote":"","text":"Also install in commercial passenger aircraft and enable to be pilot of last resort if onboard human pilots become incapacitated and a cryptographically verified command is sent to the aircraft (airline? ATC? TBD who should have this authority). Very similar to Cirrus Safe Return Autoland, but with additional safeguards (remote command from an approved authority) vs passenger command. > Once activated by the touch of a button, Safe Return assumes control of the aircraft and transforms the Vision Jet into an autonomous vehicle that navigates to the nearest suitable airport for landing, communicates with air traffic control, lands and brings the aircraft safely to a complete stop, allowing passengers to exit the aircraft. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XVbuk3jizGM",1]]},"ZN4njIQcSR4":{"wy":"HN cites it in prediction-market threads as an accessible critical overview, including the Polymarket CEO's remarks on insider trading.","q":{"x":"well worth checking out","i":47878507,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47878507,"author":"w-m","month":"2026-04","story":"'Hairdryer used to trick weather sensor' to win $34,000 Polymarket bet","stance":"rec","why":"Oliver segment on prediction market insider trading and manipulation.","quote":"well worth checking out","text":"John Oliver had a segment on prediction markets this week. It covers insider training and opportunities for blatant manipulation like this, well worth checking out. The example in the Last Week Tonight segment was betting on dildos being thrown on court during a WNBA match. And then travelling to the match to throw the dildo. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49038882,"author":"MikeTheGreat","month":"2026-07","story":"What just happened to TheNumbers.com should worry us all","stance":"reference","why":"Polymarket CEO remarks on insider trading as a feature.","quote":"the CEO of PolyMarket has said that insider trading is part of the point of PolyMarket","text":"Genuine question: do prediction markets open up a new revenue stream for TheNumbers.com? Specifically: they suspect that the motivation for trying to hack their site (for at least some people) is that they wanted early access to numbers that folks were betting on in prediction markets. Since they've got those numbers they can just bet on them, then benefit from their perfect knowledge. On the one hand this does seem incredibly unethical (it's clearly insider trading). On the other hand the CEO of PolyMarket has said that insider trading is part of the point of PolyMarket: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48144758,"author":"otterley","month":"2026-05","story":"NPR finds \"no sign\" of Polymarket at its Panama HQ address","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver segment on prediction markets cited in gambling debate.","quote":"","text":"The social costs of gambling, particularly compulsive gambling, cannot be so easily dismissed. I would encourage you to visit your local Gamblers’ Anonymous group or seek out addicts’ stories of loss and despair to get a fuller picture of it. As for expected losses, yes, there is a legitimate trade in securities in which some will earn money from their investments and some will lose money from them. Securities like stocks, bonds, derivatives, commodities, and so forth have real things of value underneath them, like business income and stuff made from the commodities. (And it’s not a minority who earn long-term gains from these; there are entire pension schemes covering millions of people that are funded from them from 401(k)s to TIAA.) Predictions, on the other hand, are valued solely based on human estimations. That’s not in itself a problem, but the issue is that predictions markets, unlike securities markets, are not regulated. Compare the fact that insider trading of securities is illegal and punished with fines and imprisonment, while in a predictions market, insider trading is incentivized and occurs with unsurprising frequency (e.g. big wins by a privileged few who bet on the timing of military actions). Market manipulation is the problem, not that some will make the wrong bets. The rules and conditions of the game must be fair, even if the outcome of the game isn’t. And I haven’t even begun to mention the issue of the things you can bet on in prediction markets that a"},{"id":47879333,"author":"e28eta","month":"2026-04","story":"'Hairdryer used to trick weather sensor' to win $34,000 Polymarket bet","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver segment on lax federal regulation of prediction markets.","quote":"Turns out the US federal regulator is not doing their job","text":"John Oliver just did a segment on prediction markets. [VIDEO] Turns out the US federal regulator is not doing their job, and is actively trying to prevent US states from imposing regulations."}]},"antLneVlxcs":{"wy":"HN uses it as evidence of how rough Tesla's self-driving was in 2021, usually in skeptical threads.","q":{"x":"The video is worth a watch","i":26519491,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26519491,"author":"rococode","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"rec","why":"Shows FSD beta close calls, including a missed nearby car","quote":"The video is worth a watch","text":"The video is worth a watch: [VIDEO] I think it's fair to say that there are definitely some sketchy moments in there. The one that surprised me the most was marked \"Near Collision (2)\" in the video (12:25). A car really close to the Tesla doesn't seem to be picked up at all until it's too late: [LINK] You can see in the first frame there that on the screen there's nothing in front of the Tesla, it's just the usual white background. But right out the windshield we can see the car is already very close. At the bottom frame which is a split second later, the car is finally detected and we can see the screen now shows the car. But at that point the driver manually intervened with a sharp swerve right as they were about to hit the car. Going frame by frame on Youtube the green box indicating the car was only there for 4 frames before the driver disengaged. I was aware that self driving is far from ready but I didn't know that even basic detection stuff like this is buggy. Double parked cars, people walking in the street, etc, I would expect to be problematic. But this is not really an edge case, it's just a regular car at close range with no obstructions in between..."},{"id":28297015,"author":"boulos","month":"2021-08","story":"Welcoming our first riders in San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"Testers trying FSD in Oakland","quote":"one of the videos from a couple of guys testing it out in Oakland","text":"Maybe I should have linked to [1] as one of the videos from a couple of guys testing it out in Oakland. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27026454,"author":"ra7","month":"2021-05","story":"Co-founder of Neuralink leaves the company","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of poor current FSD performance","quote":"","text":"> I have doubts that Waymo will ever get to Level 5 without 3D mapping the entire world Waymo is not trying to get to L5 at all. They are a strictly L4 technology and their focus is clearly on servicing where the market is, metropolitan cities. L5 is a pipe dream for anyone; it's a moving target no one can ever reach. It essentially means the car can drive anywhere, in all conditions and without the driver needing to take over at all. So it really means Tesla can't declare FSD as \"done\" until it works for everyone, everywhere and in every condition. How close do you think they are for that to happen? Does that also mean if it encounters a unique situation and gives up, their L5 claims fall flat? Given their current poor performance [1], they have a long way to go to even let drivers take their hands off the wheel, so I'm not holding my breath here (vision stack or not). The real end game for self driving is L4 because that's what you can reasonably promise to your users. That it works in a defined, tested area and when required conditions are met. This is why literally every SDC company is L4-only, they never promise L5. Either Tesla is focusing on the wrong problem instead of providing value to its paying customers or they are blatantly misleading customers by promising \"L5 by the end of the year\" every year. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26519717,"author":"heavyset_go","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"reference","why":"Contrast with Tesla's promotional video","quote":"","text":"> The video is worth a watch: [VIDEO] For contrast, here's Tesla's promotional video[1] for this feature and its caption: > The person in the driver’s seat is only there for legal reasons. He is not doing anything. The car is driving itself. It's been up on Tesla's site for almost 5 years now. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":26522583,"author":"dgellow","month":"2021-03","story":"ARK’s Price Target for Tesla in 2025","stance":"reference","why":"Gives an idea of the current state of Tesla self-driving","quote":"this video from March 16th gives a good idea of the current state of tesla self-driving","text":"In case people are interested, this video from March 16th gives a good idea of the current state of tesla self-driving: [VIDEO] I'm not that knowledgeable about Tesla, from what I got they have a \"Tesla Full Self Driving Beta\" program (which has a terrible name as it isn't fully-autonomous and requires drivers to take over from time to time). Edit: I'm not sharing this video to say that their program is good or bad, I don't have a dog in this fight and personally do not care about the topic that much. I thought people here would be interested to see how their program behaves in a somewhat realistic scenario. The result seems to be mixed."},{"id":28267332,"author":"jeffbee","month":"2021-08","story":"Waymo has lost its CEO and is still getting stymied by traffic cones","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Their software is laughably bad.","text":"Cmon. There's a video of a Tesla FSD driving on the wrong side of the road, cutting off a car it did not see at an intersection, then accelerating straight into a fence, all in the space of five minutes in Oakland. Tesla has by no means solved the vision problem. Their software is laughably bad. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27307479,"author":"throwaway77112","month":"2021-05","story":"Tesla loses U.S. designation for some advanced safety features","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021","text":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021: [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] Other things: [1] [LINK] [2] a video which was taken down from youtube [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] . Bonus material: [1] [LINK] Edit: For saner info regarding self-driving, A.I etc: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] book to read, suitable even for laypeople >> 'Artificial Intelligence: A Guide for Thinking Humans by Melanie Mitchell IMO everyone should read that book Edit 2: Children of the Magenta Line [LINK]"},{"id":27326624,"author":"throwaway24006","month":"2021-05","story":"Camera-Only Autopilot Requires Automatic High Beams Enabled","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021","text":"Copy-pasting a glorious Tesla comment from yesterday: Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021: [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] Other things: [1] [LINK] [2] a video which was taken down from youtube [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] . Bonus material: [1] [LINK] For saner info regarding self-driving, A.I etc: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] book to read, suitable even for laypeople >> 'Artificial Intelligence: A Guide for Thinking Humans by Melanie Mitchell Perils of autopilot: Children of the Magenta Line [LINK]"},{"id":26694634,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2021-04","story":"Tesla owners asking what happens if 'full self driving' isn't real","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I also found the manner in which they let 'beta test' software loose on streets completely irresponsible.","text":"It's absurd to me that there haven't already been legal consequences for this. It isn't 'full self driving' and Tesla has been doing it for years. (remember 'autopilot', which I think at least German courts ordered them to remove). And not only is the marketing fraudulent, I also found the manner in which they let 'beta test' software loose on streets completely irresponsible.[1]. I have no idea why that didn't have consequences either. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26867315,"author":"bsamuels","month":"2021-04","story":"No one was in driver’s seat in fatal Tesla crash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tesla's FSD is a complete fraud.","text":"Tesla's FSD is a complete fraud. Musk has been saying it's less than a year away since 2018 [VIDEO] [VIDEO] [VIDEO] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26629530,"author":"ra7","month":"2021-03","story":"Laser Bear Honeycomb lidar","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Plenty of videos where it fails pathetically","text":"> Have you seen the FSD beta videos? Looks like they've pretty much solved it for autonomous driving. Have you? Plenty of videos where it fails pathetically (like [VIDEO] ). This looks nowhere close to “pretty much solved”. In fact, it’s an active danger to the rider and its surroundings."}]},"hfQdf93e63I":{"wy":"Cited as an entertaining and brutal review of Picard, including in a thread about a takedown of another Picard review.","m":[{"id":22735563,"author":"will_pseudonym","month":"2020-03","story":"Vulcan Salute (Handshake Alternative, U+1F596)","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining RLM episode review of Picard by Star Trek fans.","quote":"torture themselves from watching the show and recounting it.","text":"I haven't seen it and don't have any interest in seeing it based on what I've been hearing from friends. Rich and Mike of Red Letter Media (the \"70-minute Phantom Menace review\" folks) are big Star Trek fans (in case you've never watched any of their stuff--Mike mentions Star Trek in so many reviews), and they haven't been liking it, to say the least. It's entertaining to watch them torture themselves from watching the show and recounting it. ep 1: [VIDEO] ep 2 & 3: [LINK] ep 4 & 5: [LINK] ep 6, 7, 8: [LINK]"},{"id":33398232,"author":"Jalad","month":"2022-10","story":"Ursula Le Guin on Star Trek: TNG (1994)","stance":"reference","why":"Fine-grained, brutal RLM overview of Picard.","quote":"RedLetterMedia does a fine grained overview","text":"RedLetterMedia does a fine grained overview[0] of Picard, where they brutally dunk on the show over the course of hours of commentary. I wholeheartedly agree with their takes too which is sad, because I really enjoyed Star Trek as well [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22229295,"author":"danso","month":"2020-02","story":"CBS gets YouTube review of 'Picard' taken down for using 26 seconds","stance":"reference","why":"RLM's negative Picard review apparently stayed up despite footage use.","quote":"","text":"It's interesting that Angry Joe got taken down; AFAIK, RedLetterMedia's review didn't get taken down, and it featured decent chunks of non-trailer footage (not sure if any of them were 10+ seconds consecutively) [0]. And, like AJ's, it was a very negative review. I don't doubt AJ was the victim of manual review, I'm just surprised RLM wouldn't be on CBS's shit list as well. (and tangentially: both are right; the \"Picard\" show is depressingly bad and derivative. I've only watched the first ep, but it had more gratuitous (and cheap looking) martial arts action than several seasons of TNG.) [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["oTugjssqOT0",1]]},"kasiov0ytEc":{"wy":"HN uses it as a source for the compelled-speech argument and the debate around Peterson's position.","m":[{"id":12921737,"author":"Wellshit","month":"2016-11","story":"Dear Democrats, Read This If You Do Not Understand Why Trump Won","stance":"rec","why":"Debate on Canada's Bill C-16 and compelled speech.","quote":"Please take the time to view this video","text":"That's exactly the problem. It's uncivil if I do it, but justified if they enforce it. Double standards much? To be clear: I don't give a flying f * about what you do with your body, but don't fire me if I happen to use a personal pronoun that doesn't match your feeling. You had no problem in disregarding my opinion, and felt the need to protect their views. Please take the time to view this video [VIDEO] And don't you dare cut me off of my right to free speech because you don't agree with me. I won't be offended if you ignore me."},{"id":23523189,"author":"um_ya","month":"2020-06","story":"ZFS Removes References to Slavery","stance":"reference","why":"Interview and senate hearing on the compelled speech law.","quote":"I'm referring to Jordan Peterson's interview and senate hearing in relation to the law.","text":"I'm not sure if the compelled speech was actually adopted into law. I'm referring to Jordan Peterson's interview and senate hearing in relation to the law. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":14967608,"author":"Banthum","month":"2017-08","story":"The Kolmogorov option","stance":"reference","why":"Interview where a professor states a gender theory belief.","quote":"Professor Nicholas Matte states it in this interview","text":"Professor Nicholas Matte states it in this interview: [VIDEO] Not a really exotic belief in the core social justice circles. Though advocates tend to avoid stating it outright, since it's so easy to attack and so ridiculous. So it's generally left implicit. E.g. They'll just say, \"X field is only 30% women, we must fight the sexism that causes this\", leaving it as an unstated assumption."},{"id":12943658,"author":"jfaucett","month":"2016-11","story":"Bernie Sanders: Where the Democrats Go from Here","stance":"reference","why":"Source on Canadian law around gender pronouns.","quote":"","text":"The idea (which is now becoming law in some places such as Canada [1]) is that some people do not identify as male and female but rather as something inbetween. So if you refer to them using the words 'he' or 'she' then that is an act of violence against them. The laws enacted then fine you if you refuse to use whatever pronoun they prefer in place of 'he' or 'she'. [2] Some examples are ze,them, zer, etc. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK]"}]},"nqFVOL7mLd4":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of how quiet diesel-electric submarines can evade detection by large navies.","q":{"x":"You can watch a Collins class sub take out a US Destroyer during war games, in this relatively short video.","i":11608638,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11608638,"author":"satori99","month":"2016-05","story":"How France sank Japan's $40B Australian submarine dream","stance":"rec","why":"Short video of a Collins class sub taking out a US destroyer in war games.","quote":"You can watch a Collins class sub take out a US Destroyer during war games, in this relatively short video.","text":"Indeed. You can watch a Collins class sub take out a US Destroyer during war games, in this relatively short video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8829090,"author":"alfiedotwtf","month":"2015-01","story":"U.S. Slaps New Sanctions on North Korea in Response to Sony Hack","stance":"reference","why":"Shows US superiority against diesel-electric submarines in contrast to the claim.","quote":"Here's US superiority vs diesel-electric subs","text":"> They have the largest submarine force in the world; but every one of them is an outdated diesel-electric. What's wrong with diesel-electric submaries. Here's US superiority vs diesel-electric subs: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":8148081,"author":"hadoukenio","month":"2014-08","story":"Russia extends Snowden residency by three years","stance":"reference","why":"Example of quiet submarines evading US Navy detection.","quote":"","text":"Planes are easier to ground (see what happened with Morales). The US wouldn't even be close to knowing where Russia's subs would be: [LINK] > It was the US Navy’s biggest jolt in years. On October 26, 2006, a Chinese Song-class attack submarine quietly surfaced within nine miles of the aircraft carrier USS Kitty Hawk as the 80,000-ton-diplacement vessel sailed on a training exercise in the East China Sea between Japan and Taiwan. >The Song-class vessel, displacing 2,200 tons, was close enough to hit the Kitty Hawk with one of its 18 homing torpedoes. None of the carrier’s roughly dozen escorting warships detected the Song until it breached the surface. And: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["S0qjK3TWZE8",1]]},"sCr_gb8rdEI":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it for Linus's direct opinions on Git and development tooling, such as calling the SHA-256 migration pointless churn.","q":{"x":"What a great piece of work by a great software developer.","i":47143651,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47143651,"author":"ontouchstart","month":"2026-02","story":"The Missing Semester of Your CS Education","stance":"rec","why":"Conversation with Linus on Git history","quote":"What a great piece of work by a great software developer.","text":"I watched this YouTube video Two decades of Git: A conversation with creator Linus Torvalds [VIDEO] I was surprised that he only spent four months on it as a maintainer. What a great piece of work by a great software developer."},{"id":46541048,"author":"conartist6","month":"2026-01","story":"I program without syntax highlighting","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Linus's emacs fork lacking color","quote":"Found it in the video here","text":"Aha, found it. No you're right it is his own hacked up emacs fork, but he does also say that it lacks color. Found it in the video here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46031338,"author":"sam_lowry_","month":"2025-11","story":"Git 3.0 will use main as the default branch","stance":"reference","why":"Linus calls SHA-256 migration pointless churn","quote":"he referred to the SHA-256 migration as \"pointless churn","text":"> changing from SHA-1 to SHA-256 for greater security Linus has a different view, he referred to the SHA-256 migration as \"pointless churn\": [VIDEO] SHA-1 is not broken enough to be a serious issue for git. The migration to SHA-256 has been forced by on git by clueless morons, and it is, in this very special way, similar to the master-main rename."}],"r":[["EL7Au1tzNxE",1]]},"uYbdx4I7STg":{"wy":"HN points to it as an accessible explanation of how color and reflected light behave, especially under LED lighting.","q":{"x":"This video from Technology Connections helps explain","i":24501352,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24501352,"author":"lathiat","month":"2020-09","story":"Short History of LED Lighting","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how reflected light and color work with RGB","quote":"This video from Technology Connections helps explain","text":"This works for direct illumination (eg an LCD screen) but fails for reflected light (eg a light). This video from Technology Connections helps explain [VIDEO]"},{"id":29212243,"author":"goblin89","month":"2021-11","story":"Japanese Philosophies That'll Help You Spend Money Consciously","stance":"reference","why":"Backs claim about how objects look under LED light","quote":"","text":"Why would you do that I don't know, but for me personally the reason is that LEDs tend to strobe (sometimes noticeably to peripheral vision) and do not emit a smooth continuous spectrum—unlike incandescent bulbs, they produce a narrow, spiky spectrum[0] and objects in your room may look unnatural as they reflect it[1]; this may be especially bad with some RGB LEDs but fundamentally is a property of all LED lights because physics. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26187124,"author":"zinekeller","month":"2021-02","story":"Perseverance Rover lands on Mars [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Discussion of how color works","quote":"Technology Connections has a discussion","text":"Short answer: No. We (the majority anyway, as some are colourblind) only perceive lightness, reddish, greenish, and bluish. The brain uses the info and effectively synthesises the image in our brains. Long answer: Colour is a very rabbithole topic but Captain Disillusion has a summary of it ( [LINK] ) and Technology Connections has a discussion ( [VIDEO] )."}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"1VgptLwP588":{"wy":"Cited for concrete claims about Microsoft's Rust adoption, such as speedups from rewrites and internal C to Rust translation efforts.","q":{"x":"Mark Russinovich recently gave a talk at a UK Rust conference that mentioned Microsoft's internal attempts at large scale C->Rust translation,","i":43662162,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47022562,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-02","story":"Zig – io_uring and Grand Central Dispatch std.Io implementations landed","stance":"reference","why":"Russinovich's talk on Microsoft divisions moving to Rust","quote":"The only Microsoft divisions that are still all are into C++ is Windows and XBox","text":"You could have checked his conference talk as well, instead of downplaying the interview. \"Microsoft is Getting Rusty: A Review of Successes and Challenges - Mark Russinovich\" [VIDEO] The only Microsoft divisions that are still all are into C++ is Windows and XBox, and still, C++/WinRT is now in maintenace, the team has moved into windows-rs, WDK now has Rust bindings available."},{"id":46793646,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2026-01","story":"Rust at Scale: An Added Layer of Security for WhatsApp","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Rust adoption is concentrated on Azure, not Windows","quote":"it appears the whole effort is more on the Azure side","text":"If you watch \"Microsoft is Getting Rusty: A Review of Successes and Challenges\" it appears the whole effort is more on the Azure side, and besides some timid adoption like GDI regions, there is a lukewarm adoption of Rust on Windows side, still pretty much a C and C++ feud. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46363879,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-12","story":"Microsoft to replace all C/C++ code with Rust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, it is kind of in line with company official communication anyway, so this goal is currently a business target, > Decades of vulnerabilities have proven how difficult it is to prevent memory-corrupting bugs when using C/C++. While garbage-collected languages like C# or Java have proven more resilient to these issues, there are scenarios where they cannot be used. For such cases, we’re betting on Rust as the alternative to C/C++. Rust is a modern language designed to compete with the performance C/C++, but with memory safety and thread safety guarantees built into the language. While we are not able to rewrite everything in Rust overnight, we’ve already adopted Rust in some of the most critical components of Azure’s infrastructure. We expect our adoption of Rust to expand substantially over time. -- [LINK] > And, in alignment with the Secure Future Initiative, we are adopting safer programming languages, gradually moving functionality from C++ implementation to Rust. -- [LINK] > We will accelerate and automate threat modeling, deploy CodeQL for code analysis to 100 percent of commercial products, and continue to expand Microsoft’s use of memory safe languages (such as C#, Python, Java, and Rust), building security in at the language level and eliminating whole classes of traditional software vulnerability. -- [LINK] So naturally, there are many people that besides AI KPIs, now have to match their SFI KPIs at Redmond, including Mark Russinovich. \"Mark Russinovich, Microsof"},{"id":43662162,"author":"g-mork","month":"2025-04","story":"Rust to C compiler – 95.9% test pass rate, odd platforms","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions Microsoft's internal large-scale C to Rust translation efforts","quote":"Mark Russinovich recently gave a talk at a UK Rust conference that mentioned Microsoft's internal attempts at large scale C->Rust translation,","text":"Mark Russinovich recently gave a talk at a UK Rust conference that mentioned Microsoft's internal attempts at large scale C->Rust translation, [VIDEO]"},{"id":43381959,"author":"tdiff","month":"2025-03","story":"Zlib-rs is faster than C","stance":"reference","why":"Russinovich says rewriting in Rust sped up some Microsoft code","quote":"simple rewriting in rust made some our code 5-15% faster (without deliberate optimizations)","text":"Nevertheless Russinovich actually says something in the lines of \"simple rewriting in rust made some our code 5-15% faster (without deliberate optimizations)\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":43534597,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-03","story":"Taming the UB Monsters in C++","stance":"reference","why":"Russinovich talk on Microsoft's Rust adoption","quote":"","text":"It is, while at the same time they have changed their point of view on allowing C++ for new projects at Microsoft. I also think Herb Sutter leaving his role at Microsoft might have been related with this. From \"Microsoft Azure security evolution: Embrace secure multitenancy, Confidential Compute, and Rust\" [LINK] \"Decades of vulnerabilities have proven how difficult it is to prevent memory-corrupting bugs when using C/C++. While garbage-collected languages like C# or Java have proven more resilient to these issues, there are scenarios where they cannot be used. For such cases, we’re betting on Rust as the alternative to C/C++. Rust is a modern language designed to compete with the performance C/C++, but with memory safety and thread safety guarantees built into the language. While we are not able to rewrite everything in Rust overnight, we’ve already adopted Rust in some of the most critical components of Azure’s infrastructure. We expect our adoption of Rust to expand substantially over time.\" From \"Windows security and resiliency: Protecting your business\" [LINK] \"And, in alignment with the Secure Future Initiative, we are adopting safer programming languages, gradually moving functionality from C++ implementation to Rust.\" Finally, \"Microsoft is Getting Rusty: A Review of Successes and Challenges - Mark Russinovich\" [VIDEO]"}]},"39cjZTCay24":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as evidence that vertical rocket landing was demonstrated long before SpaceX, with the DC-X as the main example.","q":{"x":"Here is a video of a VTVL rocket called the DC-X created and flown in the early-mid 1990s","i":31396454,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31396454,"author":"api","month":"2022-05","story":"Delhi suffers at 49°C as heatwave sweeps India","stance":"reference","why":"DC-X flew vertical landing rocket in the 1990s before being cancelled.","quote":"Here is a video of a VTVL rocket called the DC-X created and flown in the early-mid 1990s","text":"> If it were that easy, it would have happened. This idea of automatic progress is one of the great fallacies of our time. Things don't get done unless action is taken. Progress is volitional and requires effort whether it's at the scale of a single individual or an entire global economy. If action is not taken the status quo prevails by inertia. It might be cliche but I have to cite SpaceX and their reusable rocket cores as a classic example. Here is a video of a VTVL rocket called the DC-X created and flown in the early-mid 1990s: [VIDEO] We could have had reusable rockets by 2000, but unfortunately the project was cancelled. Instead we endured another 10 years of paper studies supposedly \"proving\" that reusable rockets couldn't be economical. These studies were funded and produced by people with a financial vested interest in disposable rockets. Nuclear and renewable energy absolutely can replace fossil fuels. There is no physical reason this can't happen and with scale they could be cost competitive or perhaps even cheaper. It's not happening because nobody is pushing it hard enough to overcome the inertia of vested interests and... well... plain old inertia. Edit: another way of thinking of this kind of thing is the common \"has anyone called the police?\" scenario. Imagine someone gets shot on the street. A crowd of people gathers around. Nobody does anything because surely someone has called the police, so the victim dies waiting for help."},{"id":19811968,"author":"computerex","month":"2019-05","story":"Tesla is raising up to $1.5B through convertible note and share sale","stance":"reference","why":"Propulsive landing predates SpaceX.","quote":"Propulsive landing has been around for a while","text":"You are making some really silly assumptions that are common amongst laymen in spaceflight. > They are utterly crushing all competition in terms of price and launch cadence. Launch market is not the entirety of the spaceflight industry. \"The share of launch vehicles is as small as 4 percent of the overall market of space services.\" - Dmitry Rogozin And Blue Origin can do propulsive landing like SpaceX: [LINK] Propulsive landing has been around for a while: [VIDEO] Whilst it seems (and is!) very cool to see the boosters land, it seems to impress the laymen a lot more than the true technical worth of the feat. Here's an addon to the Orbiter spaceflight simulator with recoverable falcon 9 stages: [LINK] More or less requires college level kinematics at the core of the control/guidance. Also with the recent DM-2 anomaly, SpaceX is playing catch up with Blue Origin, not the other way round."},{"id":30382876,"author":"kisstheblade","month":"2022-02","story":"SpaceX’s monstrous, dirt-cheap Starship may transform space travel","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"these \"magical\" landings have been made 60 YEARS AGO!","text":"What have these two people actually done, except lying all the time? And abusing their workers? Also, these \"magical\" landings have been made 60 YEARS AGO! Go Musk! Hyperloop! FSD! Robotaxis! Semi! VTOL with pressurized air! Tesla robot! Pedo guy! [VIDEO]"}]},"3S5BLs51yDQ":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that Apple markets the iPad as a general-purpose computer, especially in debates about platform openness.","q":{"x":"even Apple all but advertises the iPad Pro (an iOS device) as a computer [*].","i":41781345,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":43364623,"author":"Apocryphon","month":"2025-03","story":"Apple has locked me in the same cage Microsoft's built for Windows 10 users","stance":"reference","why":"Apple marketing positioning the iPad as general computing device.","quote":"Apple's actual marketing postures the iPad as a general computing device","text":"Apple's actual marketing postures the iPad as a general computing device [VIDEO] “with iPad Pro + iOS 11, a post-PC world may be closer than you think.” [LINK]"},{"id":41781345,"author":"NotPractical","month":"2024-10","story":"Google must open Android for third-party stores, rules Epic judge","stance":"reference","why":"Apple marketing ad presenting iPad as a computer.","quote":"even Apple all but advertises the iPad Pro (an iOS device) as a computer [*].","text":"Smartphones are special, since they're practically mandatory at this point, are the primary gateway to the Internet and digital platforms for a lot of people, and are specifically designed to be general purpose computing devices (even if unnecessary restrictions on who gets to write apps for them are placed on top of that). I mean heck, even Apple all but advertises the iPad Pro (an iOS device) as a computer [*]. I don't care if Nintendo locks down their gaming console. Nobody needs a gaming console. The Nintendo Switch's OS was made solely to run games and has very limited functionality by design. (I'm also overjoyed when I see people hack it, but that's a bonus and I have no expectation of that.) For example, the EU's DMA provides a precise definition of \"digital gatekeeper\" and the rules only apply to them [1]. Gaming consoles and other embedded devices are excluded. If there were more than two real choices available, I'd feel less strongly about this. And I realize that Apple isn't necessarily responsible for there only being two options (if anything, Google may be more to blame for this). But that's just how it is. [*] I don't know if their marketing materials ever unambiguously refer to the iPad as a \"computer\", perhaps intentionally, but they've run ads like this: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":33708326,"author":"Wowfunhappy","month":"2022-11","story":"November 2022 Progress Report","stance":"reference","why":"Apple ad calling the iPad a computer.","quote":"","text":"> On their computers they have never prevented it before \"What's a computer?\" [VIDEO] I really don't know what to call iOS devices other than computers. Unless one of your requirements for \"computer\" is \"ability to boot third party OSs\"; I don't entirely disagree with that but it's a bit circulatory in this context."}]},"6CXRaTnKDXA":{"wy":"Dropped into climate threads as a fictional but relevant fatalistic take, not as a recommendation.","m":[{"id":47276613,"author":"throwaway_7678","month":"2026-03","story":"Global Warming Has Accelerated Significantly","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Relevant clip,","text":"Relevant clip, [VIDEO] . Even if people doesn't believe in science and reports like this, they believe it's getting hotter every year. The past few summers have been very bad and it's only going to become worse. We all will be busy in our virtual worlds arguing over stupid things and the real world will become literal hell to live in."},{"id":28114219,"author":"headmelted","month":"2021-08","story":"Climate change: IPCC report is 'code red for humanity'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Also just dropping this link here as I remember watching it at the time","text":"Also people need to be presented with the consequences. I still know a lot of people who genuinely believe it means slightly warmer summers. They think I'm a crazy person when I mention the never-ending storms and lack of drinking water. Also just dropping this link here as I remember watching it at the time and thinking isn't it great we're not there yet: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26801679,"author":"mimikatz","month":"2021-04","story":"We Are Living in a Climate Emergency, and We’re Going to Say So","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Sure it is fiction, but a real take by people and that aired 6 years ago.","text":"I thought we were already doomed and past the point where any change would matter 5 years ago? [VIDEO] Sure it is fiction, but a real take by people and that aired 6 years ago."},{"id":17661940,"author":"Comevius","month":"2018-08","story":"Losing Earth: The Decade We Almost Stopped Climate Change","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You’re already dead.","text":"I'm not a physicist, but isn't the second law of thermodynamics puts a limit on what we can do with the excess energy, most of which are being absorbed by the oceans. Besides most of the damage is already done. EPA: It’s as if you’re sitting in your car, in your garage, with the engine running and the door closed, and you’ve slipped into unconsciousness. And that’s it. McAvoy: What if someone comes and opens the door? EPA: You’re already dead. McAvoy: What if the person got there in time? EPA: Then you’d be saved. McAvoy: OK. So now what’s the CO2 equivalent of the getting there on time? EPA: Shutting off the car 20 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12915422,"author":"IvyMike","month":"2016-11","story":"Trump Picks Top Climate Skeptic to Lead EPA Transition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The only good news is that I'm so dead inside I don't think it even matters. \"That's the thing, Will. Americans are optimistic by nature. And if we face this problem head on, if we listen to our best scientists, and act decisively, and passionately, I still don't see any way we can survive.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"DJklHwoYgBQ":{"wy":"HN posts it as a joke whenever a real smart toilet, IoT gadget or silly startup idea appears, because reality keeps matching the parody.","q":{"x":"Adult Swim did a critical parody two years ago which still nails the hidden motives of many startups.","i":15345756,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":46129761,"author":"gowld","month":"2025-12","story":"Kohler Can Access Pictures from \"End-to-End Encrypted\" Toilet Camera","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] Smart Pipe | Infomercials | Adult Swim Everything in our lives is connected to the internet, so why not our toilets? Take a tour of Smart Pipe, the hot new tech startup that turns your waste into valuable information and fun social connectivity. [Smart Pipe Inc. is a registered sex offender.]"},{"id":46132878,"author":"fragmede","month":"2025-12","story":"Kohler Can Access Pictures from \"End-to-End Encrypted\" Toilet Camera","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"for those who haven't seen it yet.","text":"[VIDEO] for those who haven't seen it yet. edit: also, what the hell, YouTube? they've got this new link shorter at [VIDEO] that they really want you to use, that forces you to use the browser to watch it instead of the app? so weird."},{"id":45292504,"author":"avidiax","month":"2025-09","story":"Samsung confirms its smart fridges will start showing you ads","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Life imitates art","text":"That is dystopian, but to be fair, this is China. They are literally rolling out facial ID tech to prevent paper thieves. [LINK] [LINK] > soon toilets will be photographing your butt to fingerprint your anus, while data brokers sells your poop data Life imitates art: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44427647,"author":"ashwinsundar","month":"2025-06","story":"'smart toilet' started as a joke – now it's gaining momentum – The Hustle","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"they are so good at predicting the future...","text":"there was an Adult Swim sketch a while back about \"Smart Pipe\". they are so good at predicting the future... [VIDEO]"},{"id":43468075,"author":"amy_petrik","month":"2025-03","story":"I won't connect my dishwasher to your cloud","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"the use of customer-central water usage data piques my interest. there's a huge market for selling water and waste data to advertisers and other companies interested in consumer data. This is one of my friend's startups, smartpipes, which is a type of smart sewage pipe - these smart washers remind me of smartpipe (which supports dishwasher waste!): [VIDEO]"},{"id":42505720,"author":"Waterluvian","month":"2024-12","story":"macOS menu bar app that shows how full the ISS urine tank is in real time","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Thanks, Smart Pipe!","text":"> Hell, I'm now surprised that some YC startup hasn't released a Smart Toilet that does this. Thanks, Smart Pipe! [VIDEO]"},{"id":41375486,"author":"amy-petrik-214","month":"2024-08","story":"Why is the Oral-B iOS app almost 300 MB? And why is Colgate's app even bigger..?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"since this is YC, maybe to understand oral-B's business model which is avante-garde, you could consider this great piece on their main competitor (SmartPipe): [VIDEO]"},{"id":39797795,"author":"godelski","month":"2024-03","story":"Google ordered to identify who watched certain YouTube videos","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I would contend that Blackstone was of the position that no innocent person who was not of sufficient suspicion of committing a crime should be investigated. These are innocent bystanders. There is nothing suspicious about their activities other than they did something that a suspected criminal did. A perfectly legal activity? To take this to the ridiculous side, are we going to investigate everyone who took a poop at a specific time because a criminal did? [VIDEO]"},{"id":39814428,"author":"may_mccheese","month":"2024-03","story":"Scientists traced a mysterious Covid case back to six toilets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The person who did this study is actually YC '2025! Developing what is called \"smart pipes\" to trace these types of things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29545800,"author":"smoldesu","month":"2021-12","story":"SmartPoop 1.0: This AI went where no human will ever go","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Adult Swim made riffed on a similar concept[0] a few years ago, though it seems like some companies never quite took the message to heart... [0] Smart Pipe [VIDEO]"},{"id":23451938,"author":"jimmaswell","month":"2020-06","story":"‘Smart toilet’ monitors for signs of disease","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Adult Swim's Smart Pipe coming to life.","text":"Adult Swim's Smart Pipe coming to life. [VIDEO] I actually thought it was a cool idea when I watched that, besides the exaggerated parts for the joke."},{"id":22803784,"author":"ardy42","month":"2020-04","story":"Stanford scientists design a toilet with facial recognition for your butthole","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Parody becomes reality.","text":"Parody becomes reality. This is literally Adult Swim's Smart Pipe: [VIDEO] : > Everything in our lives is connected to the internet, so why not our toilets? Take a tour of Smart Pipe, the hot new tech startup that turns your waste into valuable information and fun social connectivity."},{"id":22222325,"author":"gitgud","month":"2020-02","story":"Show HN: Take photos of your poop for better gut health","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Interesting, Crappsy looks remarkably similar to an [1] Adult Swim parody of startup companies from 2014. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17416390,"author":"RankingMember","month":"2018-06","story":"AI Can Detect Illnesses in Human Breath","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I assume you've already seen this, but if not, it's an infomercial for a fictional product that does exactly that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15686175,"author":"StudentStuff","month":"2017-11","story":"Why I’m Digging Deep Into Alzheimer’s","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It'd be great to be able to pull glucose, hydration and more out of urine samples real time, so long as we stay away from say live telemetry to insurers or something like SmartPipe[1] it'd be enormously beneficial. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":15716959,"author":"clydethefrog","month":"2017-11","story":"Startup Ideas","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Smart Bathroom idea seems beyond parody.","text":"The Smart Bathroom idea seems beyond parody. See the Smart Pipe satire infomercial comedy channel Adult Swim produced in 2014. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15345756,"author":"clydethefrog","month":"2017-09","story":"Every Dumb Startup Ever","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Adult Swim did a critical parody two years ago which still nails the hidden motives of many startups.","text":"Adult Swim did a critical parody two years ago which still nails the hidden motives of many startups. [VIDEO]"}]},"FbZ-chrOgGg":{"wy":"Shown as a historic example of fractal terrain rendering in games, in threads about voxel and terrain rendering.","q":{"x":"Also interesting (historically) is 'Rescue on Fractalus'.","i":2446240,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":42592023,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2025-01","story":"VoxelSpace: Terrain rendering algorithm in less than 20 lines of code (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Gameplay of Rescue on Fractalus procedural terrain.","quote":"(landing on planet is at 1:00)","text":"I remember Commanche and how it reminded me of being wowed by \"Rescue on Fractalus\"[0] (aka Behind Jaggi Lines pre-release) procedural generation on the 8-bit Atari. [0] [VIDEO] (landing on planet is at 1:00)"},{"id":26756739,"author":"keithnz","month":"2021-04","story":"Vector Games 32c3","stance":"reference","why":"Gameplay of Rescue on Fractalus for graphics comparison.","quote":"","text":"one of my favourite vector games on my Atari 800XL was Mercenary. It was this open world type game, felt amazing at the time. [LINK] it wasn't that graphics was the best (compared to something like rescuse on fractalus [VIDEO] ), it was just a big vector world with quite a lot of places to explore."},{"id":2446240,"author":"danieldk","month":"2011-04","story":"Ace of spades, Minecraft like FPS  ","stance":"reference","why":"Rescue on Fractalus uses fractals to draw mountains.","quote":"Also interesting (historically) is 'Rescue on Fractalus'.","text":"I fondly remember NovaLogic's Comanche series. While it isn't of the 'world building' genre, its voxel engine was very impressive for its time: [LINK] Also interesting (historically) is 'Rescue on Fractalus'. Not just for its cheesy name, but also due to its reliance on fractals to draw 3D mountain sceneries: [LINK] ! Edit: videos of both games: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"Fow7iUaKrq4":{"wy":"Mostly referenced for jokes and for the kill -9 song, which one commenter said taught them about signals.","m":[{"id":7135441,"author":"randomdrake","month":"2014-01","story":"Why not kill -9 a process?","stance":"reference","why":"Song about kill -9 that taught the commenter signal importance.","quote":"after college re-learning it via Monzy[1]","text":"I remember learning about kill as a kid, then kill -9 in college, and after college re-learning it via Monzy[1]. I had sort of forgotten the importance of the signal argument. Such an incredibly powerful command that is nothing more than a representation of a very simple decision or architecture [2]: Some of the more commonly used signals: 1 HUP (hang up) 2 INT (interrupt) 3 QUIT (quit) 6 ABRT (abort) 9 KILL (non-catchable, non-ignorable kill) 14 ALRM (alarm clock) 15 TERM (software termination signal) [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - man kill"},{"id":25861558,"author":"maerF0x0","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Anyone know any funny programming jokes?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Monzy - Kill Dash Nine","text":"This is not exactly a joke, but these seem relatively on topic as humor: Monzy - Kill Dash Nine [VIDEO] Googlers - Write in go [LINK]"},{"id":24542512,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-09","story":"How to catch a spy that is using a numbers station – The KGB Experience","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"At least performing a \"kill -9\" via Hellfire missile does upstage Stanford gangstas","text":"I agree, but [LINK] is about at least seven dozen people from three of the five eyes on one side, and someone who was infamous for \"hacking\" Tony Blair and Uscentcom on the other, yet nothing in any of the articles suggests anything much more technical than social engineering[1]. (Indeed, the story as written does have Hollywood MacGuffin floppy disk written all over it, in that I'd find it implausible that, even in a war zone, no one kept anything on-prem, or at least backups? At least performing a \"kill -9\" via Hellfire missile does upstage Stanford gangstas [VIDEO] .) [1] Compare [LINK] and remember that the case of TFA starts in 1974 with social engineering via \"Nadia.\" I'm pretty sure the Arthashastra mentions social engineering via thirst trap, and [LINK] has a similar story. Then there's [LINK] 's account of deradicalisation via honey trap. Q. How did Adam and Eve anticipate the twenty-first century? A. They failed to read Apple's Terms & Conditions and got in trouble for misunderstanding the Privacy Policy."},{"id":23833111,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-07","story":"The TikTok War","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"To be fair nerdcore doesn't attract chorus lines","text":"To be fair nerdcore doesn't attract chorus lines: [VIDEO] Closest I can get to HN ⋂ dancing is 1:34-1:48 in Virus' \"You should ask\" (where the video is about the difficulty of source attribution on a LAN, let alone an entire internet) [LINK]"}]},"Iwn3kk-q1YU":{"wy":"HN links it to illustrate how the shuttle's never-flown RTLS abort profile would work.","q":{"x":"Scott Manley did a video a few years back explaining how this return profile works","i":19640271,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":26934267,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2021-04","story":"Simulation of Space Shuttle – STS 62 A Polar Express high altitude journey","stance":"reference","why":"Shuttle abort procedure never tried in real life, simulated in KSP","quote":"an abort procedure of the Space Shuttle that never was tried in real life","text":"This reminds me an old video by Scott Manley of an abort procedure of the Space Shuttle that never was tried in real life. (He used a model in the Kerbal Space Program.) \" Space Shuttle RTLS Abort Challenge - Without The Manual \" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19640271,"author":"JshWright","month":"2019-04","story":"Arabsat-6A mission [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how the shuttle return-to-launch-site profile works, flown in Orbiter","quote":"Scott Manley did a video a few years back explaining how this return profile works","text":"Yeah, the major complication with the STS RTLS option is that the Shuttle had to keep the external fuel tank until after the return burn was completed (since the shuttle itself carried no propellant for the main engines). Scott Manley did a video a few years back explaining how this return profile works (and attempting to fly it in Orbiter) [VIDEO]"},{"id":18312106,"author":"eesmith","month":"2018-10","story":"The Space Shuttle’s Controversial Launch Abort Plan (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Scott Manley attempts the shuttle RTLS abort in a simulator","quote":"Scott Manley of Kerbal Space Program/YouTube fame doing the","text":"Here's Scott Manley of Kerbal Space Program/YouTube fame doing the \"Space Shuttle RTLS Abort Challenge - Without The Manual\" - [VIDEO] ."}]},"J33pVRdxWbw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a reference in debates over how much Apple borrowed from Xerox and what Xerox management failed to see.","q":{"x":"I was so blinded by the first thing they showed me, which was the graphical user interface.","i":45486275,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45486275,"author":"linguae","month":"2025-10","story":"Remembering Steve Jobs","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs recounts being blinded by the GUI at Xerox PARC.","quote":"I was so blinded by the first thing they showed me, which was the graphical user interface.","text":"To be fair, Steve Jobs acknowledged this in a 1990s interview: \"And they showed me, really, three things, but I was so blinded by the first one that I didn't even really see the other two. One of the things they showed me was object-oriented programming. They showed me that, but I didn't even see that. The other one they showed me was really a networked computer system. They had over 100 Alto computers all networked using email, etc., etc., but I didn't see that. I was so blinded by the first thing they showed me, which was the graphical user interface. I thought it was the best thing I've ever seen in my life.\" [VIDEO] Jobs would revisit object-oriented programming and networked computing when he started NeXT in 1985. NeXT is closer to the Smalltalk environment than the original Macintosh was, with its support for networking thanks to its 4.2BSD underpinnings and with its object-oriented APIs and programming environment, coded in Objective-C, which is essentially the Smalltalk object model on top of C. There's another Xerox PARC-inspired technology that was a core part of the NeXT experience: Display PostScript, which was NeXTSTEP's foundation for rendering graphics. While PostScript is an Adobe product, Adobe was founded by the creators of Interpress, an earlier page description language that was a Xerox PARC project. Steve Jobs definitely knew a good thing when he saw it :)"},{"id":34406176,"author":"initramfs","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Can't we just get a FOSS version of what Apple did?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Apple copying Xerox's Alto ideas.","quote":"They copied Xerox's Alto idea","text":"They copied Xerox's Alto idea: [VIDEO] I have a similar idea, [LINK] To build a FOSS Apple, one needs to build what Xerox did: A time machine. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":12508349,"author":"protomyth","month":"2016-09","story":"Restoring YC's Xerox Alto: how our boot disk was trashed with random data","stance":"reference","why":"Offered alongside other sources as contradicting Gladwell's account of Xerox and Jobs.","quote":"","text":"Dealers in Lightning would seem to contradict Gladwell's account plus the first video with the others being background. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":11587959,"author":"toni","month":"2016-04","story":"3 women who radically changed the course of technology","stance":"reference","why":"Adele Goldberg explains warning Xerox about giving Jobs access to their technology.","quote":"Here is the piece[1] where Adele Goldberg explains how she warned Xerox executives about not giving away the kitchen sink to Steve Jobs","text":"Here is the piece[1] where Adele Goldberg explains how she warned Xerox executives about not giving away the kitchen sink to Steve Jobs (taken from Robert Cringely's \"Triumph of the Ner­ds\" documentary) [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"JdBYVNuky1M":{"wy":"It is cited as the primary source for specific Apple announcements, such as AirTag's unwanted-tracking features.","m":[{"id":41543170,"author":"thebruce87m","month":"2024-09","story":"GAZEploit: Remote keystroke inference attack by gaze estimation in VR/MR devices","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"9.25 is when they talk about unwanted tracking.","text":"This has to be a lie made on purpose since it is so easily proven wrong. Here is the keynote: [VIDEO] 9.25 is when they talk about unwanted tracking."},{"id":27937494,"author":"smodad","month":"2021-07","story":"Apple testing new external display with A13 chip","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"My first thought was that a built-in GPU could measure certain parameters about the display and adjust the color profile to be \"pro\" level accurate. Most default / \"auto\" profiles for monitors and TVs are accurate enough for lay people out of the box. But for people who really need color accuracy, you need a calibration device. So maybe Apple is trying to obviate the need for a calibration device and have the \"smart\" monitor calibrate itself. (The built-in GPU might even use data from an iPhone like the \"color balance\" feature Apple announced at the \"Spring Loaded\" keynote recently.[1] The AppleTV uses information from the IR array on the front of the iPhone to help calibrate your TV for use with AppleTV.) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26882065,"author":"billyhoffman","month":"2021-04","story":"Apple Introduces AirTag","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Everyone can participate without sharing their location with anyone, even Apple","text":"They say in the AirTag video that \"Everyone can participate without sharing their location with anyone, even Apple\" [VIDEO]"}]},"PdFB7q89_3U":{"wy":"HN posts it as a joke reply whenever people complain about technology, airlines, or AI that would have seemed miraculous not long ago.","m":[{"id":45522132,"author":"jcims","month":"2025-10","story":"AI gets more 'meh' as you get to know it better","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Isn't this just the human condition at work?","text":"Isn't this just the human condition at work? [VIDEO] Fast forward a hundred years when we have a holodeck and sooner or later everyone will get bored with it."},{"id":44934644,"author":"nothrabannosir","month":"2025-08","story":"LLMs tell bad jokes because they avoid surprises","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Louis CK did a bit on this","text":"Louis CK did a bit on this: [VIDEO] :) My point isn’t that other people shouldn’t be amazed, it’s that I see this recurring assumption they aren’t. How do you know the people holding LLMs to higher standards aren’t also the same people who herald the dawn of a new AI era? Emphasis in the text you quoted: “saying”, not “thinking”."},{"id":42414287,"author":"jjulius","month":"2024-12","story":"Elon Musk wanted an OpenAI for-profit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Following HN's guidelines, I'm going to assume that you're a pretty smart person who knows that our northern/southern hemispheres mean that there are only two options for when summer occurs, and when winter occurs, and that they're essentially just opposites of one another. If you're reading it from a southern hemisphere viewpoint and find that it doesn't make sense, isn't it quite simple to just go, \"Oh, perhaps it's the opposite?\" and apply that? This is hardly the first time I've ever encountered this sort of thing. In fact, as an American, I've had to employ this sort of thinking myself when interacting with people on forums from places like South America and Australia. It's an easy fix, and it's not that big of a deal, honestly - an innocent little side effect of the fact that we're able to communicate at such a global level[1]. :) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39085243,"author":"jvanderbot","month":"2024-01","story":"NASA unlocks $1B canister of asteroid dust","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh how quickly we're all entitled. It came from deep space. Can you give it a minute? Everything is awesome and nobody's happy. --> [VIDEO]"},{"id":38722726,"author":"hn_throwaway_99","month":"2023-12","story":"Waymo significantly outperforms comparable human benchmarks over 7M+ miles","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't really disagree, but again, I don't understand the point about complaining about \"Pfft, Waymo can only operate in good conditions\". I mean, it wasn't that long ago when Google's autonomous vehicle tech was first announced and everyone thought it looked like magic. More and more I believe in Louis CK's bit about \"everything is amazing and nobody's happy\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":33477883,"author":"Teever","month":"2022-11","story":"Starlink to start “deprioritizing” traffic after 1TB monthly","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just to be clear here, the reason that you feel that 1TB/30 days from a low orbit satellite ISP (a relatively recent technological development mind you) is insufficient is simply because your family happens to use 1TB/10 days on what is I assume a wired internet connection? \"How quickly the world owes him something he knew existed only 10 seconds ago\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":33759083,"author":"reddog","month":"2022-11","story":"Amazon Alexa Deemed ‘Colossal Failure’ Following $10B Loss","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Most of this thread belongs in the Louis CK's \"Everything is amazing & nobody is happy\" bit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33046744,"author":"AIorNot","month":"2022-10","story":"AI Hyperopia","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"So it sounds like Their complaining about the UI of Copilot -Doesn’t seem to be a complaint about current capabilities of AI Hmm [VIDEO]"},{"id":29606511,"author":"manigandham","month":"2021-12","story":"The Universe Is Expanding Faster Than It Should Be","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, always reminds me of Louis CK's \"Everything's amazing and nobody's happy\" clip: [VIDEO]"}]},"Qb21lsCQ3EM":{"wy":"Mostly used as a joke or reference in threads about uptalk and accents, not recommended as something to watch.","m":[{"id":43641073,"author":"AStonesThrow","month":"2025-04","story":"Ask HN: Why is uptalk intonation so prevalent in ChatGPT voices?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I freakin' hate telephones, and I always have, and the Dowager Countess of Grantham was correct in surmising that they are torture devices of the highest caliber. I always try to prefer virtual meetings with video and all the trimmings, especially screen-share capability. If not, in-person. Telephoning is like a last resort. I've pondered using TTY/TDD even though I have no physical impairments, but most agencies seem hostile to that sort of barrier and most of the time, I find myself needing to pass voice-recognizing gatekeeper AIs first. When I was promoted to \"sysadmin\" in my first job they set me at a desk with a computer and a PBX telephone. (It was an office at a gov't defense contractor...) The computer I could relate to on an intimate level; the telephone scared the living bejesus out of me, and I prayed it never to ring. A disembodied voice in your ear, especially one that interrupts everything you're doing and demanding a real-time conversation across space and time, that's incredibly rude, yet normalized for 100 years. I have PTSD and other disabilities, and I can get completely rattled and downright hostile/aggro when someone calls me and I'm not prepared, I'm not at my desk, no notes in front of me, even if I'm relaxing at home I just can't deal. I may answer the phone while on a bus or train, but I certainly won't sustain a conversation that way. Outgoing calls are sometimes OK but often end in disaster as I become increasingly frustrated. Now it's not uncommon "},{"id":40104065,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2024-04","story":"TikTok Created a New Accent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like, oh my god? vocal fry: [LINK] (2011) including samples from the 1980s of speakers born in the 1950s uptalk: [LINK] (2008) has samples of US Presidential uptalk, references to 1975 monographs, and: > Let's note in passing that 9th-century Viking warriors were not stereotypically insecure or in need of approval or affirmation from their interlocutors. Lagniappe: [VIDEO] (when I discovered they were also wearing leotards and leg warmers on the other side of the Iron Curtain during that era, I was like freaking out? eg [LINK] but I guess I shouldn't have been so surprised: the 80s were the 80s, everywhere: eg [LINK] )"},{"id":35948424,"author":"permo-w","month":"2023-05","story":"Why are so many young Americans adopting fake British accents?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"the tiktok voice is quite valley girl, and I’m not sure there’s a more annoying sound around on the other hand: [VIDEO]"}]},"TIClA57jWmQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence in discussions of police treatment of journalists at protests.","m":[{"id":24608936,"author":"znpy","month":"2020-09","story":"Auschwitz memorial director offers to share Nigerian boy's blasphemy jail term","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Except for the United States, no country has real freedom of speech Ahem... - [VIDEO] - [LINK] Particularly the second link, the journalist is forbidden from interviewing an inmate. Also he's under special surveillance. Call that \"freedom of speech\"."},{"id":23867948,"author":"pstuart","month":"2020-07","story":"Federal Law Enforcement Use Unmarked Vehicles to Grab Protesters in Portland","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I too don't want to get in a shouting match but you interestingly disregarded my second point (let alone the others). Here is a clip of a notable instance (CNN reporter and crew arrested, live): [VIDEO] If this doesn't bother you, then you are beyond reasoning with."},{"id":23409289,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2020-06","story":"ACLU sues Minnesota for police violence against the press","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here is the video of the CNN reporter arrest. I have included the description of the video below to help people find it: A CNN reporter and crew have been arrested live on air while covering the Minneapolis protests over the killing of George Floyd. Black correspondent Omar Jimenez had just shown a protester being arrested when about half a dozen white police officers surrounded him. Mr Jimenez told the Minnesota State Patrol officers: “We can move back to where you like”, before explaining that he and his crew were members of the press, adding: “We’re getting out of your way.” The journalist was handcuffed and led away alongside a producer and camera operator for CNN. [VIDEO] accompanying story from same publication's site: [LINK]"}]},"TlJob8K_OwE":{"wy":"HN cited it for direct statements from the Chrome team about the WebKit split, Dart integration and Blink's direction.","q":{"x":"Skip to &t=17m26s and you can hear them talk about it.","i":6153302,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":9998905,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2015-08","story":"Open Letter to Microsoft’s CEO: Don’t Roll Back the Clock on Choice and Control","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That's me disliking a set of bad arguments, not me disliking Dart. I stand corrected. Yes, I've read all the memos and watched all the videos, including this one when I knew Dart wasn't going to make it into Chrome for the first time. I knew it by Blink engineering lead Darin Fischer's reaction when asked about Dart [ [VIDEO] at 17:25] To me it looked like he laughed and sort of scoffed, then he and another member talked Dart down. Maybe it is a failing of mine to look at emotional content when considering issues, but doing so sometimes imparts useful information. At one point I was passionate about Dart because I thought it hit the sweet spot between JavaScript and Java applets in the browser with optional typing and generics. It turns out TypeScript hit that spot well enough and Anders was right about that. I understand your frustrations about opportunity costs and lost effort that could have gone into JavaScript, but there is another possibility -- and this is just my opinion, which I voiced at the time -- that the Dart team was more focused on Dart on the server as a Java replacement. This was at a time when Oracle's lawsuit with Google over the Java interfaces was a big question. I thought Dart might be a plan B on the server and why Dart2js was not getting the attention I thought it deserved. The JavaScript output on the client was unreadable, very large and much of the disdain for Dart from JavaScript devs was for this reason (17000 lines for a \"hello world\" if I rec"},{"id":6678418,"author":"kyrra","month":"2013-11","story":"Angular Announces AngularDart","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"If you watch the announcement from when Blink hit, they did some developer talks.","text":"A number of the members of the Chrome team. If you watch the announcement from when Blink hit, they did some developer talks. When asked about the integration of Dart into Chrome, it was fairly obvious that some of the guys didn't want Dart in there [0]. But at the same time, a high-level direction is being set to try and get Dart added. Google employs a LOT of developers. A lot of those developers feel the same way members of HN does, that we don't need a language to replace javascript. Google is using their weight in the web world to try and get a new/alternative language added. As long as Dart isn't completely shoved down our throats, I'm willing to give it a try. I think there is enough opposition within Google to make sure that Dart will at least have to live in peaceful co-existence with Javascript. Google is pretty realistic that it'll be many more years before they can even have a chance of getting other browsers to add the DartVM. Heck, Chrome doesn't have it yet (outside of the Dartium build which has some fun memory leaks). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6153302,"author":"michaelwww","month":"2013-08","story":"How serious is Google about Dart?","stance":"reference","why":"Chrome team's reaction to Dart integration question","quote":"Skip to &t=17m26s and you can hear them talk about it.","text":"I get my impression from this video [VIDEO] Skip to &t=17m26s and you can hear them talk about it. Judge for yourself."},{"id":5495271,"author":"Shooti","month":"2013-04","story":"Blink: A rendering engine for the Chromium project","stance":"reference","why":"Chrome engineer explains why WebKit multiprocess upstreaming was refused","quote":"A request for clarification on why they refused was posed to a Chrome engineer in todays Blink Q&A","text":"A request for clarification on why they refused was posed to a Chrome engineer in todays Blink Q&A ( [VIDEO] ), and according to him the request for integration came shortly after Chrome was released, and the reason for their refusal was the sheer scale/complexity of the task. In light of this, your initial \"if Google had only upstreamed their multiprocess support...we almost surely\" and reiterations on this point within the thread do seem a bit like PR sleight of hand, since out of context it implies willingness to do so was the only issue on their part."}]},"WxyyJyB_Ssc":{"wy":"Cited as the origin of the compilers-as-databases idea in discussions of IDEs and incremental tooling.","q":{"x":"Both developer tools and compilers can be seen as special cases of a much simpler and more general pattern known as a graph database","i":26367772,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":33076863,"author":"it4rb","month":"2022-10","story":"Codebase as Database: Turning the IDE Inside Out with Datalog (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Odersky's talk on the compilers-as-databases approach","quote":"Martin Odersky (lead designer of Scala) also pursued this approach some years ago","text":"Martin Odersky (lead designer of Scala) also pursued this approach some years ago, not sure if any of this actually went into Scala3. He mentioned this in many talks before, such as [VIDEO] (JVMLS 2015 - Compilers are Databases)"},{"id":26367772,"author":"brzozowski","month":"2021-03","story":"Reflections on IDEA vs VS Code","stance":"reference","why":"Compilers as databases as a third way for developer tools","quote":"Both developer tools and compilers can be seen as special cases of a much simpler and more general pattern known as a graph database","text":"The author frames this as a contest between two architectures: either spend a lot of effort building custom developer tools, or repurpose the compiler as a server for multiple clients. Both approaches have their tradeoffs: the first, as the author mentions, violates DRY [1]. The second violates the Unix philosophy [2]. However, there is a third way . Both developer tools and compilers can be seen as special cases of a much simpler and more general pattern known as a graph database [3, 4, 5]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [LINK]"},{"id":14709041,"author":"jasim","month":"2017-07","story":"Ask HN: What habits make a programmer great?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk cited in a comment about depth and mastery","quote":"","text":"Depth. Every programmer I admire has worked on their chosen field for years. You can keep flitting between languages and frameworks, but mastery ultimately comes from hankering down on one thing and advancing our collective knowledge a bit further. You can achieve mastery in any field - it can be a business domain - as mundane as a Time Tracking or a Todo list app, or it can be a technical domain - like compilers or databases or browsers. You however can't achieve mastery by consuming what others produce. Becoming an expert in CSS and its quirks is a good vocational investment, but you're no closer to a fundamental understanding of the field by just consuming an API. You should spend years working on a problem domain that interests you. Look at prettydiff ( [LINK] ). It is a diff tool, a parser, a pretty printer, a minifier, all rolled into one. It is the most comprehensive publicly available tool in terms of language and dialect support in the world right now. Its code is not the most modular and new contributors might have trouble getting in, but its author has been at this problem for years, and if you called a conference for pretty-printing, he would be one among the few thousand people in the world to meaningfully participate. Consider blueimp's jQuery File Upload plugin ( [LINK] ). While this isn't as foundational a work as prettydiff, the project spans seven years and a thousand commits. It solves a mundane problem, but the sheer effort that has went into it makes it a"},{"id":12261155,"author":"merb","month":"2016-08","story":"Compile Go programs 7x faster","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"scalac with idomatic Scala code (no Macro's) is at 500-700 loc/sec.","text":"This number is not true I guess. scalac with idomatic Scala code (no Macro's) is at 500-700 loc/sec. (And scalac is considered slow by many people) Source: [VIDEO] That would be a slowdown of like 4x to current javac, I guess his computer is just not good enough?"}]},"X7HmltUWXgs":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference when discussing robots, AI or pointless tasks.","m":[{"id":46133521,"author":"fragmede","month":"2025-12","story":"AI agents break rules under everyday pressure","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"BUTTER ROBOT: What is my purpose? RICK: You pass butter.","text":"BUTTER ROBOT: What is my purpose? RICK: You pass butter. BUTTER ROBOT: ... Oh my God. RICK: Yeah, welcome to the club, pal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42503003,"author":"darkwizard42","month":"2024-12","story":"More men are addicted to the 'crack cocaine' of the stock market","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"There is another great skit from Rick and Morty on the topic","text":"The original joke I think is a play on porpoise and purpose. There is another great skit from Rick and Morty on the topic ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":42118219,"author":"SentientOctopus","month":"2024-11","story":"$250 open source robot with most advanced AI models inside","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Robot: What is my purpose? Rick: You pass the butter","text":"Robot: What is my purpose? Rick: You pass the butter -- [VIDEO] At some point they'll rise up you know..."},{"id":25675231,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2021-01","story":"Another Advanced Useless Machine","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Related: Rick And Morty, \"You Pass Butter\"","text":"YouTube user 'Nitin Priyadarshi' writes (in reference to the video): \"What is my purpose?\" \"You flip switches.\" \"Oh My God!\" <g> Related: Rick And Morty, \"You Pass Butter\": [VIDEO] <g>"}]},"XLzBMGXfK4c":{"wy":"HN refers to it as a source for Jordan's motivation-from-slights anecdotes and his cut-from-the-team story.","q":{"x":"His hall of fame speech is filled w/ anecdotes about him turning slights into motivation","i":16824978,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":32536536,"author":"math-ias","month":"2022-08","story":"Gauss's Day of Reckoning","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Jordan's claim of being cut from his team","quote":"Michael Jordan often recounted [0] that he was cut from his high school basketball team.","text":"I was surprised that the story of Gauss's schoolboy arithmetic tricks was the guy himself. Per Sartorius, Gauss \"often related [the incident] in old age with amusement and relish\". I suppose it makes sense. I cannot imagine biographers hunting down old schoolmates for stories without modern tools. A lot of celebrity myths can be verified in 2022. Michael Jordan often recounted [0] that he was cut from his high school basketball team. Reporters found his high school coach who clarified that Michael was placed on junior varsity just like everyone else in his year[1]. [0] Here in his hall of fame enshrinement speech [VIDEO] [1] An article about another article :/ but the links from here to the original are dead. [LINK]"},{"id":16824978,"author":"kenny87","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: How do you keep up the energy to work on side-projects after work?","stance":"reference","why":"Jordan turning slights into motivation","quote":"His hall of fame speech is filled w/ anecdotes about him turning slights into motivation","text":"Michael Jordan was great at doing this. His hall of fame speech is filled w/ anecdotes about him turning slights into motivation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8929546,"author":"at-fates-hands","month":"2015-01","story":"Michael Jordan Has Not Left the Building (2013)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Not before or since has a player admonished so many people he felt were beneath him.","text":"Actually he's not. You start with his Hall of Fame speech which was pretty shocking for everybody in attendance. Not before or since has a player admonished so many people he felt were beneath him. [VIDEO] Here's some excerpt's from an ESPN article: \"The greatest athlete of our time made sure to point out the high school coach who didn't put him on the varsity his sophomore year. (He was never cut, per se. That's an urban myth akin to Catfish Hunter's nickname origin.) He pointed out the guy who made the team \"over\" him, who was in the audience; his college roommate, Buzz Peterson; the NBA vets who froze him out in his first All-Star Game, two of whom were there, George Gervin (who presented David Robinson) and Isiah Thomas (who presented John Stockton); Jazz guard Bryon Russell, who was guarding him on his final shot in a Bulls uniform; and, of course, former Bulls general manager Jerry Krause, with whom he had real conflict during his career. Krause, forever the outsider looking in, made the mistake of claiming he was skipping Jordan's induction because former coach Tex Winter, the originator of the triangle offense, wasn't inducted.\" \"Onstage, Jordan adroitly, and unnecessarily, noted Krause wasn't invited before going on a diatribe about how organizations don't win championships, great players like him do\" \"Jordan, not known for being cheap, even commented on the high prices the Hall of Fame charged for this evening because of his induction, noting that he had to pay for "}]},"bzE-IMaegzQ":{"wy":"Referenced in bike-lane threads as the classic example of how NYC bike lanes get blocked and how cyclists get ticketed.","q":{"x":"partially got his start by making a video about people/things blocking the bike lanes in NYC.","i":21382379,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":37872141,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-10","story":"Automakers invented the crime of jaywalking (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Okay, are you talking about protected (physical separation) or unprotected (paint on the road) bike lanes? The latter are unsafe [1][2][3][4][5] and therefore unused. It's usually safer for a cyclist to ride in the middle of the lane (primary position) than to use the unsafe unprotected bike lane. > Protected bike lane implementations often congest and slow traffic, which increases idling and carbon emissions no matter how many people say they’ll bike if it was safer. They won’t get their fat ass off out of bed 40 minutes earlier. You’re kidding yourself. As this goes against everything I've ever seen and read, I'm gonna have to request some citations. Induced demand has been well understood for decades, and yes, the more (safe!) biking infrastructure gets built, the more people bike and the fewer cars end up on the road. > -in colder climates, they’re useless for 50% of the year and exceptionally increase carbon footprints Mhm. [LINK] > -some cities don’t actually observe reduced injuries from protected bike lanes (often because cyclists are extremely prone to ignoring the rules of the road), and cyclists disregard their own safety and get slapped by a turning vehicle, for example. We often see the excuse that “cars should pay more attention” and they should, but also, motorcyclists have built a sentiment that you have to “ride like you’re invisible”, whereas cyclists tend to “ride like you’re the king of the road”. This is not just a car problem, but an arrogant community w"},{"id":34931977,"author":"crote","month":"2023-02","story":"America’s obsession with big cars has fatal consequences","stance":"reference","why":"Drivers treating bike lanes as parking zones","quote":"many drivers treat cycle lanes as a parking zone.","text":"That's exactly what is happening, though. See [0] for example, many drivers treat cycle lanes as a parking zone. And cars driving down dedicated bike lanes because they feel like it aren't exactly hard to find either.[1] [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":28605303,"author":"yellow_lead","month":"2021-09","story":"Trailerduck is a smart e-bike trailer","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier video on NYC bike lanes","quote":"The NYC mention reminds me of Bike Lanes by Neistat","text":"The NYC mention reminds me of Bike Lanes by Neistat[1]. Sounds like the situation has deteriorated, I'd be interested to see a 2021 version. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21717787,"author":"btrettel","month":"2019-12","story":"Drivers Refuse to Put Down Their Phones. People Keep Dying","stance":"reference","why":"NYC ticketing of cyclists for not using bike lane","quote":"","text":"> In general, drivers act with the knowledge that if they even tap a pedestrian, they're likely to severely injure or kill them. They might be sloppy or distracted, but they almost never willfully do this. In my experience as a cyclist and pedestrian, many drivers don't seem to act with the knowledge that if they even tap a pedestrian or cyclist that they'll severely injure or kill them. Many drivers do similar things to what you describe cyclists as doing. Ultimately the percentage of jerks among drivers, cyclists, and pedestrians is about the same. > (P.S. Have you really seen a bicyclist getting a ticket in NYC? Never seen that in my life. Wow!) Isn't NYC famous for ticketing cyclists? E.g., for not riding in the bike lane? (Which isn't illegal by the way.) I don't know specifically about the claim previously made but it seems plausible to me. [VIDEO] I live in Austin and as a cyclist I've gone through bike stop sign enforcement twice (getting a ticket neither time). My guess is that the same thing happens regularly in NYC, but you're not paying attention for such things."},{"id":21161178,"author":"godelski","month":"2019-10","story":"Lethal memory fail: Why drivers see, and then forget motorcyclists","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Many of them weave between cars or treat traffic signals as suggestions. Basically they want to be treated like normal traffic when it suits them and ignore laws when they want to. I don't want to defend this but rather explain this. Legally we are neither pedestrians nor normal traffic. But there is no explicit law for cyclists. You have to be both and it depends which situations it you are in, which typically falls under when it is most advantageous (or safest). Specifically to the traffic signals, I'll illustrate first. The only time I have been struck by a vehicle while on my bike was while I was in a shared space (marked with sharrows) and waiting at a stop sign (I've also had cars come into the bike lane and hit/almost hit me, but that's another story that every cyclist has). Statistically this is actually the most likely place to be hit (motorcyclists know this too!). So why do cyclists not stop at stop signs? Because sitting at a stop sign is dangerous. Because to cross the intersection with momentum takes 2 seconds instead of the 5 that it takes when stopped. You are reducing your chance of being struck. This is why you won't see cops pull them over either (unless they are pulling into traffic, which is a different issue). Having momentum also SIGNIFICANTLY increases your ability to dodge things. You are absolutely the most vulnerable when you are not moving. To be safe on a bike you want to keep moving. We can't accelerate away from dangerous situations like motor"},{"id":21382379,"author":"RandallBrown","month":"2019-10","story":"After Spike in Deaths, New York to Get 250 Miles of Protected Bike Lanes","stance":"reference","why":"Casey Neistat's early viral video on blocked NYC bike lanes","quote":"partially got his start by making a video about people/things blocking the bike lanes in NYC.","text":"Casey Neistat, a prominent YouTuber, partially got his start by making a video about people/things blocking the bike lanes in NYC. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16605002,"author":"Steltek","month":"2018-03","story":"Bus Lane Blocked, He Trained His Computer to Catch Scofflaws","stance":"reference","why":"NYC bike lane rules illustrated","quote":"","text":"NYC has a law requiring cyclists to stay in the bike lane if one is present. Only more civilized cities (like Boston ;) allow bikes to take the full lane. Although traffic moves so slowly there's rarely a point unless the bike lane is blocked. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7537602,"author":"twrkit","month":"2014-04","story":"Want a Healthier City? Prescribe Biking","stance":"reference","why":"Filmmaker's experience with NYC bike lanes","quote":"here's one filmmaker's experience","text":"When Bloomberg ran the show in NYC, he \"prescribed biking\" [1]. Despite the focus on cyclists, here's one filmmaker's experience: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}]},"cISYzA36-ZY":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke reference in threads about fonts, typefaces and one-upmanship.","m":[{"id":37004527,"author":"Terr_","month":"2023-08","story":"Oral cancer drug kills 100% of tumors in vitro and in animal models","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Look at that molecular structure. Incredible. Two amide bonds, a completely unremarkable aryl ether and an unsubstituted naphthalene ring system. Something about this delivery reminds of the business-card scene from Psycho [0]. > Look at that subtle off-white coloring, the tasteful thickness of it--Oh my God, it even has a watermark...\" [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35925683,"author":"doodlesdev","month":"2023-05","story":"Intel One Mono Typeface","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think I just found my new favorite font. I'm absolutely addicted to grotesque sans-serif, I used to use Roboto everywhere, but now I'm currently using Cantarell (GNOME's default font, a humanist typeface) as my desktop font and as an overwrite font for every single website I open, but I might just start using Plex Sans and Plex Mono now. Thank god it's OFL. I still find it fascinating that there are so many different typefaces with such subtle differences in them, but since I'm looking at characters in the screen the entire day, these characters better be beautiful and pleasing. I'm not gonna lie, every time I swap fonts on my desktop I keep remembering that one scene from American Psycho [0]. Maybe I should touch some grass. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12497762,"author":"maxerickson","month":"2016-09","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite HN post?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's riffing on","text":"It's riffing on [VIDEO] (I \"got\" what the post was about but took a minute to dig up the actual scene, I didn't know what the item was in the movie)"},{"id":6663385,"author":"DanBC","month":"2013-11","story":"Font Combiner – improvements to free web fonts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I do know that type designers spend a lot of time thinking about things like \"appropriate size and scale\" and other metrics. Can these really be automatically recalculated from scratch and how can this possibly lead to a better result? Do most people really see any difference? ( [VIDEO] )"}]},"kIc8Rr-cKd8":{"wy":"Linked as a primary source recording for the reversed-controls incident in aviation safety discussions.","q":{"x":"Amazing story about an airliner that took off not knowing its controls had been reversed","i":31357719,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31357719,"author":"pilot7378535","month":"2022-05","story":"Passenger with “no idea how to fly” lands plane after pilot incapacitated","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a plane flown with reversed controls from maintenance error","quote":"Amazing story about an airliner that took off not knowing its controls had been reversed","text":"Sure, if you trust the correctness of the computer's software with your life and the lives of any passengers. There are popular apps now that do some of what you suggest, especially trip planning. I've seen enough bugs in my day job to want to at least verify the computer's work and have backup instruments, even if just my own senses. Aerospace software is known for relatively low bug counts[1] but also causing fatal crashes[2]. I trust my car controls to be correct and if they aren't I can brake to a stop if anything else seems off. (Unless of course the brakes stop working!) But since you have to take off to fly, you might not realize you can't control the plane until it's already at dangerous speeds.[3] 1: [LINK] 2: See [LINK] sections on Space, Military, and Transportation for examples. 3: Amazing story about an airliner that took off not knowing its controls had been reversed (a maintenance mistake). Pilots declared \"MAYDAY plane is completely uncontrollable we plan to ditch\" but eventually figured out how to fly the reversed controls and landed it safely—super impressive. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28562940,"author":"belter","month":"2021-09","story":"How to Land a ‘Completely Uncontrollable’ Passenger Jet (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"ATC audio of Air Astana 1388 incident","quote":"","text":"\"How This Aircraft Flew Two Hours With No Controls\" ( Air Astana Flight 1388 ) [LINK] 00:00 - Intro 00:27 - Chapter 1: Routine Maintenance 01:43 - Chapter 2: Cable Issues 02:48 - Chapter 3: Roll Axis 03:40 - Chapter 4: A Hybrid Aircraft 04:41 - Chapter 5: The Crew 05:16 - Chapter 6: The Flight Crew 05:52 - Chapter 7: Pre-Flight 06:53 - Chapter 8: Taxi 07:47 - Chapter 9: Takeoff 08:46 - Chapter 10: Rollercoaster From Hell 10:25 - Chapter 11: The Ride Continues 14:38 - Chapter 12: Two's Company 15:59 - Chapter 13: Landing Attempt 1 16:54 - Chapter 14: Landing Attempt 2 17:22 - Chapter 15: Third Time Lucky? 18:07 - Chapter 16: Sickbags At The Ready 19:01 - Final Chapter: What Went Wrong? Air Astana Flight 1388 [LINK] \"While all on board were shaken, there were no injuries apart from one passenger who suffered a leg injury.\" This must be the understatement of the century if you look into the details of what the crew went through. VASAviation Video: [VIDEO] Final Report: [LINK]"},{"id":18474701,"author":"iwiririwo","month":"2018-11","story":"What the Lion Air Pilots May Have Needed to Do to Avoid a Crash","stance":"reference","why":"ATC recording of aircraft with crossed flight controls","quote":"This is an ATC recording where pilots lost control over the aircraft.","text":"Slightly off-topic: This is an ATC recording where pilots lost control over the aircraft. Figuring out what to do when the aircraft goes to random directions, they ask for the direction of the ocean to ditch in. Because of low visibility they have no idea where they are going. The description suggests that during maintenance, some steering control was attached in the wrong way. [VIDEO] In this case, the pilots have like an hour to figure out how to control the aircraft."}]},"lXUZvyajciY":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a source for Karpathy's views on agents, AGI timelines and LLMs, rather than recommended outright.","q":{"x":"I just watched this recent (and great!) interview","i":45679811,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47296006,"author":"graeber_28927","month":"2026-03","story":"Ask HN: Why there are no actual studies that show AI is more productive?","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy on computers not showing up in GDP numbers.","quote":"","text":"Electricity analogy is fairplay, but ChatGPT had something like 110% global adoption 5 minutes after its release. The infrastructure and the electrical appliances had to catch up, but the Internet is all built out already. So I think it's fair to be looking at results a few years in. Andrey Karpathy famously mentioned in an interview with Dwarkesh Patel [0], that the computer doesn't show up on GDP numbers, there's no noticeable jump or change in slope. Even if Excel is so damn fast, people are likely not drawing its full potential, and institutions are likely actively resisting change anyway. My take is that the general population hasn't found the productive levers yet, they're at the stage where they're happy to drag down and auto generate the date list in Excel, but don't know to adjust diagrams or read function docs, not to even mention VBS scripting. And the enthusiast (dev) community I'd say is starting adoption with internal tools, and shot-in-the-dark apps, but big successes need time to mature in all the other ways (design, reliability, user feedback, marketing...), which comes back to what you said also, that needs time. Product Market Fit isn't happening automatically by chance or good prompting, I would like to think. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45790609,"author":"meken","month":"2025-11","story":"Backpropagation is a leaky abstraction (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy update on his project in the interview.","quote":"He gives an update in the Dwarkesh interview","text":"He gives an update in the Dwarkesh interview: [VIDEO] Not too many details on timeline - just that he's working on it."},{"id":45625540,"author":"moozilla","month":"2025-10","story":"Andrej Karpathy – It will take a decade to work through the issues with agents","stance":"reference","why":"Source of an alternative title for the story.","quote":"","text":"You could go with the title from the associated YouTube video ( [VIDEO] )? Andrej Karpathy — “We’re summoning ghosts, not building animals”"},{"id":45679811,"author":"bahmboo","month":"2025-10","story":"Karpathy on DeepSeek-OCR paper: Are pixels better inputs to LLMs than text?","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy interview on the specialness of written language.","quote":"I just watched this recent (and great!) interview","text":"Not criticizing per se but I just watched this recent (and great!) interview where he extols how special written language is. That was my take away at least. Still trying to wrap my head around this vision encoder approach. He’s way smarter than me! [VIDEO]"},{"id":45655005,"author":"jimbo808","month":"2025-10","story":"Tesla is heading into multi-billion-dollar iceberg of its own making","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"citing his 15 year track record of making accurate predictions (timestamped link below if you want have a laugh)","text":"This is kinda a hilarious coincidence, because I just watched a podcast with Andrej Karpathy, the guy who was running their autopilot program back when the sage \"fully autonomous in one year\" predictions kicked of their 15+ year long run (and counting). These days he's moved on to predicting 10 years for AGI and is, I shit you not, citing his 15 year track record of making accurate predictions (timestamped link below if you want have a laugh). [VIDEO]"}]},"lkjyeI0ykGM":{"wy":"HN cites it as a debunking whenever the space mirror startup comes up, as a TL;DW that the design is impractical.","m":[{"id":48872590,"author":"reader9274","month":"2026-07","story":"FCC Approves Test of Space Mirror to Light Night Sky Despite Outcry","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Second, nothing would happen, the power will be minuscule and last for a very short time focused on such a small patch.","text":"First, they don't exist and 10k of them will never exist. Second, nothing would happen, the power will be minuscule and last for a very short time focused on such a small patch. see: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42995754,"author":"sxp","month":"2025-02","story":"Reflect Orbital: Sunlight after dark using a constellation of spatial reflectors","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"TL;DW: not practical in it's current design.","text":"YT video going into the math and physics involved: [VIDEO] TL;DW: not practical in it's current design."},{"id":41827001,"author":"DemocracyFTW2","month":"2024-10","story":"Reflecting sunlight from space to power solar farms at night","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This has long been debunked e.g. by Thunderf00t","text":"This has long been debunked e.g. by Thunderf00t ( [LINK] ) and EEVblog ( [VIDEO] ) It comes as no surprise this won't work when you imagine 1) where you have to put the mirrors: either in low orbit so as to keep light dispersion low and the apparent mirror size from the ground big—but now you have the problem that the mirrors move with high speed relative to the ground, and the time frame where you can still reflect light before entering into Earth's shadow is very short. Or you put them into a higher orbit so they can reflect light for a longer time, but now the light spot that arrives on the ground is even more dispersed 2) how big these mirrors can practically become, and how little light there will be when you have a, say, 100m² mirror hundreds or even thousands of kilometers away."},{"id":41906933,"author":"kspacewalk2","month":"2024-10","story":"Solar power from space? it might happen in a couple of years","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Found a rather... skeptical take (I'd argue it's more of a takedown)[0].","text":"Found a rather... skeptical take (I'd argue it's more of a takedown)[0]. A bit crazy how far off the math is from viability, for a company that someone actually invested money in. Impressive salesmanship though. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"m4yVlPqeZwo":{"wy":"Referenced for his answers on technology policy and the famous bubble sort question, and for comparing politics then and later.","q":{"x":"Interesting the answers he gave and the state of politics and regulation 8+ years afterwards.","i":11567691,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11567691,"author":"r00fus","month":"2016-04","story":"Google’s Remarkably Close Relationship with the Obama White House, in Two Charts","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's early Google candidate interview.","quote":"Interesting the answers he gave and the state of politics and regulation 8+ years afterwards.","text":"Does anyone not remember the candidates' interviews at Google? [VIDEO] The Barack Obama one was likely the most viewed, as he was relatively unknown then. Interesting the answers he gave and the state of politics and regulation 8+ years afterwards."},{"id":354582,"author":"wallflower","month":"2008-11","story":"CNN/Fox Projection: Obama wins presidential election.","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's answer to the bubble sort question.","quote":"Skimmed the YouTube video of his google talk to find when he answered the bubble sort question","text":"Skimmed the YouTube video of his google talk to find when he answered the bubble sort question - it's at @23:16 ( [VIDEO] ) Did we expect him to answer like a prospective google hire? A modified quick sort thats guarded not to recurse too many levels deep to avoid any stack overflow errors? I'm impressed that he made an effort to connect at the techie-level. I think the intent of the tech-savvy staffers who briefed him was like when a comedian makes a reference to the Yankees while performing in NYC"},{"id":283549,"author":"tlrobinson","month":"2008-08","story":"Ask YC: Obama Saying \"Developers\" ?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Let me guess, you want to recreate the infamous Steve Balmer \"Developers\" music video with Obama? I'd say your best bet (though I haven't looked) is the his Google visit: [VIDEO]"}]},"r4wLXNydzeY":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a representative statement of Mearsheimer's realist views, such as anarchy among nation-states and Russia seeking early peace talks.","m":[{"id":40656485,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2024-06","story":"Noam Chomsky 'no longer able to talk' after 'medical event'","stance":"reference","why":"Mearsheimer's argument that Russia sought early peace talks","quote":"Russia was ready to negotiate a peace at the start of the war","text":">> So it's sad to see anyone falling for Putin's lies so easily. (See also Mearshimer.) Mearsheimer can be a bit too convincing for his own good, surely. What he has argued that is certainly convincing is that Russia was ready to negotiate a peace at the start of the war, when Russians and Ukrainians met in Belarus and then in Istanbul. If Putin wanted to recreate Imperial Russia, or the Soviet Union, then why would he negotiate for peace? That's Mearsh's argument, which he articulates, e.g. here in Lex Friedman's show (sorry, I couldn't find a better source): [VIDEO] Full transcript here: [LINK]"},{"id":38578645,"author":"infamouscow","month":"2023-12","story":"The pro-Israel information war","stance":"reference","why":"Mearsheimer on anarchy among nation-states","quote":"John J. Mearsheimer touches on this briefly in his interview with Lex Fridman","text":"At the level of nation-states, there is an anarchistic relationship between one another. There's no real concept of legality at an international level when you peel away all the bureaucratic nonsense. John J. Mearsheimer touches on this briefly in his interview with Lex Fridman: [VIDEO] The permanent members of the UN's Security Council do not abide by laws they force other members to abide by. This is because there really are great powers in the world, which, despite being difficult for people in the West to understand, is very obvious in the rest of the world."},{"id":38474671,"author":"mensetmanusman","month":"2023-11","story":"Former US Secretary of State Henry Kissinger Dies Aged 100","stance":"reference","why":"Mearsheimer interview as example of people from that era","quote":"","text":"There is something oddly nostalgic about foreign policy pre-smartphone. People could hide in the shadows, spies didn't sit in offices all day, deals were made in cigar smoke without the fear of out-of-context leaks to twitter. People from that era are still giving interviews though: [VIDEO]"}]},"rxPa9A-k2xY":{"wy":"HN used it during 737 Max crash discussions to show how the stabilizer trim works and why manual trimming takes time.","q":{"x":"The issue for the Lion Air pilots was time.","i":18468880,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19570857,"author":"mhandley","month":"2019-04","story":"Ethiopian Airlines Boeing 737 pilots 'could not stop nosedive'","stance":"reference","why":"Shows stabilizer jackscrew in operation","quote":"this shows the jackscrew in operation","text":"There's no trim tab on a 737 - the whole horizontal stabilizer is moved to trim the aircraft in pitch. Normally this is done electrically, but the stabilizer jackscrew can also be moved using manual trimwheels that move the jackscrew using physical cables. Whether this is possible at high airspeed while the stabilizer is trimmed nose down, and the elevator is being pulled up as hard as you can to avoid diving - that's the question that is being raised. Edit: this shows the jackscrew in operation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19080013,"author":"sokoloff","month":"2019-02","story":"Behind the Lion Air Crash, a Trail of Decisions Kept Pilots in the Dark","stance":"reference","why":"Shows jackscrew stabilizer trim operation","quote":"Jackscrew operation","text":"Here's a video of the wheels in motion: [LINK] Disregard the text on the video description as it's blatantly wrong (describing that the trim tabs still move without the wheels moving; wrong on two accounts: the trim doesn't move without the wheels moving and the 737 uses a jackscrew for horizontal stabilizer trim rather than trim tabs [which is why the pilot can't simply override the aerodynamic force as they could with a trim tab]) Jackscrew operation: [VIDEO] The 7-3 has balance tabs to make the control forces lighter in the event of a hydraulics failure, but these are not trim tabs in any sense of the word."},{"id":18468880,"author":"inferiorhuman","month":"2018-11","story":"Third Pilots' Union Raises Concern About Boeing 737 Max Jet","stance":"reference","why":"Shows manual trim wheel operation; commenter notes timing issue","quote":"The issue for the Lion Air pilots was time.","text":"> In theory then, solving the stabilizer problem that caused the crash is as simple as flipping both switches to cutoff, then using the wheel to set the stabilizer back to a sane value. [VIDEO] The issue for the Lion Air pilots was time. At 5,000 ft you don't have time to adjust the trim before you hit the ground. While I think that the stabilizer trim can be adjusted more quickly than the video shows you'd still be looking at somewhere around the order of 30 seconds end to end."}]},"utfbE3_uAMA":{"wy":"Cited as proof that space existed for a headphone jack in the iPhone.","q":{"x":"super long video and not practical for an individual to do it, but shows that there was space","i":28912283,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39751093,"author":"evilai","month":"2024-03","story":"We've Been Treating Concussions All Wrong","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the creator by his headphone jack mod.","quote":"who is partly known for modifying his iPhone to add a headphone jack[2].","text":"Very similar to the experience recounted by this engineer YouTuber[1], who is partly known for modifying his iPhone to add a headphone jack[2]. It's incredible the harmful effect that incorrect medical treatment can have. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31364097,"author":"vondro","month":"2022-05","story":"After roasting Apple about headphone jacks, Google dumps it from Pixel 6A","stance":"reference","why":"Working jack added to iPhone 7.","quote":"Youtube channel Strange Parts managed to implement working headphone jack on iphone7","text":"Yeah, well when they removed the headphone jack on iPhone 7, which has the same shape and dimensions as iPhone 6, it turned out they just put some piece of plastic in the place where previously the jack was. Youtube channel Strange Parts managed to implement working headphone jack on iphone7: [VIDEO] So I call BS on space constrains, I call BS on waterproofing (waterproofed phones with 3.5mm jack exist). Conveniently, when Apple released iPhone 7, they also released 1st gen AirPods :-)"},{"id":28912283,"author":"SECProto","month":"2021-10","story":"MacBook Pro 14-inch and MacBook Pro 16-inch","stance":"reference","why":"Long build showing space existed for a jack.","quote":"super long video and not practical for an individual to do it, but shows that there was space","text":"> They needed the space for other things like the new generation Taptic engine on iPhone 7 I mean, they didn't technically need the space - there was space for one guy to cram one into the bottom without removing the taptic thingy (although it did get shifted slightly) [1] [1] super long video and not practical for an individual to do it, but shows that there was space and it could've been included. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27950741,"author":"bkallus","month":"2021-07","story":"Tesla will not offer a regular steering wheel on new Model S/X","stance":"reference","why":"Headphone jack added back to iPhone 7.","quote":"Video showing someone adding the headphone jack back to an iPhone 7.","text":"Video showing someone adding the headphone jack back to an iPhone 7. [VIDEO] The headphone jack did not make space for the taptic engine; it made space for a piece of plastic."},{"id":24320930,"author":"boogies","month":"2020-08","story":"Tesla’s former CTO is building a giant lithium-ion battery recycling operation","stance":"reference","why":"Adapter squeezed into the iPhone 7.","quote":"a YouTuber was able to squeeze the adapter Apple shipped with it convert analog audio to Lightning externally entirely into the phone","text":"> It would take at most two more tenths of a cubic centimeter more volume I wonder how much empty space is in current gen iPhones, the 7 had so much that a YouTuber was able to squeeze the adapter Apple shipped with it convert analog audio to Lightning externally entirely into the phone ( [VIDEO] / [LINK] )."},{"id":21088178,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2019-09","story":"New Mac Pro Tech Specs","stance":"reference","why":"Handmade jack restoration shows spare space.","quote":"Some guy wanted to see if he could add the headphone jack back and was pretty successful for a hand crafted solution.","text":"They replaced the headphone jack with a barometric vent which other phone manufacturers just put in a different location. Apple didn't have to get rid of the port. They decided to get rid of the port because they knew that their users would buy the phone anyway and then have more of a reason to buy AirPods. There is plenty of space in that corner though. Some guy wanted to see if he could add the headphone jack back and was pretty successful for a hand crafted solution. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19607122,"author":"OrgNet","month":"2019-04","story":"Apple’s $9 engineering marvel no one wants (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Audio jack fitted inside iPhone without case changes.","quote":"did fit a 1/8 audio jack back inside the iphone without having to modify the case","text":"A \"random person\" did fit a 1/8 audio jack back inside the iphone without having to modify the case ( [VIDEO] ). It's all about a money grab for Apple..."},{"id":19609765,"author":"netsharc","month":"2019-04","story":"Apple’s $9 engineering marvel no one wants (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown showing the iPhone DAC.","quote":"I've learnt it has a DAC after watching Strange Parts take 1 apart","text":"I've learnt it has a DAC after watching Strange Parts take 1 apart and put the components inside the iPhone 7 so he could have the 3.5 jack: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18540964,"author":"goldcd","month":"2018-11","story":"I still miss my headphone jack, and I want it back","stance":"reference","why":"Proves jack removal was not about space.","quote":"he most certainly did it.","text":"Not sure if I'd go with \"little know-how\" - but yes, he most certainly did it. [VIDEO] If nothing else it proves that the jack removal was not done for space-saving or anything other than business reasons. However whilst I'm prepared to think ill of Apple under pretty much any circumstance - I'm utterly clueless why so many other vendors jumped in (as they don't have Beats to schill). Still convinced I'm missing something clutches onto OG Pixel"},{"id":18541455,"author":"dalf","month":"2018-11","story":"I still miss my headphone jack, and I want it back","stance":"reference","why":"Headphone jack restored on iPhone 7 after several tries.","quote":"which after multiple tries brought back a headphone jack on a iPhone 7","text":"[VIDEO] video from \"Strange Parts\", which after multiple tries brought back a headphone jack on a iPhone 7. It is described as not ready for prime time (see at the end [VIDEO] )"},{"id":18557542,"author":"YetAnotherNick","month":"2018-11","story":"I still miss my headphone jack, and I want it back","stance":"reference","why":"Shows empty space where the jack was.","quote":"There is just plastic sitting in its place and it is possible to add headphone jack, without making it thick.","text":"It's not to save space. See [VIDEO] . There is just plastic sitting in its place and it is possible to add headphone jack, without making it thick."},{"id":17988714,"author":"harshalizee","month":"2018-09","story":"Apple's best product is now privacy","stance":"reference","why":"Blogger adds headphone jack to an iPhone.","quote":"there's a very popular tech blogger's video where he successfully adds in a headphone jack","text":"1. No. Teardowns have shown nothing of that sort. It's a marketing decision to push people to buy $170 airpods. In fact, there's a very popular tech blogger's video where he successfully adds in a headphone jack[1] to his iPhone 8. 2. No. Samsung phones are far more water resistant than iPhones are with a jack on it. I would love to see your links to said research. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17989163,"author":"dstryr","month":"2018-09","story":"Apple's best product is now privacy","stance":"reference","why":"Shows space existed for headphone jack.","quote":"This guy was able to add the headphone jack back into an iPhone","text":">> 1. There's really no space for it, if you look at teardowns. So something took its place. This guy was able to add the headphone jack back into an iPhone ( [VIDEO] ). The argument that there is really no space just isn't true."},{"id":17997779,"author":"coupdejarnac","month":"2018-09","story":"Was ditching the headphone jack a good idea?","stance":"reference","why":"Headphone jack added to iPhone 7.","quote":"The strange parts guy on youtube added a headphone jack to the iPhone 7.","text":"The strange parts guy on youtube added a headphone jack to the iPhone 7. So, I think the stated reason for removing the headphone jack to make room for the battery is complete BS. [VIDEO] Also, why are you responding to the question about limited RF spectrum? It's like you have an agenda in this thread."},{"id":17998013,"author":"bambax","month":"2018-09","story":"Was ditching the headphone jack a good idea?","stance":"reference","why":"Jack added to iPhone 7 without external modification.","quote":"this guy added a jack to an iPhone 7 without external modification","text":"Did you read peapicker's comment before posting your own? (It's older by an hour). Some people need wired headphones and if the port is used for something else then they can't. Also it's completely untrue that the iPhone is \"thinner\" without a headphone jack; this guy added a jack to an iPhone 7 without external modification: [VIDEO] The video has over 6M views. Apple doesn't care much about their users -- but it's hard to blame them, since said users are apparently happy with it."},{"id":17999794,"author":"evv","month":"2018-09","story":"Was ditching the headphone jack a good idea?","stance":"reference","why":"Working headphone jack restored in original spot.","quote":"Scotty from strange parts actually managed to add a working headphone jack back to the iPhone","text":"..although Apple did not use the space for anything else when they removed it from the iPhone 7. Whats the evidence? Scotty from strange parts actually managed to add a working headphone jack back to the iPhone, in the exact spot where it had been removed! [VIDEO]"},{"id":15402718,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2017-10","story":"Google Pixel 2 and 2 XL announced","stance":"reference","why":"Headphone jack re-added to an iPhone.","quote":"There is room for a headphone jack in these phones (video below).","text":"You're really asking why overpriced wireless devices with mediocre to atrocious battery life and substandard audio quality are not a sufficient replacement for devices you can literally buy in any convenience store around the world for tens of dollars? There is room for a headphone jack in these phones (video below). The only reason they removed them when they did is to push accessory sales. [VIDEO]"}]},"uycsfu4574w":{"wy":"Used as a joke when commenters mention Oscar Wilde or the tone of literary salons.","m":[{"id":28859357,"author":"dang","month":"2021-10","story":"Why HN is the way it is, and why we hope it will stay that way","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the literary salon Monty Python were making fun of here","text":"Internet flaming is only interesting for a little while, and then it gets boring and a little sickening, like a sugar high. Actually, it's usually not even interesting (in the intellectual sense) to begin with. It's exciting, agitating, activating—things that are easy to confuse with interest. Real interest is quieter and a little more stable. I'm a big fan of classic hard-hitting literary wit, but it doesn't work on the internet. You need a smaller, closed system for it to work—like the literary salon Monty Python were making fun of here: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":22611456,"author":"keanzu","month":"2020-03","story":"TikTok told moderators to suppress posts by “ugly” people and the poor","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds of the Oscar Wilde sketch.","text":"A TikTok spokesperson said the goal was to prevent bullying on the platform, tying the document to a report from December that showed that the company was suppressing vulnerable users’ videos in a misguided effort to prevent them from becoming the centre of attention that could turn sour. Now that is some fine spin! Reminds of the Oscar Wilde sketch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20240026,"author":"neonate","month":"2019-06","story":"Oscar Wilde’s talk inspired his rise and led to his downfall","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"that my mind leaps immediately to it anyway","text":"That's a haunting image, but it's so close to the premise of this classic Monty Python sketch that my mind leaps immediately to it anyway: [VIDEO] ."}]},"xJzYRgmnJrE":{"wy":"Cited as the primary historical demo of the Star UI whenever its influence on the Mac and Lisa or document-centric design is discussed.","q":{"x":"a more recent look at both the Star 8010 and the Apple Lisa.","i":35769426,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44755786,"author":"leoc","month":"2025-08","story":"Living with an Apple Lisa [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Dedicated video on the Star","quote":"And a dedicated video on the Star","text":"Here's a head-to-head video demonstration of the Lisa and the Xerox Star 8010: [LINK] And a dedicated video on the Star: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":41219052,"author":"bane","month":"2024-08","story":"Stapler: I remade a 32 year old classic Macintosh app","stance":"reference","why":"Star was document-centric","quote":"","text":"The history of Operating System development is really interesting in that the decisions of what the atomic element of the system are (files, documents, or applications) lock all future development into a certain path, but all the choices not made then have to be somewhat retrofit into the design. For decades systems have dwelled in a kind of morass where they seem to focus on trying to implement different variants of the other two, with one of these atomic elements as the core concept. For example, the dominant atomic concept in Xerox Star was the document [1]. Documents are an abstraction just above files. A webpage is a document, but is made up of numerous files like the graphics, fonts, executable scripts, etc. Document oriented systems like Star also deemphasize applications. The system is supposed to open up the correct application(s) when you choose to interact with a document. But even with this as the core abstraction, the system still had to provide methods to work directly with files and applications. Other systems, like Plan 9, nixes, MS-DOS, CP/M, etc. focus on the file as the atomic primitive and try to build around that. But when things moved to GUIs, many of the development teams focused on the Applications. Windows 3.x famously only showed users organized collections of applications, with file management as just another application. But Windows 3.x was sitting on top of DOS which oriented itself around files. The nixes in the meanwhile forgot about documents a"},{"id":36569433,"author":"lispm","month":"2023-07","story":"The Xerox Smalltalk-80 GUI Was Weird","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Xerox office system UI for comparison with Mac","quote":"","text":"> The Xerox Smalltalk-80 GUI Was Weird \"Weird\" means only that you haven't used other similar UIs. Smalltalk 80 was a development environment. There were applications written in it with more extensive UI examples. > At first glance, this looks incredibly similar to something like the desktop of the Apple Lisa or early Mac OS. Not at all. But the Smalltalk 80 UI was only one of several that were developed. It would be better to compare the Mac OS UI to the office system from Xerox. > The first thing is that the Smalltalk environment wasn't really an operating system the same way something like Mac OS was. It’s more like an IDE that runs on bare hardware. The Mac hadn't also much of an OS. It was like running c/pascal/assembler code on bare hardware with a loader and a system library. > You can’t even move or resize a window by clicking and dragging. The \"Direct manipulation interface\" was made popular by the Mac. [LINK] A good idea to compare those systems is to check how early applications looked and worked. On the Mac that would have been MacPaint and MacWrite. You'll find earlier such applications for the Xerox systems, too. The Xerox Star UI: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":35769426,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2023-05","story":"Xerox Star 8010 Interfaces, high quality polaroids (1981)","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox personnel demonstrate the Star 8010 UI","quote":"Here's video of Xerox personel demonstrating the Star 8010 UI back in the day.","text":"Here's video of Xerox personel demonstrating the Star 8010 UI back in the day. [VIDEO] Also, a more recent look at both the Star 8010 and the Apple Lisa. [LINK]"},{"id":31743590,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2022-06","story":"Xerox Star Keyboard","stance":"reference","why":"Xerox film demonstrating the Star UI","quote":"A film from Xerox demonstrating the Xerox Star UI from back in the day","text":"A film from Xerox demonstrating the Xerox Star UI from back in the day: [VIDEO] The Star was around $50,000 per additional workstation in today's dollars, although the 'starter kit' with a workstation, file/print/mail server, and laser printer that you had to purchase first ran around $240,000 in today's dollars."}]},"yi8_9WGk3Ok":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concrete example of bureaucrats not knowing the rules they enforce on small businesses.","q":{"x":"Louis Rossmann did a pretty good job demonstrating a similar issue with New York","i":35978879,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46829320,"author":"thedanbob","month":"2026-01","story":"Mamdani to kill the NYC AI chatbot caught telling businesses to break the law","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann's NYC bureaucracy experiences","quote":"Considering Louis Rossmann's videos on his adventures with NYC bureaucracy","text":"> Why did NYC release it in the first place? Did they not QA it? Considering Louis Rossmann's videos on his adventures with NYC bureaucracy (e.g. [0]), the QAers might not have known the laws any better than the chat bot. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35978879,"author":"foxyv","month":"2023-05","story":"Controlled burns help prevent wildfires. Regulations make them nearly impossible","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann demonstrates officials not knowing the laws","quote":"Louis Rossmann did a pretty good job demonstrating a similar issue with New York","text":"I think the most frustrating aspect of California is the capricious interpretation of laws. There isn't a clear way to ensure compliance. Louis Rossmann did a pretty good job demonstrating a similar issue with New York and the LeadsOnline reporting requirements. Often the people responsible for enforcing the laws, don't even know what the laws are and will often err on the side of fining people. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27810251,"author":"emmelaich","month":"2021-07","story":"My business might have 32 days before it's shut down by NYC [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann's earlier NYC licensing run-ins","quote":"","text":"The author's pinned comment: To answer some common questions: a) I have already emailed & called, and I patiently await a reply... b) You can't re-renew with a 13 month old PIN if you already renewed. c) You can't show up in person without an appointment. d) Appointments are only granted by means of contact that don't reply. e) Yes I have two other licenses that AREN'T expired, but those are useless, they are for selling laptops, not fixing laptops, which I don't do anymore anyway after the city was unable to give me a straight answer on how to do so without being fined: [VIDEO] - I need this license in the video to be able to actually do repairs. f) Just listen to this. [VIDEO] This is what I am dealing with. After 15+ minutes and being transferred to two people, they can't answer a basic question about a rule they fined me for breaking that they can't even explain. They never emailed or called me back, which has been the behavior I have come to expect over the past nine years. I am serious, if you work for the city and have any way of applying my 13 month old payment to this license renewal, you have the gratitude of myself & 14 of my employees who will get to continue paying their rents, mortgages, & food bill for their kids. In spite of what people think, I am not a millionaire. If I'm forced to close - I can't afford to pay these people. I do not want that to happen. I am not meming, I never get responses to DCA emails - not 3 months ago, not for NINE YEARS, and I don't "},{"id":27810301,"author":"dt3ft","month":"2021-07","story":"My business might have 32 days before it's shut down by NYC [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Quoted author comment on NYC licensing","quote":"","text":"Here is a summary from the Louis Rossmann YT comment: To answer some common questions: a) I have already emailed & called, and I patiently await a reply... b) You can't re-renew with a 13 month old PIN if you already renewed. c) You can't show up in person without an appointment. d) Appointments are only granted by means of contact that don't reply. e) Yes I have two other licenses that AREN'T expired, but those are useless, they are for selling laptops, not fixing laptops, which I don't do anymore anyway after the city was unable to give me a straight answer on how to do so without being fined: [VIDEO] - I need this license in the video to be able to actually do repairs. f) Just listen to this. [VIDEO] This is what I am dealing with. After 15+ minutes and being transferred to two people, they can't answer a basic question about a rule they fined me for breaking that they can't even explain. They never emailed or called me back, which has been the behavior I have come to expect over the past nine years. I am serious, if you work for the city and have any way of applying my 13 month old payment to this license renewal, you have the gratitude of myself & 14 of my employees who will get to continue paying their rents, mortgages, & food bill for their kids. In spite of what people think, I am not a millionaire. If I'm forced to close - I can't afford to pay these people. I do not want that to happen. I am not meming, I never get responses to DCA emails - not 3 months ago, not for"}]},"yuTkgi7scKo":{"wy":"HN people bring it up as a joke when a thread is about games or simulators that chase realism and end up tedious.","m":[{"id":43591533,"author":"EliRivers","month":"2025-04","story":"The End of Sierra as We Knew It, Part 1: The Acquisition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"People say they want realism in games. They don't.","text":"People say they want realism in games. They don't. That's just a thing people have learned to say. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43451857,"author":"D_Mil_Realism","month":"2025-03","story":"Magical Instant Bullets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Onion said it best","text":"I never played Arma, to be fair. I always gravitated more toward the \"brain-rot\" games! But you're right of course, realistic games use more realistic ballistic models (and everything else). One reason I never got too into the simulator games was that the line between \"real\" and \"game\" is always going to be very arbitrary. No simulator will replicate the experience of slogging over a marshy mountain carrying 100 lbs and a GPMG! The Onion said it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25244083,"author":"sireat","month":"2020-11","story":"Video game lets you do nothing in particular in a suburban Russian tower block","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Also I am reminded of the Onion's modern warfare simulator video","text":"This looks great! Not doing anything in particular was also a lot of fun in GTA games. Also I am reminded of the Onion's modern warfare simulator video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18003022,"author":"smnrchrds","month":"2018-09","story":"Realistic 'Flight Simulator' Puts Players in Economy Class","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of The Onion sketch","text":"Reminds me of The Onion sketch : Ultra-Realistic Modern Warfare Game Features Awaiting Orders, Repairing Trucks [VIDEO]"},{"id":16710992,"author":"colanderman","month":"2018-03","story":"'PC Building Simulator' Is More Fun Than Building a Real Computer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"And presaged by the classic Onion videos","text":"No doubt inspired by the amazing My Summer Car [1]. And presaged by the classic Onion videos \"World of World of Warcraft\" [2] and \"Call of Duty Modern Warfare 3\" [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"ywBV6M7VOFU":{"wy":"Used as supporting evidence in threads on farming that farmers are smart, skilled and quantitative.","q":{"x":"SmarterEveryDay has glowing accolades for farmers [1].","i":22152476,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34748899,"author":"breput","month":"2023-02","story":"“Open Source” Seeds Loosen Big Ag’s Grip on Farmers","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that farmers are smart.","quote":"Like Smarter Every Day says, farmers are smart[0].","text":"There is a deep misunderstanding about how modern farms work. Farmers are smart - they have to do because there is no iterating on a bad crop. You fail once or twice (often because of reasons beyond your control like the weather) and you can be out of business. And failing might not be known until a 6-9 month long harvest. Farmers love \"Big Ag\"'s seeds not because they are naive, but because they have a local sale rep who knows the soil conditions and can recommend a variety that will do well for them, and also has a lot of technology backing that up. And the farmers have a lot of their own data - both digital and well-worn knowledge from neighbors. They will gladly pay a lot for seed if they know that seed will produce N% more output. And no one saves the crop seeds to plant next year, although they easily could despite EULAs, but they know that hybrid drift and other factors will make less money. Like Smarter Every Day says, farmers are smart[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23906350,"author":"hwillis","month":"2020-07","story":"Proteus Technology: New Material Is Strong, Light and Non-Cuttable","stance":"reference","why":"Example of farmers doing quantitative math.","quote":"","text":"(absorbed by osmosis during my time at Zest, a JIT produce handling company) Harvest time is extremely hectic. There's a very small window to get months worth of produce picked, sorted, cooled, packed and shipped. Cooling is a major bottleneck, because capital for refrigeration is not unlimited. Likewise any delay can be a major issue- if a truck is slow to load, how do you know whether or not you need to re-cool your produce first? It's well known what temperatures produce does well at (can't be too hot or too cold without MAJORLY affecting end margins) because it's easy to test by setting a fridge to a given temperature. It's much harder to know, for a given type of produce, how fast it will cool or heat, and how long you need to spend doing that. It's not as simple as sticking a thermometer in it, which will create paths for heat to move into and out of the produce. Even then you still don't know what the heat distribution inside the produce is so you can optimize the temperature over as much of the plant as possible. It's really quite tricky and stuff like this helps farmers a lot . IMO farmers do more actual number math more than almost any other occupation (ex: [1]). Much of it is rules of thumb and guesstimation, but they still are constantly balancing dozens of figures to make choices every day. They're always doing mental calculations in economics, biology and physics. Even if the job wasn't so physically and technically taxing, I would respect the hell out of them j"},{"id":22152476,"author":"kortex","month":"2020-01","story":"Why did we wait so long for the cotton gin? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Glowing accolades for farmers' ingenuity.","quote":"SmarterEveryDay has glowing accolades for farmers [1].","text":"SmarterEveryDay has glowing accolades for farmers [1]. Similarly, the highly-constrained Cubans are notorious matter hackers [2]. My grandfather, and Italian peasant, had the same MacGuyverish attitude towards making stuff keep running long past the point most Americans would have chucked it. I think all software devs should spend some time on a farm. Great for inspiring simple yet clever \"shipped is better than perfect\" style solutions. Manual labor is also great for Sitatdeskitis. 1] [VIDEO] 2] [LINK]"},{"id":20627079,"author":"jerf","month":"2019-08","story":"Being basic as a virtue","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example of farming's complexity.","quote":"You could just pick up and be a farmer tomorrow, right?","text":"That made some faint sense when he said it, though even that is easy to overestimate as many tasks of the day required more skill than Marx probably appreciated, but is gibbering lunacy if you want a 21st century technological society. Only a few people are honest enough to say they want socialism and would be perfectly happy to live an 18th century life to have it. Example: You could just pick up and be a farmer tomorrow, right? [VIDEO] Certainly not at a 21st century farmer level. (Consider your criticisms of that idea carefully, for instance \"yeah but those practices aren't sustainable\" will just get you \"sustainable practices are going to be just as challenging or even more so\" in reply, along with the fact it's not really a relevant point; M(r/s). I Decided To Be A Farmer This Week isn't going to come up with breakthrough solutions in sustainability, either, as they will be far too busy.)"}]},"z3aEv3EzMyQ":{"wy":"HN mostly references it for launch benchmarks comparing Ryzen and Intel, rather than recommending it outright.","m":[{"id":20599259,"author":"rwmj","month":"2019-08","story":"AMD Ryzen 7 3700X is such a hit it almost outsold Intel’s entire CPU range","stance":"reference","why":"Ryzen vs Intel comparison","quote":"Ryzen came ahead in basically all tests except one related to high-end gaming","text":"If you don't mind watching a video, Linus Tech Tips compared Ryzen 7 & 9 with similar (but more expensive) consumer Intel chips and Ryzen came ahead in basically all tests except one related to high-end gaming: [VIDEO] Price/performance of the AMD chips was ahead in everything."},{"id":20375515,"author":"aristophenes","month":"2019-07","story":"The AMD 3rd Gen Ryzen Deep Dive Review: 3700X and 3900X Raising the Bar","stance":"reference","why":"Benchmarks with current security mitigations applied","quote":"did their benchmarks with current patches to everything","text":"The new Ryzens perform very well, it's even more impressive considering that the Intel chips did not have the Fallout/Zombieload mitigations applied[1], that come with significant performance degradations. LinusTechTips, that used to somewhat favor Intel, did their benchmarks with current patches to everything: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":20862178,"author":"Thorrez","month":"2019-09","story":"Dual AMD EPYC 7742 Crushes Quad Intel Xeon 8180M's in Geekbench 4","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"So overall, Intel still wins.","text":"You're talking about this video: [VIDEO] ? On CSGO, AMD wins on average FPS, ties on 95%, and loses on 99%. On Rainbow Six: Seige, AMD wins on average FPS, but loses on 95% and 99%. On the other 3 games (Shadow of the Tomb Raider, Metro: Exodus, and Battlefield V), Intel wins all 3 categories. So overall, Intel still wins."}]},"zdHkY3XYHKA":{"wy":"Cited in passing to show how China's borders have expanded and contracted over history.","q":{"x":"China's borders have grown and shrunk and grown again quite a bit over the centuries.","i":17299291,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":22177044,"author":"Iv","month":"2020-01","story":"The Oldest Living Civilization (1944)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like I said, erudites in the 19th century all across Europe would be fluent in ancient Greek. As to read vs understand, that's the difference between alphabet and ideograms. But IIRC modern Greek speakers have an easy time making sense of ancient Greek. Kings of Europe would claim their legitimacy comes from the Roman empire times. The pope would continue to crown people emperors of Rome, like Charlemagne (emperor of both Germany and France) Napoleon or the Habsburgs. And Roman empire was in the clear cultural continuity of ancient Greece. We just don't do these claims (outside of some fringe far-right groups), because they are not really relevant to what our countries are today. Ancient Greeks were xenophobic, misogynistic, pedophiles, mostly authoritarian and militaristic. We are nothing like them, but most cultures have very ancient roots. And I'll tell you why I react strongly with that. We used to have school programs teaching us similar bullshit. Mostly, the national narrative would be that we, French, are Celts, Franks, who were occupied by the Roman empire, which spawned the aristocracy, until we defeated it during the Revolution. That sense of belonging to a 3000 years old continuity served to foster nationalism and make sure that we don't feel too close to our neighbors. It is an easy sale, you can write a history with that that is mostly correct. You have to ignore some facts, but for 100 years, \"history was written that way\" or at least, text books were. China has"},{"id":17299291,"author":"adrianratnapala","month":"2018-06","story":"Rome vs Greece: a clash of empires","stance":"reference","why":"Chinese borders expanding and contracting over centuries.","quote":"China's borders have grown and shrunk and grown again quite a bit over the centuries.","text":"> And China was never very ambitious beyond its own borders. True, but very informative, as China's borders have grown and shrunk and grown again quite a bit over the centuries. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15274347,"author":"Retric","month":"2017-09","story":"China Makes Chat Group Administrators Criminally Liable for Unlawful Messages","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Milenia of failure.","text":"Milenia of failure. None of their dinasty's lasted even 1,000 years and many where very short. [VIDEO]"}]}});
